      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>Itttas in flit abitor.</head>
        <p>Itttas in flit abitor.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. THK Eorrog of "The Examiner" invites corry LIMERS must in all cases be accompanied by the spondence on all subjects of public interest, name and address of the writer. LETTERS should be brief and pointed. TheirCommuNicATioNs received early in the week— object must be to impart or seek information. except urgent matters—can always be given not abuse. the best attention.</p>
        <p>to "Go and teach all nations," and so the test which the True Church must fulfil is that it must teach, and it must therefore have the characteristics or nature of a teacher. Now for a man to be teaching, he mast be definitely and explicitly laying dowa positive facts. A man who is only offering an opinion is nat teaching. At school the teacher lays it down positively that 2 multiplied by 3 equals 6. Not that such is his opinion, or that it may be so, but that it is so. Nor is the scholar at liberty ;to state that he thinks 2 multiplied by 3 equals 5 or 7, but must accept that 2 multiplied by 3 equals 6.</p>
        <p>It is the very essence of teaching and leax fling that the teacher be definite and the scholar accept what is taught. In-other words, teach- ing consists in a man having definite knowledge, and imparting it to those who have not. Now the Catholic Church is a teacher, and her clear, definite and positive teaching is one of her most striking characteristics, and one which completely marks her off from every other Church in the world. It is hurled against the Catholic Church that she does not allow liberty of thought in matters of doctrine. Certainly not! She cannot. The school teacher does not allow the child to say 2 multiplied by 3 equals 7. His work is to teach 2 multiplied by 3 equals 6. And so the priest, the teacher in the Church, says to his people: "This is true and that is true, not because I think so, nor that it suits my taste, sentiment or convenience to say so, ' but because it is my work to teach such." It is a wonderful thing that men are so blind and cannot see that that attitude of definite and insistent teaching which the Catholic Church ever presents, and which is untruly termed in- tolerance, is nothing less than the real evidence of the truth of the Catholic Church.</p>
        <p>But to come to the point of my argument, viz.: What is the difference between the Catho- lic Church and all others? Simply this, that the Catholic Church teaches, and no other does. The priest teaches, and ministers outside the Catholic Church do not, and, not being teachers, , do not fulfil Christ's command. Now this needs proving, and as it will take up more space than I am sure you can spare at present, Mr. Editor, I will reserve my argument for another letter a fortnight hence. In the meantime I commend for the considera- tion of " M.A. (Cantab.)" the aspect of the office of teaching, which is so perfectly fulfilled by the Catholic Church.</p>
        <p>I suppqse he holds that the Catholic Church is not the true one. If it is not, then which is? Presumably he holds his own religion, because he believes it to be the true one, and, if so, it must have better evidence of being true than the Catholic Church. Will he kindly show how this is? Failing this, where does he. stand?</p>
        <p>T. RICHARDSON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>ROMANIST CLAIMS.</head>
        <p>THE ROMANIST CLAIMS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am inclined to wonder if your Roman- ist correspondents recollect the genesis of this correspondence, and the circumstances of its origin. It originated in the attack on Pro- testantism by the Roman Catholic lecturers, denouncing all other Churches and claiming their own to be the only Christian Church, out- side of which there was no salvation. We have a right to rebut this claim, and to show that heresy, where it exists, is in our oppon- ents' Church and not in ours. We can very well State our position on the lines drawn by Mr. Richardson, but I am afraid I shall not be able to satisfy him with what I have to say. First: Protestantism. Protestantism is not our religion; it is an accident in that religion, and a very important kandmark in its history; our religion is Christianity, and that in its purest and freest sense. Second: Our teaching is the Love of God, shed abroad in the hearts of men, the Redemp- tion of the Cross of Calvary, and the blessing of the Holy Ghost the Comforter. Our charter is the Bible, accepted absolutely by us as the infallible Word of God; the only infallible authority in the world. In the case of the Anglican Church we have the addition of the Prayer-Book, the Homilies, the Canons, and the Thirty-nine Articles, but none of these over- ride the Scriptures. As a beautiful example of what these teach, let me give two quotations from the Catechism " My duty towards God is to believe in Him, to fear Him, and to love Him with all my.heart, with all my mind, with all my soul, and with all my strength; to worship Him, to give Him thanks, to put my whole trust in Him, to call upon Him, to honour His Noly Name and His Word, and to serve Him truly all the days of my life." Again : "My duty towards my neighbour is to love Him as myself, and to do to all men as I would that they should do unto me: To love, honour and succour ray father and mother: To honour and I obey the King, and all that are put in authority under him: To submit myself to all my gov- ernors, teachers, spiritual pastors and masters: To order myself lowly and reverently to all my betters: To hurt nobody by word or deed: To be true and just in all ray dealings: To bear no malice nor hatred in my heart: To keep my hands from picking and stealing, and my tongue from evil-speaking, lying, and slander- ing: To keep my body in temperance, soberness, and chastity: Not to covet nor desire other men's goods; but to learn and labour truly to get mine own living, and to do my duty in that state of life, unto which it shall please God to call me." These two quotations, along with the Apostles' Creed, can be heartily subscribed to by all Pro- testants, and are absolute proof of the truth of our Christian profession. The third and fourth questions may be ans- wered together, and I may say that in addition to the Scriptural command is the splendid suc- cess of the Christian doctrine proclaimed from the Protestant point of view. Is not the splendid Empire of Britain abso- lute evidence of the Divine favour towards Pro- testant zeal? On the other hand, look at the</p>
        <p>• 9 . 0 t 7 14 its best, the rest:,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>CATHOLIC LECTURES.</head>
        <p>THE CATHOLIC LECTURES.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l deeply regret the tone and manner of your correspondent. Had he left personalities severely alone, there paight have been a corre- spondence of some value. Even now, from the abuse of paper and ink, it may be clear to your readers that there is a chasm that is unbridge- able between Free Churchmen and Roman Catholics. The chasm is the vast difference be- tween Sacerdotalism and Evangelicalism. There is no religious harmony between the Sacerdotal Roman Catholic and the Free Church Evan- gelical, as any Roman cleric knows well. Take, foh example, the question of religious authority. Modernism was a form of truth that would have undermined Papacy. The author of it was a French Roman cleric. When its conclusions were understood the Papacy mercilessly crushed the author and his doctrine. The final arbiter of all doctrine must be the Pope. On that mat- ter he cannot err. That is a sacerdotal preten- sion. The Rev. It. J. Campbell, a modernist of another school, remains in a Free Church pastorate, and indeed is the President-elect of the Congregational body. The individual con- science must settle the ground of authority. In a sacerdotal scheme the priest is always the via media of everything. Religiously a Roman Catholic is helpless without the priest. A canon of the Evangelical faith is that every believer is a priest unto God. His ordination is his trust in the Lord Jesus Christ. One of your corre- spondents grasps that situation very clearly, but may I correct his sentence and say that "Protestantism is absolutely contrary to Romax Catholicity in all its doctrines." Catholicity is no exclusive prerogative of the Roman Church. , A discussion shorn of personalities might have placed before your readers the fundamental differences of the Roman Church and Protest- ants, but no correspondent with a thread of self-respect will continue to correspond with an antagonist whose arrogance betrays him even into impudence. I would direct your readers' attention to a note appended by you, sir, to one of "J.D.'s" letters. You yourself, sir, consid- ered his letter so abusive that out of regard for the decency of your own columns you refused to insert his abusive matter..</p>
        <p>_ But, sir, " J.D." is not content with abuse. Having a coward's pluck, he hides behind the fence, whilst he slings mire. The mode of war- fare conducted by "J.D." is not English and despicably mean. The plane of discussion might have been a lofty one. But some of us remember that there is a maxim', "The end justifies the means." There is another, "Always the same." Some of us know whence those maxims eman- ated. Hence the tone of " J.D." is no surprise. We know his religious paternity. May I remind your readers I had, and have, no ambition to have either the first or the last word in this correspondence. I did not initiate the correspondence. A sermon from my pulpit was reported in "The Examiner." "J.D." fol- lowed the report of "The Examiner" with the abusive letter which the editor censored. Pro- bably in the interests of decent correspondence I ought to have ignored " J.D." The inquiry of "Anxious" and "Phil Squod" will receive, I am sure, the careful consideration</p>
        <p>of the Free Churches. I presume the Roman Catholic Evidence Lecturers are experts in that office. It would only be fair that we on our side should secure the man who devotes his time to Protestant Evidence, and I never yet met the Protestant lecturer who ever dreaded questions from a Roman Catholic audience.</p>
        <p>The correspondence, sir, has evidently aroused much local interest, and I hope abler hands than mine will continue it, and that for and against will endeavour to maintain a lofty plane. lam indebted to you, sir, for your indulgence, and to "The Examiner" for the printed corre- spondence. Golborne-street Baptist Church.</p>
        <p>11. OGDEN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>CHRISTIANITY AND POPERY.</head>
        <p>CHRISTIANITY AND POPERY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln his letter to you last week " M.A.</p>
        <p>(Cantab.)" states that "One of the essential ' differences between Christianity and Popery is that while the former makes Christ the centre and foundation of our hope, the latter makes the so-called Church such, and that system puts the Church in the place of Christ." What he really means is best known to himself perhaps, but in trying to state the Catholic view of the Church, "Cantab." has essayed a task which is quite out of his province. I think that your readers will agree that the true idea of the Catholic Church can be best given by a Catho- lic, and so I venture to correct " Cantab.'s " notions, and those of your readers who may have been misled by his statement. And just a word explaining the meaning of the term Catholic Church. I paean by this that Church, of which the Pope is the spiritual head. A lot of people outside that Church call them- selves Catholics nowadays. But what's in a name? It is the identity of the true Church and the possession of truth that matters. Catholics identify their Church as being " God's voice on earth," because it is in perfect harmony and conformity with Christ's words to His Apostles when lie endowed them with authority to speak for Him. The words, " He that heareth you heareth Me." (Luke x. 16). The true Church then speaks in Christ's name, and is therefore, as I have stated, "God's voice on earth."</p>
        <p>But the contradiction which e%ists betwoakt all the religious sects must ,:ereamly bewilder those men who are earnest and anxious to pos. sees the truth, and as there an one rciigicn only which is true, the question such men must often ask themselves is: "Is there not sums simple test by which the True Religion csn be distinguished from all others -vhicn are false?" There is, and so simple is the rest that any reli- gion not fulfilling it cannot be true, and mus- consequently be ignored by right-thinking meu. I confidently state that the test I propound will define the essential difference between Cathol- icity and Protestantism in marked contrast to that so ambiguously suggested by " Cantab." But your readers must decide for themselves, Mr. Editor. In addition to having authority to speak in Christ's Name, the True Church must also use that authority, for Christ charged the Apostles</p>
      </div>
