      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 2, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 2, 1914.</p>
        <p>raters to the nit= continued.</p>
        <p>miserable failures in countries dominated by Romanist claims. How many Royal houses are fugitives from their thrones •in Romanist countries and have had to take refuge in Pro- testant England? Will Mr. Richardson and "J.D." explain why Romanist countries are so backward compared with England? Will they explain why civil and religious liberty does not exist to the same extent that it does in Ehg- land? Surely tolerance and religious fivedom ought to be good for all. Then Why is It not encouraged in Roman Catholit Militiges? Seeing that we both worship elye vtv,tlslNye ought to live at peace With each other. Why is it not so? Is it because of the multitude of traditions and sophistry that has been allowed to grow up around the simple story of the Cross that it has become so difficult to live in harmony together? If the Papacy _ had a _ _</p>
        <p>Divine Origin .what a glorious light would have</p>
        <p>shone down the Ages of Time; for God would be worthy of Him, and would have taken care that the purity of the Faith should not be cor- rupted. But what has been the character of the !Popes? Have they been worthy of their great office? No! Many of the occupants of the Papal chair have been among the worst char- acters in history, and on more than one occasion there have been rival Popes, each denouncing the other. Can such men as these represent the Son of Righteousness? It is impossible for good to come from evil. It is impossible for truth to come from falsehood. I leave the case unhesi- tatingly to the verdict of history, and challenge my Roman critics to controvert what I have stated and subscribe myself PHIL SQUOD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>HOLY CV 110LIC v ROM 'IN CATHOL IC</head>
        <p>HOLY CV 110LIC v ROM 'IN CATHOL IC</p>
        <p>CHURCH.</p>
        <p>you allow me to point out that with all false systems, more importance is attached to a fleshly corporate system of ecclesiastical organisation, than belonging to the spiritual Church of Christ—more importance to being Churchmen than Christians? A mere external Church is only the potsherd of a Church, and such a carnal system can never constitute the Bride of Christ (Rev. xxi. 9), which alone is His Body, the Church (Col. i. 24). Such an erroneous notion is going back to Judaism, and therefore contrary to the fundamental principles of a Church in the New Testament (John iv. 21).</p>
        <p>The Church of England, in her Communion Service, calls the mystical Body of Christ "the blessed company of all faithful people." , See also the nineteenth article of our Protestant Church, which declares in effect the Greek and Romish Churches to be in a state of fatal apostasy—" having erred in matters of faith." " Churchianity " is the devil's counterfeit for Christianity. All visible Churches become more or less apostate in time. Even five out of the Seven Churches of Asia became so in St John's time. The Church of Sardis had "a name to live, but was dead" tßev. iii. I). It had works plenty, but no faith, and works without faith are as dead as faith without works. Its mem- bers were not partakers of the Holy Ghost, without whom they are none of His (Born. viii. 9)—to whatever Church they may profess to belong. . .</p>
        <p>The theory of Apostolical or Episcopal Suc- cession "has been made the basis of all sorts of priestcraft and sacerdotal impostures, including the Confessional as well as sacramental errors. whereby the sacraments are put in place of the Gospel, and " The Church" in the place of Christ.</p>
        <p>The late learned Dr. Lightfoot, Bishop of Dur- ham, and formerly Regius Professor of Divinity at Cambridge, says (in his Essay on the Chris- tian Ministry, page 201) that "as late as 70 An. no distinct signs of Episcopal Government have hitherto appeared in Gentile Christendom." Hallam says that Archbishop Laud was re- buked in 1604 by the -University of Oxford, for saying that there could he no Church without Bishops (Constitutional History, chapter vii., 2nd note "M.")• Archbishop Usher was asked by King Charles 1., in the Isle of Wight, wherever he found in Antiquity that Presbyters alone ordained any. And he replied, "I can show your Majesty nrrre, even where Presbyters alone successively ordained Bishops," and instanced in Hierome's words, " Epist, ad Evagriurn," of the Presbyters of Alexandra choosing and making their own Bishops from the days of Mark, till Heracles and Dionysius—nearly 300 years. (Hooker re- fers to this case in his Ecclesiastical Polity, Book vii., chapter 5.) Macaulay, who is at least an authority in history, says that "it is probable that no clergyman could trace his spiritual genealogy from Bishop to Bishop, as far back as the Con- quest, and then, a little further on: " Extreme obscurity overhangs the history of the Middle 11 Ages; and the facts which are discernible I through the obscurity prove that the Church was extremely ill-regulated. We read of the Sees of the highest dignity being sold, trans- ferred backwards and forwards by popular tumult, bestowed sometimes by a profligate woman on a paramour, sometimes by a warlike baron on his kinsman, still a stripling. We read of Bishops five years old. We read of Bishops ten years old, of many Popes who were mere boys, and who rivalled the frantic dissoluteness of Caligula--nay, of a female Pope," 8/c. (Essay on Gladstone and the Church.) We are not so much indebted to Rome for our religious orders as for our religious disorders. We state in the Creed that we believe in the "Holy Catholic Church," which, of course, must be spiritual rather than corporate and invisible rather than visible, as it would be needless to say that we believed in a system, the existence of which could be proved by ocular demonstra- tion, for faith (or belief) is the evidence of things not seen (Heb. xi. 1). It is subjective or personal religion we should contend for, not objective or ecclesiastical, the latter of which is a species of idolatry. Tertullian, A.D. 219, says: "Christians were made priests by Christ (Rev. i. 6), so that where three are gathered together they make a Church, although they be all laymen; and where no clergyman is present laymen may baptise and celebrate the Eucharist, the distinction between clergy and laity being only of the Church's ap- pointment." See also Justin Martyr to the same effect, who says that "We (i.e. Christians) are God's true sacerdotal race." (Dialogue with, Trypho the Jew, section 116.) One essential difference between Christianity and Popery is that while the former makes Christ the centre of salvation (John iii. 16), the latter makes what they call the Church such. Hence the fatal mistake.</p>
        <p>Of course, the tactual succession, as it was termed in correspondence between the Pope and the Archbishops of 'Canterbury and York some years ago, is most fallacious. The " Church Quarterly" for July, 1884, shows Ithat there are 58 legal flaws in the Papal suc- cession, from simony and other fatal irregu- larities, and huge gaps of thirty and forty years (repeated), as well as the three rival Popes who cursed and deposed each other during a period of nearly fifty years, to the great scandal of Christendom (see Dr. Virylie's "History of *Pro- testantism," Vol. I, p. 161), aid as an illegal Pope cannot make legal Cardinals, nor ,-et Bishops, it rather tends to invalidate such a fable which almost rivals heathen mythology. The value of Apostolical succession would not be of the slightest use, even if it were true, any more than it would be for an ironmonger to be able to trace his genealogy to Tubal Cain, I who, we read, was the first to make brazen linstruments (Geri. iv., 22), as there is no Scrip- tural warrant for attaching importance thereto. If the strength of a chain is equal to the weak- est link of that chain, the whole theory of I Apostolical succession will be found to be a mere rope of sand. "On Christ the solid Rock I stand, All other ground is sinking sand." M.A. (CANTAB.).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>SUGGESTED FOOTBALL BENEFIT.</head>
        <p>SUGGESTED FOOTBALL BENEFIT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l read with pleasure the letter in your last week's issue, and am very glad that a benefit for Ernest Brookes has been proposed. I have always had a great appreciation of his work with our club, and besides being a "townie," he has, without a doubt, provided me with many a good hour's sport on a Saturday. If the members take the matter up, I hope the committee will assist to give him a bumper benefit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>ANOTHER ADMIRER.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER ADMIRER.</p>
        <p>PROMISED HOME RULE SETTLEMENT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The outlook for a settlement this week is brighter than it has been for some time. Last week we appeared to be on the verge of civil war. No doubt most people are working for peace, and I have no hesitation in saying if attempts at party advantage could be swept. away peace would prevail. It is our ridiculous party system that at the present moment is hindering and delaying not only a settlement of this Home Rule and Ulster question, but also social reform. Federalism appears to be the soundest and best way to a solution of the Home Rule matter, and it would lead to this system being adopted throughout the United Kingdom. And it is I badly needed, too, for not only does our House of Lords need reforming, but also the House of Commons as well.</p>
        <p>I may add that I have been engaged in busi- ness in London during the past few weeks, and have occasionally at night been in the House of Commons at the debates, and in the smoke- room, having a chat with several of the mem- bers of Parliament. The prevailing opinion is that a settlement will be arrived at by consent. E. ENGLAND. London.</p>
        <p>Y M.C.A. AID FOR EMIGRANTS. Sir,—ln response to many appeals, both home and foreign, the National Council of Young Men's Christian Associations has organised a special Emigration Department for the benefit of the many thousands of young men who leave our shores each year for other lands. Secretaries of the Association are now at work at the ports of Liverpool, London, Southamp- ton, Bristol and Plymouth, interviewing young .men on sailing days, supplying them with in- formation and advice, and giving personal in- troductions to branches of the Y.M.C.A. in all parts of the world. The services of these secre- taries is free to all young men, irrespective of class, or creed or nationality. Representatives of the Association now meet ' arriving passenger vessels at all the larger for- eign and colonial ports, and are ready to assist in the matter of lodgings, information and ad- vice regarding employment. Over 20,000 men leaving the United Kingdom were helped in some way by the Associhtion Emigration De- partment during 1913. No passages are booked and no fees of any kind are charged for the services rendered. Young men who have decided to emigrate are -ordially invited to communicate with this lffice, or with the Emigration Secretaries at the various ports, in order to avail themselves of the privileges offered. ADAM SCOTT. Secretary Emigration Department, National Council of Y.M.C.A.'s, 13, Russell-square, W.O</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>DREGS OF ARGUMENT.</head>
        <p>THE DREGS OF ARGUMENT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The warm Easter weather has brought Prematurely into the columns of "The Times" a "silly season" correspondence. The subject is "Votes and Dress," and the gist of it all is that because women wear hobbled skirts and other extremes of fashion they are quite un- qualified to use a vote. No one ever suggests that because Lord Beaconsfield made his first speech in the House of Commons arrayed in the amazing costume described by Mr. Justin McCarthy in his " His6tiry of Our Own Times," or because Mr. Gladstone persisted to the eild of his days in impaling his chin on an impossible looking collar, these great statesmen w.;re devoid of political intelligence. But since Anti- Suffragists find it so hard to discover reasonable objections to the enfranchisement of women,' by all means let them base their hostility on slit ' or hobbled skirts, crinolines, or poke bonnets. or whatever mode of the moment may reveal the human passion for variety. SUFFRAGETTE.</p>
        <p>THE BEST TREATMENT FOR ITCH- ING SCALPS DANDRUFF AND FALLING HAIR. To allay itching and irritation of the scalp, Prevent dry, thin and falling hair, remove crusts, scales and dandruff, and promote the growth and beauty of the hair, the following special treatment is most effective, agreeable and economical. On retiring, comb the hair out straight all around, then begin at the side and make a parting, gently rubbing Cuticura. Oint- ment into the parting with a bit of soft flannel held over the end of the finger. Anoint addi- tional partings about half an inch apart until the whole scalp has been treated, the purpose being to get the Cuticura Ointment on the scalp skin rather than on the hair. It is well to place a light covering over the hair to protect the pillow from possible stain. The next morning shampoo with Cuticura Soap and hot water. Shampoos alone may be used as often as agreeable, but once or twice a month is gener- apnodtte Srons, Drug ally Sufficient for this special treatment for women's hair. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post free. Address: F. Newbery 27,a2%eernbio aAloLn, onduoisi ~Ao.r</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>LETTERS BY AN IRISHMAN—continued.</head>
        <p>LETTERS BY AN IRISHMAN—continued.</p>
        <p>proposition. Three years later Mr. Walter Long came over as Chief Secretary under the same Government, to reverse the policy of which Lord yet the Tory party was not convinced of this Dudley was the exponent. lie was perfectly explicit in denying the principle that Ireland should be governed according to Irish ideas. This reversal policy illustrates very well the disastrous see-saw alternating between conces- sion and coercion which would have demoral- ised any country, and has inflicted grave injury on Irish political life. It illustrates it all the better because this see-saw took place under a period of unbroken Tory administration. But the essential point to note is this: Mr. Long ' spoke too late. The administration of which Lord Dudley was the head, had conceded the main point for which "Irish ideas" had been contending ever since the English landed in Ireland.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wyndham's Act of 1903 established by law the principle that land in Ireland should be owned by those who occupied and worked it; that the soil of Ireland should belong to the Irish people, and the history of English, rule in Ireland may be briefly exemplified in relation to land. When the English came first to Ireland their object was to take Irish land from Irish tribes, and to give it to English nobles to deal with at their pleasure. Met, by the difficulty of separating the Irish people from the land, they tried the expedients of killing the Irish people out altogether; of exporting them into slavery; later, of penning them into the uncultivable parts of the country; of driving them "to Con- naught instead of Hell "; and finally of reduc- ing them to a state of serfdom, under cover of religious disability. The end of the struggle was that the Irish recovered, first the free exer- cise of their religion and then ownership of their , land. But the struggle, prolonged over centuries, was disastrous to Ireland, and to England also. The struggle is, not completed, nor can it be, until the Irish have gained the full manage- ment of their own affairs; until Ireland is gov- erned according to Irish ideas by Irishmen chosen by the Irish people in Ireland.</p>
        <p>F.W.R. Next Article: " Detailed Review of English Rule in Ireland."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>LETTERS BY AN IRISHMAN.</head>
        <p>LETTERS BY AN IRISHMAN.</p>
        <p>Survey of the Facts Governing the</p>
        <p>Demand for Home Rule.</p>
        <p>1.-WHY HOME RULE IS WITHHELD.</p>
        <p>We shall for the next few weeks publish a special article under the above heading, deal- ing with the many phases of the Irish problem frcm the Irish point of view. The writer, " F.W.R.," whose letters have appeared in the columns of "The Examiner" from time to time, is well versed in his sub- ject, and may be trusted to deal with the question in a judicious and reasonable way.</p>
        <p>It is always far more difficult to combat an argument than a prejudice and it is undeniable that the present Tory opposition to Home Rule for Ireland rests "whole and entire" upon a deeply-seated prejudice which is absolutely inconsistent with the political principles of the English masses Englishmen have generally stood for Liberty, Democracy and Self-govern- ment, but the Tory party, or what was the party, in this age have appeared to lose all sense of respectability and to have questioned the real meaning of Democracy. Mr. A. J. Balfour, speaking in the House of Commons in July, 1910, stated that Democracy was government by consent, and the general indignation was expressed by Englishmen at the autocratic treatment of Finland by Russia proves this to be the case. Yet surely Mr. Bal- four would be the last man to assert that the government of Ireland has the consent of the governed. This general appreciation of Demo- cratic government is not a matter of opinion, or a sentimental outburst, for " The Times," in its leader of April sth, 1909, on "Local Patriotism and the Imperial Idea," commented upon the excellent results wrought by the concession of Self-government to British Colonies. In other words, ,"The Times" and Mr.. Balfour admit that Home Rule is the best rule—the only rule for the British Empire—with one exception in Ireland.</p>
        <p>Argument for Home Rule as a sound practical policy is of two kinds—positive and negative. The grant of local autonomy, wherever it has been made, has been followed by prosperity, contentment, and loyalty—that is the positive side of the argument. The negative side is seen in Ireland, where poverty, discontent and dis- affection have been the consequences of denying Home Rule.</p>
        <p>The other evening I heard a Tory remark that Ireland was unfit for self-government, that to give her Home Rule would mean danger to England. When asked: "Do you mean then that you deny Ireland the form of government best suited to her needs because you are afraid of Ireland?" and pressed for an answer, the reply came that Home Rule "would be an injury, not an advantage." Evidently the pugilistic politician of Cornhill fame does not hold the same views as his " sacked " leader. He, like the rest of the Tory caucus, informs us that the Irishman is incapable of managing his own affairs. But without being the least dis- courteous or quarrelsome, surely we Irishmen have a perfect right to ask, by way of rejoinder, "Is England fit to govern Ireland?" Any fair- minded Englishman who scans the pages of " Lecky's History" will agree that the govern- ment of Ireland since the Act of Union has been a lamentable failure. But even if Ireland had prospered under foreign rule, her honour and her dignity would force her to demand the privi- leges of freedom, for indeed Sir Henry Campbell- Bannerman was correct when he stated that "Good government is no substitute for self- government." The demand for Home Rule rests primarily on that ground. Self-govern- ment is claimed, first as a natural right, and, secondly, by historical legal title, as a right which Ireland enjoyed, which was ratified to Ireland by a solemn declaration of the British Parlia- ment, and was filched from Ireland by a most corrupt and shameful transaction, to which Ireland never consented, and against which Ire- land has protested for more than a century.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>ARE ENGLISH SYSTEMS JUSTIFIED?</head>
        <p>ARE ENGLISH SYSTEMS JUSTIFIED?</p>
        <p>But setting aside all these claims of right, the question shall be argued as far as possible from the British standpoint, which regards the mat- ter as one of expediency. Has the present sys- tem, or indeed any system, employed by Eng- land justified itself? It is to that end, and not with a desire to renew painful memories, that, before considering the problems of to-day, some attempt must be made to discern its causes. In order to examine England's fitness to govern Ireland—as so often appraised by our Tory friends—it is necessary to pass in review, briefly, the history of English rule in Ireland. The name "England" is here deliberately used, because, in Scotland and Wales, the major- ities are in favour of Home Rule for Ireland, as is Ireland herself, and public opinion gener- ally throughout the British Empire. Let us briefly summarise our review of the history of British rule in the country. Ireland was first treated as a territory to be exploited in the in- terests of Great Britain. The native race were regarded as an element to be got rid of. Every effort was made to prevent their fusion with the settlers. Vast confiscations were made. Wars of extermination were undertaken again and again. This policy, according to Lecky, is sup- posed to have lasted to the final conquest under William 111. and James 11.</p>
        <p>During the 18th century no attempt was made to kill out the Irish. The country was regarded as containing two castes, divided from each other by religion. " One form of religion," says Stephen Gwynn, an Irish Protestant, " that of the minority, was highly privileged. That of the majority was cruelly proscribed. As against the Catholic Irish, who were mainly of the native race, the interest of the Protestant faith was protected." But as against the interests of England, the interests of any class in Ireland, or .of all Ireland, were deliberately sacrificed. The governing principle of this period was to keep Ireland weak, by maintaining religious dis- sensions and by repressing Irish enterprise and industry. In 1782, when the Irish Parliament gained its first freedom, England still nominated the ministry, and that ministry pursued unswerv- ingly the interest of England, and finally engin- eered the Union. Up to this point, not the most partisan historian can affirm that Ireland was governed tolerably. - Since 1800 the interests of Ireland have been committed to the care of the Imperial Parlia- ment. For half a century it cannot be said that Parliament made any attempt to consider Irish interests or Irish opinion. Then the appalling disaster of the great famine did something to awaken public conscience. Yet until 1870 no remedial legislation of any importance was undertaken. From that date frequent efforts have been made, often with the most honest in- tention, to promote the welfare of Ireland. Par- liament has frequently given Ireland the kind of legislation which England thought good for her, but the experiment of governing Ireland from Westminster, even with the best intentions, proved to be a pitiful failure. England is unfit to govern Ireland by temperament, by tradition, or by equipment. She has been legislating for a people whose soul she can never know, and with a country with which she can have no. natural sympathy.</p>
        <p>IRISH IDEAS FOR IRISH GOVERNMENT. Well might Lord Dudley, the last Tory Vice- roy, acclaim that "the time has come when Ireland ought to be governed by Irish ideas." It has taken 61 centuries before a spokesman of the Tory party in England could be brought to admit that Irish ideas were the ideas accord- ing to which Ireland should be governed, and</p>
        <p>Continued in preceding column.</p>
        <p>If your meals don't taste right, or you feel bloated after eating, and you believe it is the food which affects you; if what little you eat lies heavy on your stomach; if you have difficulty in breathing after eating, eructations of sour, undigested food and gas, you can make up your mind that you need something to stop fermentation and avoid your food from souring. To make every bite of the food you eat aid in the nourishment and strength of your body, you must first get your stomach in a healthy condition by using ICIMOC BLOOD TONIC. Then you can eat heartily, and your meals will taste good. You won't have any more sick headaches, biliousness, constipation griping and other disorders. h ,o</p>
        <p>SS</p>
        <p>THE SUN AS DOCTOR.</p>
        <p>In bygone ages the sun in its gorgeous blazing course through the heavens seemed nothing less than a god to the. people on earth. They saw that its presence Drought light and joy, abundant vegetation and case g life, and its absence caused greyness and sadness, scarcity of food and hardship. Ilence they venerated it as an omnipotent being to be propitiated by all possible means, for its enger was a thing to be dreaded and its fay- eur a blessing inestimable. But the sun went on its way unmoved by prayers or gifts, and sun-worship died out, and other religions and ether gods took hold on men's souls.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>CHANGE OF ATTITUDE.</head>
        <p>A CHANGE OF ATTITUDE.</p>
        <p>As the ages rolled on another attitude was iisplayed by men to the great sun. As with other blessings that surround mankind, un- provided by himself, its presence became taken for granted with indifference and thanklessness—the sun would shine by day and set at night as a matter of right, and it all meant nothing, there was nothing godlike or remarkable in it course, and certainly no special reason for awe or gratitude. Its very gratuitousness caused it to he undervalued. But now in these latter days men's attitude towards the sun and its light is changing. It is beginning to dawn upon them that, after all, the sun is a, mighty influence against the powers of good and evil—those powers which express themselves so forcibly in disease and death: and that it is not only as a life-giving influence causing the earth to bring forth her increase that it must be regarded, but also as an active combatant on the side of good in the ceaseless warfare that is waged, moment by moment, between physical good and phy: sical evil.</p>
        <p>The whole of the wonderful facts that havi led to this change of attitude are practically the direct outcome of the germ theory of dis- ease origin. Popularly stated, this theory holds that many specific diseases are due each to the presence of a tiny living germ peculiar to itself whose activity and develop- ment in the body brings about that particu- lar disease. These germs are living organisms that can be isolated, examined, propagated, carried from place to place, and even trans- ported in glass tubes, to be set free as the devil's own agents at any time or place at the decree of man. Thus cholera, typhus, diph- theria, yellow-fever, erysipelas, consumption, are a few of the ills Of the flesh that have been traced back to the direct agency of germ life. SUNLIGHT AND GERM LIFE. dlifisecoovf- thesNoewmiinntistetudoyria'gagaistrahes eirdhiatisonbes eonf ered that to germ life, as a whcle, sunlight is rank poison. In ordinary diffused light tchhezrfcualtinsoutnlb4hst the said to flourish, but ut diine: _brigke ht sinners, they love the dark bec,ause their deeds are evil. A very striking illustration of this fact has been furnished by h clever Ger- n scientist,' Herr Hans Buchner, in an in- teresting experiment. He prepared a basin of special nutritive broth, such n broth as pre- vious experience had taught him was the best i possible n which to cultivate germ life:lnto this basin he put a large number of typhus barVli, those germs whose presence in the body causes the dreaded typhus fever. He had previously cut out in black paper the let- ters T Y P lli 1.1 S, and affixed them to the glass lid of the basin, so that the word stood out in large bold letters right across the lid. Then he put the basin into bright sunlight for an hour and a half, afterwards he re- moved it into diffused light, and then into darkness, and kept it there for twenty-four hours. , Next day he examined it, and took away the patwr .etters, and beho'd there were no dreaded typhus germs in the basin except under the brack letters, ar.:l there, where these had been, the baleful word typhus stood out, fashioned in myriacl.3 of these germs. Wherever the sunlight had penetrated the germs had died, but under the black letters where the sunlight could not find its way and they were protected from its rays they had flourished and increased, and that increase spelt typhus. But not to typhus germs alone has sunlight been shewn to be S`O inimical. Experiments en Similar lines have given the same results with other disease germs. Thus the bacilli of Asiatic cholera, of consuraption, of anthrax, of ery.sipelas, of swine fever, the germs that give rise to white and yellow "matter " in wounds, and many others, are all killed by the direct rays of the sun. At first it was not believed that the sun's rays could be directly poisonous, but now it is proved that sunlight in itself is actual poison to the germs and suf- ficient to cause tlieir destruction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>TIIE CASE OF CONSUMPTION.</head>
        <p>TIIE CASE OF CONSUMPTION.</p>
        <p>Look again at tho change of view which this new knowledge has brought about with regard to consumption. Years ago a consump- tive was coddled up and kept in warm, stuffy rooms, from which sunlight and fresh air were religiously excluded; to-day his treat- ment is one of unlimited sunshine and pure, dry air. Patients are -placed out of doors to bask in the sun from first thing in the morn- ing until the last thing at night. Probably if some device could be invented whereby the direct rays of the sun could be poured down into the lungs, and thus illuminate with ra- diant light every nook and cranny, consump- tion would be known no more, for the activ- ity of the bacilli would necessarily be arrest- under such conditions. No wonder that under the old regime consumption grew to be such a veritable scourge in this country that one-seventh of all the deaths were attribut- able to it.</p>
        <p>Although the world of medical men and scientists realise now very generally the in- estimable value of sunshine in the mitigation of disease, the idea has not yet permeated among the mass of people, and the very sim- plicity and cheapness of the remedy stands in the way of its 'appreciation. Could sun- shine be put up' by a druggist, retailed at is. lid. a bottle, and. well "boomed," thous- ands would fioet- to t-v +l,o now -,,nedv with</p>
        <p>(Continued in neit Oilitthtt N</p>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>tWarrington Workingmen's Associs</p>
        <p>tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>N.A. CENTRE.</head>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE.</p>
        <p>THE GOWY. A fair mnster of locals visit the Gowy</p>
        <p>and taking the angling all round since the cl° season came in, some fair sport has been c tamed with eels, trout and pike. We in VSI! rington have had our attention called to fact that as the pike is a coarse fish, and as N.A.A. passed a resolution at their gen_eil,</p>
        <p>meeting at Bamfurlong to observe the cic season in its entirety, we have no option butt stop pike fishing until June the 16th. No mut what other clubs do; we must be loyal to c't selves.</p>
        <p>Several clubs are already objecting to being allowed to take pike and they thews(</p>
        <p>are -barred from doing the same-, so in matter we must sink personal opinion and these matters to other bodies. Future lc tion may provide anglers with a close s'</p>
        <p>that will be fishable for certain specimens barred.</p>
        <p>The fact, if it is a fact, that other anglero still taking pike, does not absolve North Anglers from the consequences of their res tion. Neither can we plead that the prelE</p>
        <p>of pike in a trout stream would not be t-oleofrf,' by a landowner or Conservancy Board. fortunately there is no Conservancy Board 01' the Gowy, or we might have applied for the ruling. But on the other hand, the Gowl strictly preserved trout stream above and belt' the length rented by the N.A.A., and it is certain that those owning the fishing will to thank us for preserving pike. Indeed, the 11°b lord from whom we lease our fishing, might PI something to say, if he knew what Nye ssl doing.</p>
        <p>However, for the present, we must all strict', adhere to close season, and particularly we wish to remain members of the Natiri. Federation of Anglers, as the Liverpool Ane,, Association intend to propose at the meeting in May that all associations affili to the N.P.A. must observe the close season a; coarse fish under penalty of expulsion.</p>
        <p>WIGAN CENTRE.</p>
        <p>We have received word from the Centre that what Mr. J. Melling told us at t amalgamated meeting, the stocking of the le,ll/ of the Leeds and Liverpool Canal near has already commenced. They have .t.urne° 10,000 stock fish ranging from Boz. to 20ort special facilities will be offered to the Warrl' ton Centre.</p>
      </div>
