      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>SEND BY NIGHT TRAIN.</head>
        <p>SEND BY NIGHT TRAIN.</p>
        <p>In despatching chicks get them off by the night mail trains where possible. The chicks then sleep most of the journey, and by the time they reach their destination are just ready for their first feed, should you be receiving chicks instead of despatching. Then it is best if yoh let them rest near the fire for a time to throw off any chill from the journey. A drink of warm milk will soon enable them to pick up and commence picking about. Some people give bread crumbs and boiled egg for the first meal, but this sometimes causes indigestion, and giv- ing the fine dry chick food overcomes this diffi- culty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>FOR TABLE BIROS.</head>
        <p>FOR TABLE BIROS.</p>
        <p>- _ To those who are going in for table' birds alone, hatching operations can be carried on much longer than those requiring layers or ex- hibition birds. Chicks should be fed every two hours for the first few days and fro pa one month to three months about four times per day. After three months three feeds are enough; little and often is the best Method, and don't forget tender greens, such as lettuce, water- cress, chickweed, and don't allow chicks to perch until four months old. .</p>
        <p>The management of brooders and foster mothers all depends on the make, and it is best to follow the maker's instructions as far as possible. The temperature must be regulated in accordance with the outside atmosphere or room, but it is best to start in, say, February or March, at about 80 degrees, and by the end of the first week run down to 70 or 75, and gradually lower as the chicks harden off. If kept too high your chicks will be troubled with leg weakness and liable to chills and colds. Try and avoid running baby chicks with those a month old, the bigger birds frequently bully the smaller ones and they don't get the amount of food they should do. It is best to keep the ages as near as possible. At the end of six weeks to two months it is best to draft out the most for- ward birds, and also to separate ,the cockerels from the pullets, as their sexes will be easy to distinguish at this age.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Eggs, Poultry, etc.</head>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, etc.</p>
        <p>ADE'S Sussex Eggs. Oldest strains of Brown, Speckled, Light and Red; reliable eggs; 215., 10s. 6d. dozen; utility, 3s. 6d. dozen, or 30s. per 100; unfertiles replaced.—John Ado, Hel- lingly, Sussex.</p>
        <p>LE ZOUTE-KNOCKE SEASON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>Popular Golfing Resort Near</head>
        <p>The Popular Golfing Resort Near</p>
        <p>Ostend.</p>
        <p>The wonderful summer weather we have en- joyed here during the past fortnight has at- tracted an unusually large number of visitors from all countries, especially from England, to this popular golfing resort. Le Zoute-Knocke becomes more fashionable every day, as we see by the numerous 'fine villas springing up, as if by magic, on every side. The sceptics of a few years ago now admit that the enterprise of the Le Zoute Administra- tion has been fully justified by recent events, .for this pretty seaside health resort, in spite of the feverish building activity that reigns here, is still not big enough for the crowds of visitors who flock here during the summer season; not to speak of the week-ender's difficulties in get- ting rooms, unless he writes to the Cie Le Zoute, Rue courte du Marais at Ghent, some weeks in advance, to ask that rooms be retained at one of the hotels or pensions. The Managing Committee of the Golf Club found, two years ago, that there was not suffi- cient room for all the lovers of the health-giv- ing game, and so they set to work to plan and lay out a second golf course, with plenty of natural hazards, and this will be quite ready for play at the beginning of 1915 season. There are numbers of visitors whose one wish is to play on the new links now, but the committee is taking a firm stand, and will not allow any sport on the second course until all the green and everything is in quite perfect condition. So we must be patient and wait. The thermal springs of .Le Zoute attract many families now that the healing properties of their waters have been proved on a par with Vichy and Aix-les-Bains. Many far-seeing members of the British Golf Club have recently bought plots of land for villas, and are now building in the road between the Club House and the English Church, which road bids fair to become one of the prettiest of the tree-lined avenytes that make the soft rolling sand Alines of Le Zoute-Knocke, S /Men an ideal nook for a restful holiday, whilst the northern breezes make of it a cool refuge from the sum- mer's heat. The seven-mile-long beach, several hundred yards wide, provides a much appreci- ated spot for the children to paddle about with- out danger, to their heart's content, and to build castles in the fine white sand. CHARLES KNOTT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 2, 1914. FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 2, 1914. FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>RATING AND AGRICULTURAL LAND.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Farmers and the Report on Local</p>
        <p>Taxation.</p>
        <p>The Cheshire Chamber of Agriculture, at a meeting at Crewe on Monday, discussed the re- port of the Departmental Committee on Local Taxation as it affects agriculture. Mr. James Sadler pointed out that the report recommended the abolition of the grants under the Agricultural Rates Act, which had been an enormous boon to agriculturists, but they were promised certain other grants which they hoped would i:nove than make up for the assistance they had received from the Agricultural Rating Act.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. A. M. R. Legh) said lie thought that land, like raw material, should be as free from taxation of any description as a bale of cotton coming into Liverpool. He thought' that they should make a strong effort to see that agricultural land should be free from taxation.</p>
        <p>Professor Daine said that Lord Lucas, the Permanent Secretary of the Board of Agricur. ture, had promised that in the forthcoming Budget it was the intention of the Government to make considerable grants • out of Imperial taxation to assist the rates in the country dis- tricts, and instead of agricultural land being exempted one-half, he hoped that the grants the Government would make would be equiva- lent to three-fourths. Mr. W. J. Dutton (Nantwich) said he thought that under the recommendations of the Depart- mental .Committee agriculturists would un- doubtedly receive considerable • relief. Any relief they had had from the Agricultural Rat- ing Act had been swallowed up by the Educa- tion Act. The discussion was adjourned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>BRILLIANT SUCCESS.</head>
        <p>A BRILLIANT SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>"Was your (garden a success last 3-ear?" "Great Scott, yes? My neighbour's chickens took the prizes. at the poultry show I" Scribner's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>POTATO CULTURE.</head>
        <p>POTATO CULTURE.</p>
        <p>The fine division of seed potatoes is interest- ing as an experiment, but it is not adapted to practical work. A whole set is desirable, but should not be too small; on the other hand, a large set increases the cost of the seed. A medium-sized set will answer all practical pur- poses, and I doubt whether it is not the most economical. The successful culture of the potato, however, also depends upon other con- ditions, the variety, the time of planting, the suitability of the soil, the manure used, and after cultivation. These remarks are addressed to those who have already planted. Growers this year, whether in a large or small way, will be well advised to use a nitrogenous manure, in dressings of 561 b- each, if nitrate is used equal to newt. per acre; first two or even three when the plants appear above ground, later when they are one-third grown, and finally before flowering. This suggestion is made on the assumption that they have re- ceived a good dressing of dung or preferably half a dressing, suPplemented with basic slag or superphosphate, and some potash. I believe the slag is to be preferred owing to the purifying and decomposing action of the lime which'it contains.—James Long, in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>MANURING POTATOES.</head>
        <p>MANURING POTATOES.</p>
        <p>Except on the obdurate soils, says "The Times," the situation is satisfactory and the outlook encouraging. The early spring corn came up well, some of it already obscuring the clod, while the winter cereals and beans, espe- cially the latter, are healthy and active. A good deal of the wheat is thin and irregular, but if the warm weather continues it may till suffi- ciently to fill up the intervals. Much depends upon the weather of the next week or two as to w fdeficiency of plants can be made goodoyr N branching of those that u The planting of main , crop potatoes is making good progress, although some difficulty is experienced in procuring suffi- cient b far dr thet theh the stooling winter. or hands to complete the work as quickly as could be desired. The practice of sprouting the tubers beforehand has many advantages, but it does not expedite the planting of the sets, as care has to be taken to avoid breaking the shoots. The early potatoes planted in January and the'first half of February are already show- ing above ground.</p>
        <p>With reference to potato growing, the results of an interesting experiment in the winter and spring. application of farmyard manure is described in a report published from the Harper case was ploughed down, and in Athdeatmle formerlasttCerollsegpreec' Newport, Salop. The dressing inad in the drills at the time of planting. All artificial manures, which were the same in both cases, were put on when the tubers were planted. The effects upon the crop were well marked and in accordance with reasonable expectation. The potatoes planted directly on the manure were much stronger and healthier in May and June than those on the other plot, which had not yet been able to draw upon the more widely distributed supplies of nutriment. By the first week in July the COI) on the land manured in winter began to overtake the spring manured crop, and had forced well ahead by the end of the month and maintained the advantage till the end, the final result being a margin of from 16s. to 365. per acre, according to the variety of potato, in favour of winter application. The sowing of mangels has made a promising start, the medium soils having worked into a fine mould without trouble.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>TITHE EXTINCTION. A Slow Process.</head>
        <p>TITHE EXTINCTION. A Slow Process.</p>
        <p>A report issued by the Board of Agriculture deals with proceedings under the Tithe, Copy- hold, Inclosure, Common Land, Drainage, and other Acts during 1913. It shows that the tithe rent charge, which in 1836 amounted to just over four millions, is now &lt;0,691,237, the pro- visions for extinction having in 77 years re- sulted in a diminution of the total by 9 per cent. At the same *rate of progress it will take nearly 900 years before the process is completed. Ap- plications for " merger," by which process the tithe owner who is also owner of the land may merge the charge in the land and so extinguish it for ever, show a considerable increase. The total number of manors affected by the enfranchisement of land under the Copyhold Acts, with the sanction of the Board or their predecessors, has reached 2,611, the number of transactions involved being 22,840. , The gross sum paid for enfranchisement has been A:2,750,102, in addition to the creation of rent charges amounting to &lt;41,227, and the assign- ment of 1,388 acres of land to the lords for com- pensation. One hundred and eighty-three ap- plications were received in 1913. The septennial average prices of British corn issued show that wheat in 1913 was 4s. Id. per bushel, a price not previously reached since 1889, when • it was 4s. 21d. - •• "</p>
        <p>In accordance with the septennial prices of corn, the value of the rent-charge has steadily increased since 1905, and was in 1913 „t 7.5 16s. 4d. For the whole period 18364913 it stands at .t9l 17s. old., so that the tithe payer has had a good deal the best of the bargain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>SHIP CANAL COLLISION.</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL COLLISION.</p>
        <p>Sequel to the Sinking of the Solway Prince at Latchford.</p>
        <p>The Court ,of Appeal on Monday delivered judgment in the appeal by the Manchester Ship Canal Company from an order of Mr. Justice Eve in an action brought by them against Mr. Frederick W. Horlock. The dispute between the parties arose over a sinking of the Solway Prince in the fairway of the Ship Canal at Latchford. The owners of the vessel gave notice of its abandonment, and the underwriters paid on the basis of construc- tive total loss. At an expense of £3,600 the Canal Company raised the ship, and at their in- stance it was sold by auction to Mr. Horlock for £1,675 under the statutory Powers conferred Oil the Canal Company. . Difficulty arose about the completion, Mr. Horlock declining to pay the balance of the pur- chase money unless the Canal Company exe- cuted a bill of sale instead of the delivery ord6r offered by the plaintiffs. The position of the company was that the vessel was constructively lost, and that the register was or ought to have been closed, and that if a bill of sale were exe- cuted by them it would have been nugatory on that ground. They, therefore; brought an action for specific performance, but the action was dismissed on the ground that the purchaser ' was entitled to have transfer by bill of sale. Lord Justice Swinfen Eady, giving judgment, regarded the decision of Mr. Justice Eve as er- roneous. It was clear, that the cost of raising and repairing the Sol*ay Prince would greatly have exceeded the value of the ship when re- paired. It was, therefore, a constructive total loss, and under such circumstances the register was by the act to be considered as z;losed, and the purchaser was not entitled to refuse to com- plete because the Canal Company would not execute a bill of sale. It was urged that, although this might have been the case if the vessel had been sold when it was at the bottom of the canal, yet that posi- tion was altered once the ship had been raised.</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>IN tHE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN tHE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>General sowing of all kinds of vegetables should be done during May. Peas, lettuce, turnips, carrots, onions, B:c., for main.crops can now be sown._</p>
        <p>Hardy annuals of all sorts should be got in not later than the second week of May, and suc- cessive sowings of sweet peas should be made. The hoe should be kept busy in all borders, as weeds have the art of germinating very quickly during May.</p>
        <p>EARLY FLOWERING CHRYSANTHEMUMS.</p>
        <p>Old plants which provided cuttings for the season's stock should not altogether be dis- carded. These will be found to be extremely useful for giving an early supply of flowers, either planted in some odd corner or in the mixed border. The young plants should now be fully exposed to the weather, and planted out as soon as they are sufficiently hardened. They may be pinched now or after they have obtained a start.—" The Garden."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>BRIGHTENING UP WINDOWS AND BACK</head>
        <p>BRIGHTENING UP WINDOWS AND BACK</p>
        <p>YARDS.</p>
        <p>The whole appearance of a back yard is much improved by a few plants. Even if the whole yard surface is devoid of soil plants may still be grown. An old bucket, butter tub, or rough. made box will serve for the growing of plants, such as ferns, London pride, periwinckles, creeping Jenny, nasturtiums, ivy or convolvu- lus. These plants are suitable for shady yards, but if there is plenty of sunshine, other plants can be utilised very effectively clematis, scarlet runner beans, Canary creeper, and sweet peas might do if the atmosphere is not too smoky. Window boxes may also be utilised for brightening up the surroundings, and there are I lots of suitable plants which may be grown in them to relieve the dull monotony of the bricks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>TWO BEAUTIFUL AUBRI,ETIAS.</head>
        <p>TWO BEAUTIFUL AUBRI,ETIAS.</p>
        <p>When visiting the famous rock gardens at Friar Park, Henley-on-Thames, on Friday last, we were pleased to find aubrietias—Mrs. Lloyd- Edwards and Fire-Bing—growing to perfection. Both were artistically grouped in large masses between huge sandstone boulders, and the first named was a perfect sheet of violet blue flowers. Fire Bing, though not so free with its blossoms, was, however, a charming sight, the warm red flowers showing to perfection when kissed by the afternoon sun. Both ought to be grown in , every rock garden where spring flowers are op- preciated.—" The Garden."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>ASPIDISTRAS.</head>
        <p>ASPIDISTRAS.</p>
        <p>_ No doubt some readers may have as a room plant the parlour palm or aspidistra. This may be grown for several years in the same pot, but there comes a time when the pots get too full of roots, and the soil becomes exhausted, with the result that the leaves take on an unhealthy ap- pearance and split. When this happens it is high time to set about repotting, and now is the best season for doing so. If the plants are very large they should be broken up, and we have found it a good plan i to wash away all the inert i soil, as in this way t s an easy matter to dis- entangle the roots with the fingers without using a knife. A good compost is a mixture of fibrous loam and leaf mould with plenty of sand added. Some years ago we used only the fibrous parts of he loam after shaking out all the finer parti- cles, and we were so pleased with the growth the plants made that we have continued the treat- ment. It may be well to say that silver sand was added freely to the soil used. After potting give a good watering, and be very careful with the water pot until it is seen that the roots are on the move and are working in the fresh soil.</p>
        <p>In his view, the raising of the ship made no difference. When a ship had ceased to be regis- tered by reason of abandonment she must be re-registered, and this could not be done until she had been re-surveyed. Moreover, this was not sold as a registered ship, as Mr. Horlock contended. The Master of the Rolls and Lord 'Justice Pickford concurred. Tbe appeal was, therefore, allowed, and judg- ment entered for the Canal Company, with costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>EARLESTOWN PROOF –</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN PROOF -</p>
        <p>And not only Earlestown proof, but proof which has stood the test of time. More con- vincing testimony would be impossible to find. On May 18th, 1907, Mrs. J. Poden, of 77, Earle- street, Earlestown, said: " Doan's Backache Kidney Pills are very good indeed. I have used them several times for backache, and they have always proved very satisfactory. I've suffered a good deal with backache and shooting pains for many years. These pains were so severe that they aragged me almost to the ground. "I find Doan's Pills good for headaches and : also dizziness; in fact, they benefited my health altogether. They have done wonderfully in my case, and I have advised a good many to use the pills. (Signed) J. FODEN." On November 13th, 1913—OVER SIX YEARS LATER ! —Mrs. Foden said: "I am still keeping in the best of health. I heartily confirm all I said , for Doan's Pills some time ago." If von have any such clear signs of kidney and bladder disorders as backaches, urinary troubles, gravel, dropsy, lumbago, or rheuma- tism, persevere with Doan's Backache Kidney Pills until every trace of the dread kidney disease is gone. Doan's Pills assist the urinary system like a laxative assists the bowels. Price 2/9 a box, 6 boxes 13/9; of all dealers, or from Poster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Don't ask for back- ache or kidney pills—ask DISTINCTLY for DOAN'S Backache Kidney Pill, the same as Mrs. Paden had.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. 33ellamey, president of the National Union of Railwaymen, spoke at a mass meeting at Wigan, when a resolution approving the union programme was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>MOTOR NOTES.</head>
        <p>MOTOR NOTES.</p>
        <p>THE STORY OF RUBBER.</p>
        <p>We of the present generation live in an age of rubber, and should have difficulty in imagin- ing the world-without it; yet how many people —even among those thousands who use Michelin tyres all the year round—have any but the vaguest ideas on the history of the useful com- modity, or have seen a specimen of the raw material as it is brought into the market? Although it is only within comparatively recent years that rubber has come to play such an important part in our daily life, it was known to the Spanish conquerors of South America as far back as the beginning of the sixteenth century, and it is on record that Columbus met with it in Hayti while on his second voyage of discovery. Several writers of the period mention that. the Indians of Mexico played a sort of tennis with light springy halls made from the gum of a tree, and we learn that the Spanish soldiers smeared their hempen cloaks with the same material to render them waterproof. The reports of this wonderful gum created some sensation in Southern Europe, and the few samples which were obtainable found a ready sale to enthusiastic collectors of curiosi- ties at a price which puts the present market value quite in the shade In several instances, small pieces of solidified rubber changed hands at as much as a guinea an ounce. In 1736 the eminent naturalist La Condamine, who was in charge of an expedition appointed to settle the question of the shape of the earth, sent from Ecuador to the Academy of Sciences at Paris a parcel of dark-coloured, resinous matter to which in an accompanying report he gave the name of caoutchouc. The solidified body which reached Europe did not at first seem capable of being put to any practical uses, but the wonderful stories of the variety of ways in which the sap was employed by the natives soon induced scientists not only in France, but also in England and other countries to 'attempt the discovery of a solvent. They did not meet with much success at first, and it was only in 1823 that experiment produced a solution by means of which cloth could be covered with rubber; while the process of vulcanisation, which is all- important in the manufacture of rubber articles to-day, was not discovered for a hundred years. Although rubber was first met with in Span- ish America, and fine Para is still considered to some extent as a standard of raw rubber, almost the whole of the region lying within about 250 miles north and south of the Equator may now be classed as rubber-producing country. Indeed this will be very apparent to anyone who has studied the many prospectuses of rubber com- panies, for they refer to districts not only in America, but in many parts of Africa, the East Indian Islands, and Ceylon. A considerable amount of rubber is exported also from India and Madagascar, and experiments in plantation are being carried on in Queensland. Rubber is obtained from an immense variety of trees, ranging from the giant Hevea (from which is obtained the famous " Para to the Guayule (which seldom exceeds a height of three feet) and a diversity of shrubs and vines, The methods of obtaining the rubber-bearing juice or latex, and coagulating it to form mar- ketable raw rubber, naturally vary to some ex- tent in different parts of the world. In the case of large trees it is usual to make a cut or punc- ture in the bark, of such a depth as not' to injure the wood, from which incision the milky liquid flows. It is then caught in cups which have been placed in readiness. This is invariably done on plantations, but in many parts where the juice is collected from forest trees the natives engaged in the work have adopted the short-sighted policy of cutting down the trees, with the natural result that only one yield of rubber is obtained instead of many. Efforts are being made to put a stop to this practice, which if persisted in would obviously have the result of destroying the whole of the indigenous supply in many districts. As regards smaller plants and vines, these are often chopped into short lengths, from which the precious juice is obtained by crushing. (To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
