      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</p>
        <p>Are the Roads becoming too Danger- ous for Cyclists?</p>
        <p>Several recent distressing accidents must give us furiously to think. Unfortunately, there seems to be no getting away from the fact that our roads are becoming more dangerous every year, and from a healthful pastime cycling seems likely to degenerate into a nerve racking exercise. In towns, electric cars with their dangerous rails, omnibuses and, worst of all, taxi cabs, make riding a very doubtful pleasure, and in the country scorching motorists seem to think that a swift car carries with it the free- ' hold to the highway. Personally, I have always I found the passing motorist both considerate and courteous, but it is just the few black' sheep who give the whole flock a bad name. How- ever, that be as it may, the effect on the wheel man is the same. But there is a way, out. The various local authorities hold the future of cycling in their own hands. By giving atten- tion to by-roads thousands of miles of country will be opened to the rider which are now closed to all but the most venturesome, pedes- trian by mud in winter and ruts and dust in summer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Punctures.</head>
        <p>Punctures.</p>
        <p>The pneumatic tyre has one great disadvan- tage and one only, the liability to puncture. Personally I put little faith in the various pre- ventives which are put on the market and then disappear. Always excepting such articles as inch nails bolt upright, a tyre becomes liable to puncture in proportion to its age and condition. Whole solution and patches are all very well in their way, but there comes a time when the only remedy is a new tyre. Incidentally the various repair outfits are a marked improve- ment on those of a few years ago. Who, for ex- ample, ever used the small piece of sandpaper to prepare the inner tube for patching, when a match with a little moisture gave a much better result? Now makers are beginning to adopt the same principle, and what looks like a glorified wax vesta has taken the place of the useless sandpaper. Now that a tarred surface for roads is so common, cyclists will do well to see that no small pieces of grit become firmly attached to the tyre, as such almost invariably work through the rubber, damaging both outer cover and inner tube.</p>
        <p>Second-hand Machines. During the last year or two the number of ad- vertisements for second-hand machines has shown a very Marked decrease. To an old cyclist it does not seem very long since the only alternative to a new sixteen-guinea machine was a second-hand one, and consequently the number of people who found it necessary to go abroad at the commencement of the cycling season, or to give up riding by doctors' orders, was really astonishing. "Punch's" advice to those about to get married can well be applied to the intending purchaser of a second-hand machine: "Don't." Genuine bargains may be had sometimes, but I am afraid the contrary is far more often the case. Even if only a com- paratively small sum is necessary to put the purchase into good running order, a cheaper new machine is a far better investment, par- ticularly if by a new firm. First-grade tyres, for example, after a season's wear, are no better than second-grade ones, and the same is true of almost every other part of the machine. If a bicycle can be picked up at second-hand price, in practically new condition, well and good; otherwise, keep clear of the second-hand machine.</p>
        <p>Ac6uracy of Taximeters. I daresay many of our readers have often had their doubts about the reliability of taximeters, as they have watched the money ticking away at an alaiming rate. To the comic paper Scotch- man the ordeal must be an awful one, but to he overcharged is quite the last straw. In this connection the annual report of the Chief of the Weights and Measures Department of the Liver- pool Corporation affords interesting reading. It appears that during the last year 503 taximeters used on hackney cabs in the Mersey city were sent for examination, and no fewer than 157 were found tA be incorrect as regards time or distance 'registration, or both, or faulty in that fraudulent manipulation was possible. What the state of affairs is in the Metropolis, the taxi cab paradise, I have no information, but rather suspect a. similar state of affairs. If anything a defective taximeter is worse than the most extravagant estimate of the jeliu, as people are inclined to place implicit confidence in mechanical contrivances, while retaining no misconception of human infallibility,</p>
        <p>An Exacting Engine Test.</p>
        <p>The "practical Engineer" gives some inter- esting details of a recent engine test carried out in New York under the auspices of the Auto- mobile Club of America. An ordinary four- cylinder motor was run continuously for two weeks without adjustment of the motor, car- buretter, sparking plugs, or magneto, at a speed of 1,100 revolutions per minute. At the end of the time the speed was increased by about one- fifth per cent. for one hour, when the engine developed 53.6 horse-power, compared with 38 horse--power previously. According to some rather fantastic calculations of doubtful accu- racy, the mechanical activity during the period under question would have carried it 147,000 miles on a level at 46 miles per hour, or have climbed an 8 per cent. gradient 10.000 miles long at 27 miles per hour.</p>
      </div>
