      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>TWO NEW MAPS.</head>
        <p>TWO NEW MAPS.</p>
        <p>BY SILVER-STREET.</p>
        <p>I have received from Messrs. Pearse, the War- rington agents for the sale of Government maps, Sheets 35 and 44 of the new series of maps on j</p>
        <p>the scale of 1 inch to 1 mile. They are 2s. each mounted on linen to fold in case, and are a striking improvement on the old shilling sheets. No. 35 is called Bolton, Warrington and St. Helens,' but includes parts of Manchester and Liverpool, and all Wigan and Bury. In the four corner e are Altrincham, Gateacre, Ormr— kirk and Bury, and as the map measures 27in. by 18in., it covers an area of nearly 500 square miles. Appleton Hall and Walton Hall mark its extreme southerly border. No. 44 starts southward from where the fore- going leaves off, and, roughly, its four corners contain Eaton Hall, Rode Heath, Castle Mill and Garston. Hattford marks the centre. It includes Chester and Crewe. Of course, they are beautifully engraved and absolutely accu- rate. The roads are all picked out in brown, the water in blue, the woods in green, and every pond, farm, bridge, church, and even windmill. is marked. The great fault of all these Ord- nance maps is that there is no overlap. The portion of Rostherne Mere in one map is not repeated in the next. Now the French Ord- nance maps have included these overlaps for thirty years, and Messrs. Bartholomew have always made it one of their attractive points. But the British Ordnance Survey, never. Some day I hope to return to this subject. Mean- while you who ramble and you who cycle are unfortunate, for Warrington is so close to tli, edge of one map that you are obliged to purcha, the other in order to plan out your routes into Cheshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>MOTOR CYCLE AND CAMERA,</head>
        <p>MOTOR CYCLE AND CAMERA,</p>
        <p>Bicycling has been responsible for a good deal of the popularity of the hand camera, the cyclist rarely bothering himself with a stand camera and tripod, at least in one of the larger sizes usually associated with stand work. The rapid growth into favour of the motor bicycle and sidecar might tend to alter this. Not that the stand camera can ever resume its old pre-emi- nent position; the advantages of the other type are too marked for that. But at least the modern methods of locomotion remove the ob- jection that a stand camera is a burden. A half-plate out :t makes an inappreciable addi- tion to the load of a motor cycle, and whatever advocates of the hand camera may say, it un- questionably allows a far higher average of successes by the way it lends itself to more deliberate wo:</p>
        <p>A DUTCH MOTOR CAR. By the way, I was in collision at the Cause- way crossing on Tuesday morning. I was struck on the handlebar by a snellpaardelooszoondeer- spoorwegpitroolrijtung. lam still alive. Ne, it has not affected my brain. I have been read- ing "A Wanderer in Holland," and the author says this is what the poor Dutchman has to say when he means a motor car. Fancy being laid out flat by a thing that long! It's a good deal better to be an Englishman, isn't it?</p>
        <p>SIXY'S HOLE. The only map on which you will find it marked is Sheet No. 44, mentioned at the be- ginning of this article. Of course, Bacon's mars don't show it, and not sken on the nearly fault- less sheets from Bartholomew's do you find this perfect gem of a lake. It is a quarter mile long and occupies a hole caused by a salt subsidence 70 years old. In fact, the sides are giving way now in many places. It is where the Warring- ton Wesleyan C.C. spent last Saturday evening. Twenty-four of them had had a delightful spin to Mrs. Williamson's, Plough Inn, at White- gate, 13 miles, and after a rare tea they set oi down the fields to Sixy's Hole. It is only three- quarters of a. mile from the inn, and, ye anglers, it is well stocked with fish, including pike an trout. How to find it by rail? It is a mile to the east plougo W Plough Inhni,teagnadte tShtaattioisn.haAlfs_kwatyh.e owayf:loourte she. you will order tea there. To photographers it is a bundle of pictures. but after a rainy spell, good strong shoes are needed..</p>
        <p>CLUB RUNS TO-DAY. Wesleyan C.C., Davenham, 2.30. Sankey Bridges Wesley Guild, Knutsforda 20.</p>
        <p>Lamp time: To-night, 8.35: next Saturdays 8.50.</p>
