      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>MOTOR 'BUS FATALITY.</head>
        <p>MOTOR 'BUS FATALITY.</p>
        <p>Shocking Accident near Crosfield's</p>
        <p>Works.</p>
        <p>DRIVER EXONERATED.</p>
        <p>Still another fatality has to be recorded in connection with the Corporation motor 'buses. The victim was John Geraghty, aged 25, a general labourer, employed at Messrs. Cros- lield's Soap Works, and lodging at 27, Kerfoot- street, and he was killed on Saturday as he was coming from the works. Mr. Coroner Brighouse conducted the inquest at Warrington on Monday, when Mr. A. T. Holloway appeared for the Corporation, Mr. Crawford for the firm, and Mr. Dickinson for Mr. H. A. Pilkington, St. Helens, another party concerned. Dr. Joseph and Mr. Pilkington were also present. Patrick Geraghty, farm labourer, Hallam House Farm, said the deceased, who was his brother, was a very healthy man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>APPEARED BEWILDERED.</head>
        <p>APPEARED BEWILDERED.</p>
        <p>- -- William Henry Grundy, insurance agent, 54, Pinner's-brow, said that at 12.3 noon on Sat- urday he stood opposite Crosfield's Works by Milner-street. He saw the 'bus coming from Warrington in the direction of Green-street, pass the Fire Station, and at the same time he heard the sound of a motor horn from the oppo- site direction. He saw a motor car travelling in the opposite direction to the 'bus. Both were on their proper sides and travelling about eight miles per hour. Deceased was in the act of cros- sing the road from Crosfield's side, and he ap- peared to be bewildered, and on hearing the motor horn paused in the centre of the road. He then stepped back out of the way of the car right in front of the 'bus. The driver, doing his utmost to avoid deceased, swerved towards the pavement, but the right-hand wheel or the mud- guard struck deceased and knocked him down, the front wheels passing over his legs and the back wheel over his neck. By the Coroner: He did not notice a stationary horse and lurry by Milner-street. Andrew Fleming, employed at Crosfield's and living at 35, Wakefield-street, said the bus was a yard from the tram lines. After the accident the 'bus pulled up in 15 yards. The driver made the 'bus swerve.</p>
        <p>THE DRIVER'S STORY. The driver, Thomas Evans, 63, Slater-street. said that he noticed deceased standing on the tram line on the left side of the road. There was a stationary horse and lurry opposite the works and a private car coming from the direc- tion of Liverpool. As the private car passed the lorry he heard someone shout "Hey up!" De- ceased, who had his face towards the private car, stepped backwards, but the mudguard of the 'bus hit him in the centre of the back. He had the brake on, and he would have stopped a few yards further on to pick up some of the people from the works. After the accident the deceased was picked up and taken into the works. As the workpeople were all leaving it made the thoroughfare busy. By the Coroner: There would be a space of five yards between the 'bus and the private car. Deceased would have been all right had he stopped still.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>MR. R. A. PILKINGTON'S VERSION.</head>
        <p>MR. R. A. PILKINGTON'S VERSION.</p>
        <p>I Mr. B. A. Pilkington, who was in the private car, said they saw the man in the street as they came round the corner. He was walking to- wards them, and would be about 60 yards away, They sounded the horn twice, and as soon as the man saw them he moved diagonally across tht, road towards the people coming out of the works. The 'bus was coming behind the man and to them, in the car. It did not seem as if the man saw the 'bus until it was right on him. De- ceased made an attempt to get out of the way, but was knocked down by the mudguard. He thought the driver of the 'bus did all he could. P.C. Chrimes, who saw the body after the acci- dent, did not think the man had had any drink. The driver, Evans, was re-called, and in answer to the Coroner said he was about eight or ten yards from deceased when he first saw him. He swerved as much as he could, but he could go no nearer the side, owing to the hun- dreds of people coming from the works. If the man had stepped forward instead of backward, the accident would not have happened.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>DRAMATIC WITNESS.</head>
        <p>A DRAMATIC WITNESS.</p>
        <p>It was stated that a man wished to give evi- dence oil behalf of the brother, and William Emmersdn, 37, Beswick-street, off Lovely-lane: went into the box, and in somewhat dramatic fashion gave his story of the accident. Aftei coming out of the works with the man he made for Milner-street and the deceased made for the road-side. Witness continued: "I was about five yards from him when the thing (the 'bus) came smash down. The 'bus "whizzed round."</p>
        <p>The Coroner: What would have prevented the accident taking place? Witness: The 'bus was too quick. If the 'bus had been going slower this would not have hap- pened. By the Foreman: He did not notice the motor car coming from the opposite direction. The road was clear and he did not hear the sound of a horn.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S ABNORMAL TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>The Coroner said he never liked a case where all the witnesses agreed, but those who saw an accident never saw it the same way. Such accidents must happen nowadays, because when the roads were first made no one thought they would be put to the use they were. If they had known the roads would have been made differ- ently. The roads of Warrington had always something of an abnormal nature on them, of which no one thought when the roads were made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>"ACCIDENTAL DEATH."</head>
        <p>"ACCIDENTAL DEATH."</p>
        <p>The jury retired, and on their return gave a verdict of "Accidental death," and exonerated the driver from all blame. They expressed their sympathy with the relatives, as also did Mr, liallaway and Mr. Crawford. The Coroner said he agreed with the jury, and in reply to his question Evans said he had only been driving for the Corporation a fortnight, but had a clean licence for nine years as a driver for another firm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WIDNES CHEMICAL VAT FATALITY.</head>
        <p>WIDNES CHEMICAL VAT FATALITY.</p>
        <p>Coroner Brighouse held aft inquest on Tues- day at Widnes on Patrick Dowd (38), of 23, Walmsley-street, employed at Sullivan's Works, who died from burns. The deceased was en- gaged emptying a vat of sulphide salt in a solidified state. The salt was about four feet deep, and planks were placed upon it for Dowd to stand on. When he had made a hole two feet deep he stepped back and fell into the liquor that had been dra.Wn off. In answer to Inspector Brothers, Mr. East- wood, assistant manager, said they would have to wait two or three days for the vat to cool off, and as they emptied one each day, it would in- convenience the working of the plant. This was the only fatal accident in eight years. The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death," and the coroner intimated that the firm wculd take note of the inspector's remarks.</p>
        <p>A rather remarkable position has arisen in connection with the arrangement which exists between the Warrington Education Committee and the Governors of the Grammar School. The latter propose the erection of a new school, and have applied for the sanction of the Board of Education. The Educatton Committee thought that the Grammar School governors should have officially supplied them with the details of the scheme before it went to the Board of Education, as the payment or non-payment of ,1400 a year by the committee to meet possible deficiencies is involved. This was the basis of a discussion at the meeting of the Education Committee on Monday evening, Councillor 11. Roberts presiding. The matter came up on the following minute of the School Management Sub-committee : " A letter was read from the Board of Educa- tion with reference to the proposals of the Governors of the Grammar School, requesting to be informed whether it is the intention of the Local Education Authority to continue their present guarantee of grant; and, further, sug- gesting a conference between the authority and the Board upon the general question of suitable and sufficient provision of higher education in the borough. It was decided to postpone fur- ther consideration of this matter until the par- ticulars of the governors' proposals, now in possession of the Board, were received. The Director was instructed to communicate this de- cision to the Board and ask to be supplied with the particulars referred to."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>What Indigestion does.</head>
        <p>What Indigestion does.</p>
        <p>Any man or woman who is suffering from indigestion is gradually being starved, because nourishment is not being obtained from the food eaten. That is not all, for the harmful products given off by undigested food enter the blood stream, and are carried to every part of the body, to the great detri- ment of your health. Thus indigestion means starvation and suffering, too. But Mother Seigel's Syrup possesses such a remarkable power to tone, strengthen and regulate the action of the stomach, liver, and bowels that indigestion becomes impossible. Good health reigns in its stead. After forty years' testing by tens of tholsands, Mother Seigel's Syrup is still renowned all over the world as a successful remedy for those distressing ailments which arise from a weak or disordered condition of the stomach, liver, or bowels.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MA</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MA</p>
        <p>Pi DIVERSION</p>
        <p>NEW GRAMMAR SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The Question of £4OO Grant From Education Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>MATTER FURTHER DELAYED.</head>
        <p>MATTER FURTHER DELAYED.</p>
        <p>A GOVERNOR'S VIEWS.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. S. Bin:toms, representing the Governors of the Grammar School, objected to the minute, as it would cause delay. He thought the con- sideration of the question should not be delayed any longer, as already there had been a great deal of delay. He thought the committee should then and there decide whether they were to con- tinue the grant of a sum not exceeding £4OO a year. He hoped the Chairman of the Sub-com- mittee would withdraw the minute, in view of the proposals the Grammar School Governors had submitted to the Board of Education. It was well known that a provisional site for a new school had been selected. The site con- sisted of 14 acres, as the Governors had in- formed the Board of Education. The price of the land had been provisionally fixed as be- tween the owner and the Governors. Councillor ROBERTS said he did not wish to in- terrupt Mr. Burgess, but he submitted that any discussion was informal and out of order, be- cause the proposals of the Governors ought to have come officially and formally to that com- mittee as the Education Authority, and upon such a formal presentation, due and proper consideration would be given to the matter. They could hardly listen to what was an ex- parte statement.</p>
        <p>'PERFECTLY IN ORDER." Mr. BURGESS: I thought I would be perfectly in order in telling you what our proposal is, But if you rule me out of order I shall say nothing further about it. COIMCMGE ROBERTS : As a matter of procedure, the proper course for this authority, to enable it to consider, as it ought to consider, these pro- posals, is for the proposals to be submitted offi- cially by somebody, either by the Governors or the Board of Education. Mr. BURGESS Said he submitted that such was not the point the Board of Education had raised. The Board had raised the question as to whether the grants would be continued. Councillor ROBERTS said that was part of the question. The Board of Education had asked them as the Education Authority if they were prepared to continue the present grant of a sum not exceeding £4OO a year, and it was inferred that the proposals submitted by the Governors depended to some extent for approval of the Board of Education upon the continuance of the grant. The Governors had submitted cer- tain proposals, including financial proposals, for the approval of the Board of Education, and the Board had asked the Education Committee whether it was prepared to continue the grant. The latter authority merely said before they de- cided they must have before them the Gover- nors' proposals. Ile thought the best way would be for the Governors to submit formally their proposals. Mr., BURGESS: There has been very great delay. We are anxious to get on. Councillor ROBERTS: You will agree this ' authority is not responsible for that delay. The matter dropped and the minute was unanimously adopted.</p>
        <p>It is alleged that the picture shows have a detrimental effect upon the children. As pub- lished in "The Examiner" last week, the School Medical Officer, Dr. G. W. Joseph, stated in his annual report that infectious diseases may spread through children excluded from school being free to go to cinematograph shows; and that such exhibitions might be one of the causes of defective vision- ' •</p>
        <p>The subject was again raised at the meeting of the Warrington Education Committee on Monday night—Councillor 11. Roberts presiding —when the Medical Inspection Sub-committee reported that they had considered the Medical Officer's report, and, after discussion upon ob- servations contained therein with regard to cinematograph exhibitions and their influence on the health of school children, it was decided to request the School Medical Officer and the Director of Education to submit alternative pro- posals for the consideration of the committee. It was further decided that the co-operation of the Health Committee should be enlisted in en- deavouring to bring about improvements in these matters, so far as they affect school chil- dren.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>ISILVER 'STREET SCHOOL</head>
        <p>ISILVER 'STREET SCHOOL</p>
        <p>The Education Committee Discuss the Famous Wesleyan Institution Taken Visit of Scholars to Picture Shows. Over by Education Committee.</p>
        <p>IS INFECTION SPREAD THERE?</p>
        <p>JOINT ACTION NEEDED.</p>
        <p>-••• • - - Mr. HOLMAN KINGDOM, moving adoption, said his sub-committee felt very strongly about the attendance of children at cinematograph places, especially of those debarred from going to school for various reasons. They should take joint action with the Health Committee, and any recommendation made would be taken in con- junction with the Health Committee. Canon MORLEY STEVENSON said that he saw from the report that in cases of measles children who had not already had measles were excluded from the schools. That meant a considerable amount of loss in the grants, and the children were allowed to mix with those infected at the street corners and elsewhere. Mr. KINGDOM thought that policy required to be extended rather than minimised. He felt more strongly, however, upon the question of cinematographs. They had case aftei• case where children who had been debarred from going to school, and who were attending the clinic going to the pictures. That made their medical system an absurdity. He would not allow a child who could not attend school to go to the picture places. They hoped to bring some particulars before the committee to show how they could deal with the matter. The report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>CHILD EMIGRATION.</head>
        <p>CHILD EMIGRATION.</p>
        <p>settling of the or What the Salvation Army Does and Could Do. There, is no phase of the emigration question more interesting than that concerned with the Great Britain in the larger and more hopeful environments which the Dominions afford. Of direct value to the Old Country, the work has orphaned and needy children of deep Imperial significance in the contribution it forms towards the development of the younger nations under the British flag. To this form of service the Emigration Department of the Sal- vation Army, under the direction of Commis- sioner Lamb, has been devoting considerable at- tention of late, being convinced that this was the best method of giving these unfortunate children a chance of becoming honest, indus- trious, self-supporting men and women. The success attending the efforts of the Army in this work has been very considerable, and a feature of the present season's emigration busi- ness has been the marked increase of interest on the part of Boards.of 'Guardians and Parish Councils in juvenile emigration, due probably to the operation of the New Local Government Board Order that no child over three years of age shall be maintained inside the workhouse. According to Mr. G. Bogue. Smart, chief Govern- ment Inspector of British Immigrant Children and Receiving Homes in Canada, it is esti- mated that 15,000 children could be placed an-. nually in comfortable homes and positions were they available. The Salvation Army has its Children's Homes in all parts of the Empire. where the young people afe under a kindly and sympathetic training until suitable foster par- ents can be found for them. Much care is exer- cised in this direction, while the children are visited periodically after they have been placed, and reports made to those interested in the wel- fare of the budding colonists.</p>
        <p>The passing of the famous Silver-street School out of the hands of the Wesleyan man- Cagers and into the possession of the Education ommittee will doubtless cause a pang of regret to many who can recall how nobly the school kept the banner of Nonconformist education fly- ing in the borough for a long series of years. The transfer was decided upon at the meeting of the Warrington Education Committee on Monday night, Councillor H. Roberts presiding. The Sites and Buildings Sub-committee re- ported that the managers of the Silver-street Wesleyan School attended the committee, and on behalf of the Trustees submitted the follow- ing proposals as a basis for the transfer of that school to the Local Authority, viz.: (1) The school and the caretaker's house to be leased to the Local Education Authority for a long period at an annual rental of .6120. (2) The authority to have the use of the buildings for a public elementary school, and on three evenings for an evening school. (3) The Trustees to have the use of the school at such times as it is not re- served for a public elementary school. (4) The authority to appoint a caretaker and to pay for all cleaning, decorating and repairs, and all rates. (5) The Trustees to pay the authority in accordance with the existing scale when the school is used by them. (6) The Trustees to pay the authority the amount of any loans which may be outstanding on any improvements on the buildings at the termination of the tenancy, if the tenancy is terminated at the instance of the Trustees.</p>
        <p>After the managers had withdrawn, it was unanimously resolved: "That the offer on be- half of the Trustees of the Silver-street Wes- leyan School for the transfer of that school to the Local Education Authority be accepted, and that the necessary steps be taken to complete the transfer by the issuing of the public notices and the provision of an agreement with the Trustees embodying the above proposals."</p>
        <p>IN A GENEROUS SPIRIT. Councillor lIENSHALL, proposing the adoption of the Minutes, said: We consider the manage- ment of the Silver-street School met us in a very reasonable, and even in a most generous spirit. (Applause.) Alderman BENNETT thought the committee should express their appreciation of the trustees for having done so much to provide educational facilities for so many years. The Silver-street School had filled a very useful space in the edu- cational requirements of the borough for more years than ho. could remember. He thought they were deserving of the committee's best thanks.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said there was no objection to the expression of such an opinion as uttered by Alderman Bennett, but the committee had not made a precedent of doing so when taking over isuch schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>MANAGERS' GENEROUS SPIRIT.</head>
        <p>MANAGERS' GENEROUS SPIRIT.</p>
        <p>THE PERIOD OF YEARS.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. JOLLEY asked what section 1 of the agreement about "for a long period" meant. He was not satisfied that it was desirable for the Education Authority to pay rent for old and out-of-date buildings for a long period, as it was only a temporary experiment. It was no part of their duty to retain old and unsuitable buildings, but to provide proper and suitable buildings. If the period meant 20 or 25 years, he would move the minutes back. Councillor HENSIIALL said the phrase was used, as they did not know how long the Board of Education would allow for the repayment of the money. They did not know whether the loan would be long or short. The proposal was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WIDNES SCHOOLBOY'S FATE.</head>
        <p>WIDNES SCHOOLBOY'S FATE.</p>
        <p>At an inquest at Widnes on Tuesday on Nor- man Day (9), of Dixon-street, another boy of nine years, Patrick Keenegan, was the chief witness. He said: "We Were playing together. I had a piece of tin in my hand and he snatched it from me and cut a wart." Dr. Porterfield said blood-poisoning was acute when he was called to see the boy. Had he not been left so long something might have been done.</p>
        <p>A verdict of "Accidental death" was re- turned.</p>
        <p>Tyne and Blyth shipbuilders have responded to the men's application for an advance of wages by granting id. per hour advance, to take effect next January:</p>
        <p>The second part of the Report deals with the acquisition of land, both urban and rural. Land is required by public authorities for hous- ing, drainage, street improvements, gas and water undertakings, schools and playgrounds, parks and open spaces. Hence the vital need for its being obtainable for these purposes, at a fair price by a rapid and cheap process. Under existing circumstances local authorities, railway companies and other quasi-public bodies can only obtain compulsory powers for acquisi- tion by a cumbrous and expensive procedure. In most cases either a private Act of Parliament is needed, or else a Provisional Order, which has to be confirmed by Parliament. Further, the method of fixing the price of land is cumbrous and expensive, and the prin- ciples upon which the price is fixed are unjust. As a result excessive prices are often paid at present. The Committee recommend that except in very special circumstances, local authorities and quasi-public bodies such as railway companies, should be able to obtain compulsory powers with- out having to go to Parliament in each case. In certain cases they should be able to make their compulsory order themselves; in others it should require confirmation by a Government Depaitment. In order to secure that a fair price should be fixed, and that the cost of fixing it should be small, it is proposed that the new Land Commissioners should fix a fair price for land acquired compulsorily. The price should be based on ,the principles contained in the Finance Act. It is also recommended that local authorities should be able to acquire land in advance of requirements.</p>
        <p>The Committee further recommend that pri- vate individuals who cannot acquire land re- quired for factories or houses, shall, subject to certain safeguards, acquire a lease of land com- pulsorily, the rent to be fixed by the Commis- sioners. They make a like proposal for the ac- quisition of way-leaves and easements. As the Committee point out, industry, and_the building of working-class dwellings are constantly ham- pered under existing circumstances, and in order that there shall be free and full development, it is necessary that land shall be acquired expe- ditiously and cheaply.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>CINEMA CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>CINEMA CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>THE LAND. FIFTH ARTICLE.</p>
        <p>Summary of the Inquiry Committee's Urban Report.</p>
        <p>TWO MILLIONS IN SLUMS.</p>
        <p>BY R. L. REISS, Secretary of the Land Inquiry Committee.</p>
        <p>The Urban Report of the Land Inquiry Com- mittee contains an exhaustive account of con- ditions in the towns and urban districts of Eng- land and Wales. It contains nearly 700 pages and costs only one shilling. The comprehensive nature of the investigation undertaken is illustrated by a map which indi- cates the towns investigated. The Report is divided into four main parts, the first of which deals with</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>HOUSING AND TOWN PLANNING.</head>
        <p>HOUSING AND TOWN PLANNING.</p>
        <p>Great as has been the improvement since the passing of the Housing and Town Planning Act, the housing conditions in many towns are de- plorable. A comparatively small number of 1 workers live in really good houses, -With a decent amount of open space. The majority live in long, dreary rows of houses, crowded too many to the acre, while probably two million persons live in dwellings which can accurately be described as slums.</p>
        <p>According to the last Census, no fewer than one-tenth of the whole population was over- crowded, that is to say, living more than two Persons to a room (including 'as rooms not merely bedrooms, but kitchens). The results of bad housing and over-crowding can be seen in the health and general standard of living of the nation. For instance, it is shown that one-half of all the tuberculosis patients at the London Dispensaries come from tenements of one or two rooms.</p>
        <p>In order to deal with the shortage of houses, which exists and has long existed in many towns, the Committee recommend that it should be a statutory duty upon all local authorities to see that adequate and sanitary housing accom- modation is available for the working-class population employed, or reasonably likely to be permanently residential, within their area. So as to ensure proper development in the future, every local authority should, within a stipulated period, prepare a preliminary town planning scheme, restricting the number of houses built to the acre and providing adequate open spaces. Proper housing and town planning can only be carried out if plenty of land can be obtained at fair prices. It is recommended that the price of land required by local authorities should be fixed by the Judicial Commissioners, that where there is not a sufficient supply of good sites there should be improved transit facilities, and lastly there should be a rate levied on land values which will have the effect of bringing a larger quantity of land into the market. With a sufficient supply of houses, and with the Rating reforms indicated in another chap- ter of the Report, rents will be more reasonable than they are at present. Even then there will be a residuum of the population who under existing circumstances are unable to pay for a decent house. The Committee, therefore, recom- mend a wide extension of the minimum wage principle so as to ensure that workers shall have an adequate wage. Adequate knowledge is an essential prelim- inary to the clearance of slums and the general improvement of housing. Therefore it is recom- mended that local authorities should make a complete survey of the housing conditions in their area, within'a limited period of time, and submit a scheme for dealing with the evils thus disclosed. The Central Authority will have greater power to insist upon local authorities performing their work satisfactorily, by the power of with-holding the proposed grants in aid of local rates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>ACQUISITION OF LAND.</head>
        <p>ACQUISITION OF LAND.</p>
        <p>TENURE.</p>
        <p>The third part of the -1:e-Port deals with the various forms of tenure under which land and buildings are owned and occupied. The Com- mittee recommend that Copyhold tenure, which is both inconvenient and irritating, should be abolished. The Leasehold system in its various forms is condemned as it at present exists, both as being unfair to the leaseholder and also against the interests of the public. For various reasons the Committee do not think that com- pulsory enfranchisement is, in general, the best remedy, but they recommend it in the case of land leased for schools or chapels, and buildings leased to local authorities. To deal with the general case of leaseholds of over 21 years, they recommend the right to renewal on terms to be fixed by a land commissioner. This would en- sure complete security for the leaseholder. But where the land is required for public improve- ments, there should be compensation to the leaseholder for the improvements lie has made. It is not proposed that the lessee should have to wait until the end of the term before making his application for renewal. In the case of tenancies of business premises, whether for a week, a year, or longer, the Com- mittee also recommend the right to renewal on terms to be fixed by the Commissioners. In , fixing rent, no account should be taken of the goodwill built up by the tenant, nor of any future improvements he may make. The Com- missioners should further be able to grant relief from unreasonable covenants and exactions by way of fine. _ - -</p>
        <p>This part of the Report coneliftles with a recommendation that the law of primogeniture should be abolished.</p>
        <p>R AT IN G.</p>
        <p>The concluding part of the Report deals with the intricate question of rating. Existing con- ditions are condemned on the ground that they</p>
      </div>
