      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Olive Branch Lodge. —Warrington District (M,11.)</head>
        <p>Olive Branch Lodge. —Warrington District (M,11.)</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO PAST GRAND.</p>
        <p>A pleasant time was spent on Saturday even- ing at the above lodge, the occasion being the presentation of a Past Officer's Certificate to Past Grand Fred Broadhurst, who has recently passed through the various offices of the lodge. The function was all the more interesting, as the District Officers made a special visit for the purpose of the presentation, and there was scarcely a lodge in the district that was not represented: The officers of the,district present were: Prov. G.M. Harry Lawrence, Prov. D.G.M. Edward Spann, P.P.G.M. Frank Webster (correspond- ing secretary), and I.P.P.G.M. George Highana. The lodge officers present were: N.G. William Hammon (presiding), supported by V.G. J. J. Cook, Lodge G.M. fly. Bentley, Assistant Secre- taries F. Broadhurst and T. S. Garnett, P.P.G.M. Alban Boyle' (secretary of lodge), P.G. Arthur Cotteril (treasurer), P.G. John Brown, P.G. Wm. Houghton, and P.O. George Brown. There were also many visiting brethren, amongst whom were: P.P.G.M. Massey (district lecture master), P.P.G.M. Ellis H. Clare (Doug- las, 1.0. M.), , P.P.G.M. Peter Lester, P.G. Walter Bennett and P.G. Albert Ayres. After the conclusion of the-ordinary business of the lodge, P.G. George Brown, on behalf of the lodge, extended a very hearty welcome to the District Officers, and referred in appropriate terms to their special visit that night in order to take part in those proceedings. P.P.G.M. James Massey then gave a short, but very interesting, lecture describing in an able manner the signs and symbols depicted on the emblem of the Manchester Unity of Oddfel- lows. THE PRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>The District Grand Master (Bro. Harry Law- rence) having been requested to make the pre- sentation, in the course of his remarks said he felt sure that Bro. Fred Broadhurst would ap- preciate the Emblem or Past Officer's Certifi- cate considerably more after hearing the ex- planation of same so ably given by P.P.G.M. Massey. He sincerely hoped that Bro. Broad- hurst would continue in his efforts to bring his lodge into pre-eminence in the district, and that the presentation which was being made to him that night would bring him pleasant recollec- tions of the many happy hours he had spent among his fellow Oddfellows. (Applause.) The Deputy Grand Master (Bro. Ed. Spann) referred to the services rendered to the lodge by Bro. Broadhurst, and said he could only echo the words expressed by the Grand Master, that the recipient of that Past Officer's Certifi- cate would not relinquish his efforts on behalf of his lodge, but would continue his valuable services, although he had completed his term as principal' officer of the lodge. (Applause.) The District Secretary, Bro. Frank Webster, said he was very pleased to be present that evening. It seemed to him that P.G. Broad- hurst was a worthy son of a worthy sire. His father had been a worker for the lodge, and was at present one of its trustees. He trusted that Bro. Broadhurst would push forward the pros- perity of his lodge. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>THE ACKNOWLEDGMENT. P.G. Fred Broadhurst, in acknowledging the presentation, said that he felt he could hardly realise ,the kind words spoken by the Grand Master and his deputy. Going through the various offices had been an education to him, and he would certainly continue to serve the lodge to the best of his ability. (Applaitse.) P.P.G.M. Clare proposed a vote of thanks to the District Officers and visitors for their pres- ence, which was seconded by P.G. G. Brown, and responded to by Immediate P.P.G.M. Higham. A hearty vote of thanks was given to the ar- tistes who had helped to enliven the evening's proceedings, namely, Bro. P. Lawless, who ably acted as pianist, and P.G. Dennett, P.G. Albert Ayres, Bro. Ayres and P.G. A. Cotterill, who contributed songs. P.P.G.M. Massey was also thanked for his able description of the Emblem of the Order. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>FIRST AID</head>
        <p>FIRST AID</p>
        <p>FOR WEAK DIGESTIONS.</p>
        <p>Almost everybody experiences times when the organs of digestion show painful signs of weak- ness. Some slight disturbance of health starts the trouble; then the patient turns against food, and dull, heavy pains in the abdomen give warning that the stomach is unable to do its proper work. Sometimes a false craving for food arises; if satisfied, the result is additional torture. Flatulence, a drowsy depression, sick headaches and nausea are other common signs of indigestion. _ .</p>
        <p>The foolish practice of flying to drastic, weak- ening purgatives at such times should be avoided. Indigestion arises from stomach weak- nesses, and the only effective method to remedy this debilitated state is to strengthen the feeble organs of digestion by supplying them with richer, better blood, so that they are made fit to perform their work. This is the true tonic treatment of Indigestion, by which natural method Dr. Williams' Pink Pills achieve great results. These Pills make the rich, new blood on which the digestive system depends to carry on its work of assimilating food, and because of this special power, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People strengthen weak digestions, impart a healthy appetite, and dispel all the painful dis- orders that arise from Indigestion, Nervous Dyspepsia, and Stomach Disorders. Begin to-day to strengthen your Digestion by starting Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. From the first you will enjoy a better appetite and your food will do you more good. Sold by all dealers, or supplied direct by Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, 28. 9d. a box, or 13s. 9d. for six boxes, post free. Substitutes are useless. FREE.—" What to Eat" is a useful diet book offered free to all readers who send a postcard to Book Dept., 46, Holborn-viaduct, London.</p>
        <p>In response to the appeal of the Dean and Chapter of the Chester Cathedral for £14,000 to complete the work of restoring the cloister buildings and refectory, sums amounting to £4,B6l'now have been promised.</p>
        <p>Q.—Please explain Jer. vii. 7: " The stork in the heaven knoweth her appointed times, and the turtle and the crane and the swallow ob- serve the time of their coming, but my people know not the judgment of the Lord." (.1.M.8.) A.—The prophet is upbraiding the Jews for their ignorance of God's purposes concerning them. In this they have less discernment than the migratory fowls. "Theirs were the cove- nants, the law, the promises," and from guch favours God had surely a right to expect some return. Yet when Jesus sifted Israel, chaff pre- dominated. The people perished for lack of knowledge; they did not discern the signs of the times.</p>
        <p>So in Christendom, the Gospel was sown at the beginning of the Age. We are now in the time of ,harvest, but only the few wise under- stand and discern the times and the signs; the vast majority "know not the day of their visitation."</p>
        <p>G.—Please explain what Jesus meant in Luke xii. 50: "I have a baptism to be baptised with, and how am I straitened till it be accom- plished." (G.H.) A.—Baptism means immersion. When Jesus was baptised by John, his immersion meant much more to Him than to John. The out- ward act was but a symbol of the immersion of Jesus into the will of God. From Jordan Jesus began to die; daily the process continued till Calvary saw the end. The symbol was over quickly; the 'reality lasted three and a half years. All this time Jesus was straitened. He longed to have his •baptisin accomplished; He was consumed with zeal to finish his sacrifice and to be immersed even unto death, for he knew his death meant the blessing of the many.</p>
        <p>Q.—What did the angel Gabriel mean when he told Mary that. her son should have given to him "the throne of his father David, and should reign over the house of Jacob for ever" (Luke i. 32, 33)? (P.E.D.) A.—While there is to be a literal restoration of God's ancient people Israel, we do not under- stand that the literal house and throne of David is referred to, but that the dignity, power and authority of that throne will be restored. Christ Jesus was of the line of David, and he was to be the one who in God's due time would re-establish the Kingdom of Israel. David was a type of Christ, and it is recorded of him that he "sat upon the throne of Jehovah" (I. Chron. xxix, 23), that is, he exer- cised power and authority as Jehovah's repre- sentative; so will it be with Christ: he will be vested with authority from Jehovah. He will reign until he "shall have put down" all that is opposed to truth and righteousness. The wilfully wicked will be finally cut off in the second death, while the willing and obedient will enjoy the blessings of the long-promised Kingdom.</p>
        <p>The basis of all this lies in the fact that Jesus tasted death for every man (lieb. ii. 9) and gave himself a ransom for all, to be testified in due time.</p>
        <p>God is now seeking those who are willing to suffer with Christ, and to be thus fitted to share with him in his glory. They reign with Him for the purpose of uplifting in that day "who- soever will."</p>
        <p>Q.—What does the prophet Obadiah mean when he says that certain of the Edomites who Presumably had immortal souls "should be as though they had not been" (v. 16)? (P.E.D.) A.—We understand that the Prophet Obadiah is referring to the Heathen nations as nations, not as individuals. The prophet Daniel shows that after the fourth universal Empire has served its purpose, God will establish a fifth kingdom—Messiah's—which will stand for ever (Dan: ii. 44). God made a promise to Abraham that his seed should bless all families of the earth, and many Scriptures show that it will be through the Israelitish nation that mankind will get their blessing, and only such as recognise and accept the channel through which God purposes to work, will obtain the blessings; these will even- tually be recognised as Abraham's children (see Rom. iv. 17).</p>
        <p>The doctrine of the inherent immortality of man is not taught in the Bible; on the contrary, it declares that "the soul that sinneth it shall die" (Bulk. xviii. 4).</p>
        <p>Q.—Please explain ha. xvii. 6. What it the symbolic meaning of the grapes left in the uppermost bough? (F.W.L.) A.—lf you refer to a critical translation of this verse, you will find that there is no reference to grapes in this connection. The statement is merely one of gleanings.</p>
        <p>We believe this 17th chapter has figurative reference to the eveningtide (ver. 14) of this present age (Gal. i. 4); the ruinous trouble com- ing from the political and ecclesiastical phases of Christendom (vv. 1-3) and the sad and appar- ently hopeless condition of Israel (v. 4). Our Lord said: "Except those days were shortened there should be no flesh saved; but for the elect's sake those days shall be shortened" (see v. 14), and there will be some left willing to hear the word of God—the pure language (vv. 7-8). The time of trouble will give place to a calm, and a few gleanings will be found when the olive tree—which probably represents the Jewish nation—shall be shaken. A few berries here and there from which oil (light and truth) may be expressed, will be found; and so from such a small beginning the blessings for man in the Coming Age will increase and finally culminate in Glory to God.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>BUCKLEY-STREET ANNIVERSARY.</head>
        <p>BUCKLEY-STREET ANNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>The anniversary of the Buckley-street Metho- dist Free Church Sabbath School took place on Sunday. Two sermons were preached by Mr. J. W. Cannon, of Leeds. There was an excellent attendance at the morning and evening services. During the afternoon there was a musical ser- vice. 16. A. Taylor, the Sunday School super- intendent, occupied the chair, and in the course of his address he laid great stress upon the value of the good work accomplished by the Sabbath School, but 'regretted that so many of the scholars left the school after attaining the age of 20.</p>
        <p>Mr. Cannon addressed the audienee upon the subject "Inquire Within." Miss L. Richardson read the lesson.</p>
        <p>The singing of the children and choir received great praise, and much credit was due to the conductors for their untiring efforts in training the children to such a pitch. Mr. A. Taylor con- ducted the singing in the morning and evening. and Mr. W. Richardson officiated in the after- noon. The collections for the day amounted to £l2 2s.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening Mr. Cannon delighted a large audience with one of his popular lectures, the title of which was "The pathos, humour and tragedy of child life." The collection at the close brought the previous day's amount to 415 9s. id.</p>
      </div>
