      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>CHANCELLOR'S VISIT.</head>
        <p>CHANCELLOR'S VISIT.</p>
        <p>The new Chancellor of the Liverpool Diocese, the Worshipful Harold Chaloner Dowdall, will hold his first misitation to Warrington this month for the purpose of admitting the wardens and sidesmen appointed at the Easter Vestry to their offices.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>RURI-DECANAL CONFERENCE.</head>
        <p>RURI-DECANAL CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>The meetings in connection with the Ruri- decanal Chapter and Conference will be held at the Church House, Bridge-street, on Wednes- day, May 20th, 'when the subjects for considera- tion by the Chapter will be "The Bishop of Oxford's Letter to his Clergy" and "The Revi- sion of the Prayer-Book," "The Diocesan Finance Scheme," and "The Duty of a Patriotic Churchman to Ireland."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>PARISH CHURCH ORGAN CLAD.</head>
        <p>PARISH CHURCH ORGAN CLAD.</p>
        <p>The- appeal for contributions to the Organ Repair Fund has been met with sums amounting to £l5, about half 'of the amount required for the alterations of the blowing apparatus.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>WYCLIFFE SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERsAny. •</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERsAny. •</p>
        <p>The Rev. Fred Hibbert, of Southport, occu- pied the pulpit at the special services held on Sunday in commemoration of the Sunday School Anniversary. There were large congregations at all the services. The choir rendered the anthems, "Blessed be the God and Father" (S. S. Wesley), and " Sing, 0 heavens" (Sullivan), in the morning and evening respectively. In thealfternoonthe scllolars of the Sunday School gave special music, under the conductorship of Mr. W. Brown, their superintendent. Miss A. Hall in pleasing manner, which w aqUartet from the Misses Hall and s v a and Messrs. Parr and Poole. Theorgan manipulated by Messrs. Payton followed renderediiing w ta b os y a solo and Wallington. The collections for the day (taken in aid of the school) amounted to £4l.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>ST. JOIIN'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. JOIIN'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The difficult service of standing and waiting e of the sermons preached by the the themetlelßev. W .of one at St. John's Church on Sunday. The Divine intervention in the second missionary journey of St. Paul was the illus- tration used. Intending to proceed into Bithynia, he and his fellow-missionaries were preventea without the slightest reason being given. And yet, as is shown by' sacred and pagan literature, a Christian Church existed there towards the end of the first century, sug- gesting very strongly how promising was the ground at the time of the intervention. To the enterprising advocate of Church extension, to stand and wait must have been exceedingly painful. But the Bithynian door closed in order that the Macedonian one might open to ampler and harder service. " There's a Divinity that shapes our ends, rough hew them how we will."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>WHO SHALL BE SAVED?</head>
        <p>WHO SHALL BE SAVED?</p>
        <p>Striking Discourse by Rev. H. Ogden.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. Ogden, preaching in Golborne- street Baptist Church, taking as his theme "The Aristocracy of the Spiritual Life," and basing his remarks upon Matthew xx. 16, "Many are called, but few are chosen," said : Christ was ever warning the disciples against the leaven of the Pharisees, and yet they were for ever feeding from the leavened loaf. The disciples should know that Christ seeth not as man seeth. That was just :what they did not know. The rich young man had just failed in a critical examination; the rich young ruler had gone away, self-sondemned. The disciples natur- ally inquired " Who, then, can be saved?" Wealth, rank, station, influence, do not count in this strange kingdom. Who, then, can be saved? We ask exactly the same to-day. We ought, but do not know that the first shall be last. We repeat the blunders of the past. if churches of fashion, wealth, influence and powei fail, who can succeed? 'Many are called, but few are chosen." We cannot divorce the text from the parable of which it forms a part. Different labourers are employed in the vineyard at different hours of the day. One labourer works twelve hours, an- other labourer works one. When the wages were paid the man who commenced last received his wages first, and in addition received in Pay the equal of him who toiled all the day. The I narrative says they murmured against the good man of the house. Had it been in the twentieth century there would have been a strike. The long hour toilers would have done more than murmured. The good master replied: "Is it not lawful for me to do what I will with mine own?" Had he lived 2,000 B.C. he would discover it was very unlawful. But the point of the Master had no relation to the labour market. No federation controls His labourers. No one could be a member of the Kingdom unless there was 103' in sacrifice Many can do the ordinary thing, follow the usual method, run in the rut, but only the un- usual can reach the joy of sacrifice. Carlyle said: "Without sacrifice life goes all to tatters." It becomes a frayed garment. 'The few" are the folks who can rejoice when an- Other's happiness is bought at the expense of their own. Their highest joy is the reflected joy of others. Their highest prosperity is another's opulence. Can we wonder "that few are chosen."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
