        <p>th,oro-n-g-hl3-- iun down in health, and so weak that everything I tried to do was an effort. I had dread- ful headaches almost daily, and violent attacks of Neuralgia. Then Neuritis came on in ray leg. It was - as though pins and needles were being stuck into me. And,oontiod add t tooloomyp. l troubles. Mrs' Elthant, London. I trying one thing and an- other in vain. When I got Dr. Cassell's Tablets, however, I soon found benefit. They put new life into me, and I shall never fail to recom- mend them." •</p>
        <p>_ Cure after cure, even in the most severe cases, have proved Dr. Cassell's Tablets to be the surest remedy ever devised for Nervous Break- down, AnEemia, Debility, Sleeplessness, Nerve Pains, Heart Weakness, Kidney and Stornll Disorders, Children's Weakness, Spinal and Nerve Paralysis, general vital exhaustion, brain fag, and all run-down conditions. Send 2d. to- day to Dr. Cassell's Co., Ltd., 418, Chester-road, Manchester, for a free sample. All chemists sell Dr. Cassell's Tablets at 103 d., is. 13d., and 2s. 9d. the 2s. 9d. size being the most economical.</p>
        <p>Afb</p>
        <p>name. The sweetness of her presence had been shed abroad in many homes, and her tenderness and sympathy had, on many occasions, brought help, consolation and hope to many present. They could not measure her work, for in these days love, loyalty and devotion were not to be measured by ordinary material standards. In the school she left a beautiful and wonderful influence, an example for the younger to follow. LETTERS OF APPRECIATION. Mr. KNOWLES read letters from those who had been connected with the chapel, and each was full of appreciatioa for the work done by Miss Houghton. The Rev. W. H. Drummond stated that generations of girls in the Sunday School owed more to Miss Houghton than they could ever repay, and in times of sickness she had gone like a ministering angel into their homes. The Rev. F. K. Freeston stated that Miss Houghton had worked for both chapel and Sunday School with a splendid zeal, and from pure love of her Church and scholars. She had set a high tradition, which would never be forgotten. If her name could not be put on the old school tablet, for she was not dead yet, it would always live with happy remembrances in the hearts of her scholars and fellow-teachers. Mr. T. Knowles, now in Ireland, a former superintendent of the school, stated that Miss Houghton's name had been synonymous with everything successful connected with Cairo- street.</p>
        <p>------- TAUGHT TWO GENERATIONS. Mr. PLINSTON spoke at length on Miss Hough- ton's good work, and said that during the 30 years she had been a teacher there were many instances where she had taught two successive generations of a family: Her length of service was in itself a record of which she could be Proud. (Hear, hear.) After dealing with the difficulties with which a teacher had to con- tend, he said that she had been a personal friend of hundreds of scholars. She had that affection and sympathy which alone could make a Sun- day School successful, There was no field of labour which had sucll a harvest as the Sunday School. Miss Houghton had been highly suc- cessful in her work. (Applause.) THE PRESENTATION. _ _</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN read the illuminated address, which was as follows:</p>
        <p>Dear Miss Houghton, The members, friends and scholars of Cairo-street Chapel and Sunday School desire to express to you their sincere appre- ciation of your long and faithful services as teacher in the Sunday 'School, and in connection with other institutions of the chapel. They feel that your loyalty has been, and will continue to be, an example and incentive to many, and that you have exercised, in countless ways, an influence which has always been for good. It is the earnest prayer of all that the light which you have shed over the lives of others may reflect back upon you richly through days of increasing peace and blessedness, and may be at all times a source of consolation and of joy.—(Signed) STANLEY A. MELLOR, minis- ter; F. W. MONKS, chairmah Chapel Com- mittee; F. HOLLINGSWORTH, DAVID PLINSTON, superintendents of Sunday School. 27th April, 1914.</p>
        <p>Making the presentation, the Chairman said that £26 was enclosed in the purse and empha- sised the fact that subscriptions had come in willingly. It was some slight acknowledgment of the valuable services Miss Houghton had rendered to the chapel, and a sincere expression °Move and affection they all felt for her. (Ap- plause.) MISS HOUGHTON'S REPLY.</p>
        <p>In reply, Miss Houoirrox said she could not tell them how much she valued the presents. She assured them she would always treasure the presents. It had been a pleasure for her to work for the school and .chapel. She, however, felt the gainer, beciftse she had received so much more than she had given. She would always be willing to help with the work that was being done there for the school, and for the cause of free reli,gion that was so ..I.:)ar to their hearts. (Applause.) She would look balk on that oeca-. siOn as the great event of lur life. Referring to Dr. and Mrs. Mellor, she said she felt the parting very much. Dr. Mellor had been a very great help to her, and she knew that the best way to thank him was for them to have courage to be loyal to their faith —(ap- plause)—and go on in the work that had been started, each of them doing their best. Then they would succeed. (Appla,Lse.)</p>
        <p>11CTV011. !fit Age</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>GIFT TO DR. MELLOR. The Pastor's Splendid Work.</head>
        <p>GIFT TO DR. MELLOR. The Pastor's Splendid Work.</p>
        <p>Cheers greeted the Chairman as he rose to refer to Dr. and Mrs. Mellor, who were the recipients at the second presentation. At any rate, for the present he said that was the last occa'sion on which they would have Dr. and Mrs. Mellor with them. They were ringing down the curtain on the close of the second act of Dr. Mellor's ministerial career. Ife thought they realised now perhaps better than ever that they might be thankful that they had had Dr. Mellor with them for two years, and it was</p>
        <p>(Cindinued in next °Junin.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>ST. PAUL'S CHURCH SALE.</head>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CHURCH SALE.</p>
        <p>Daughter of Former Vicar Recalls Days of the Past.</p>
        <p>NEED FOR GREATER MONEY HELP.</p>
        <p>To augment the Additional Clergy Fund, Parochial Organisations, and a grant towards the .21,000 required for the Day Schools in con- nection with St. Paul's Church, a sale of work was held in the Schools, Kendrick-street, on Saturday. The Vicar (the Rev. W. Brace(,lmp; presided, and the sale was opened I:y Mrs. Wray, of Timperley, daughter of a former Vicar of St t• U"s. _</p>
        <p>The VICAR said they were oblivd to have that sale of work for the reason that they could not do without money. They needed £5OO every year to carry on the Additional Clergy Pa ad and the Parochial Organisations, and he wanted to Day Schools, or they would Le Landed over to the Education Authority. They were glad to have Mrs. Wray with them, and her coming helped them to keep up connection with the Wilkinson family in that parish. (Hear, hear.; Mrs. Wray's father was the I.ighly-respected Vicar of that parish for 15 v..ars, and it vas nice to have his daughter there that day. He was sorry that Mrs. Wilkinson was unable to be present, but she had written a letter to Mrs. Bracecamp expressing her regret.</p>
        <p>ROOMFUL OF MEMORIES. Mrs.. WRAY recalled her long a.sso3iation with St. Paul's, and said that room was full of memories of childhood. The parish of St. Paul's Was, to all, of them, hill of memories of her dear father's work, and she hoped they would get all they wanted. She had'received nothing but kindnesses from St. Paul's, and she was glad to kaow how well and prosperously they were getting on, due, in a large measure, to their Vicar and his wife. She thanked them all for the help they had given and wished them every ' success. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>"GOADED HIM ON."</head>
        <p>"GOADED HIM ON."</p>
        <p>Mr. J. H. SMETHURST proposed a hearty vote of thanks to Mrs. Wray, and said that afternoon one was carried back to the days of years ago. As a small boy in that room Mrs. Wray`goaded" him on to do something in the prize distribu- tion. He thanked also the ladies who had worked hard with Mrs. Bracecamp at the head, and all who helped in the parish. He 'thought that afternoon marked a change in their procedure. For many years they had held the sale in the Conservative Club-room, but that day they had come back to the old premises, and he thought it was fate that Mrs. Wray should come to re- juvenate it. (Hear, hear.) Lady Greenall had sent her usual donation. (Applause.) The Rev. E. DOWNHAM (curate) seconded, on behalf of the assistant clergy in the parish. Mrs. WRAY, in reply, said she came to the school when she was five years old.</p>
        <p>„ The VICAR also thanked Mrs. Wray and the helpers, as well as the Rev. T. T. James, wile was also present The proceeds amounted to about £2OO.</p>
        <p>DR. MELLOWS FAREWELL—continued. •</p>
        <p>also a cause for thankfulness when they con- sidered the splendid work which he had been able to do there.</p>
        <p>He had presented religion, and the results of truly religious thought in an altogether differ- ent aspect, and from a different point of view from what it had been customary for them to listen, and it was a view that had been ex- tremely inspiring. It was likely to have good and permanent results in helping them to carry on the work for which that congregation and Sunday School stood, and had stood for so many years. They had every cause to be grate- ful and thankful to him for what he had been able to do.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>APPRECIATIVE RESOLUTION.</head>
        <p>APPRECIATIVE RESOLUTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. PERRIS proposed the following resolu- tion :</p>
        <p>This meeting of the members of' the con- gregation and Sunday School desires to ex- press to Dr. and Mrs. Mellor their appre- ciation of the services they have rendered to the chapel and school during their resi- dence amongst us, and to wish them God- speed in the work they are now taking up in Liverpool, and a happy and prosperous future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>PLEASURE AND REGRET.</head>
        <p>PLEASURE AND REGRET.</p>
        <p>Mr. Perris said he had feelings of pleasure, mingled with regret, in proposing that resolu- tion. He regretted that Dr. Mellor's residence with them had been so short, but it was better to have loved and to lose than never to have had the opportunity of loving at all. (Hear, hear.) The short ministry which Dr. Mellor had given them had been a great time in the history of Cairo-street congregation. (Applause.) It was bordering on 40 years since he, the speaker, came to Warrington, and he could not remember so short a period being taken up in such a remarkable manner as those two years had done with them.</p>
        <p>Dr. Mellor had stirred up Warrington to an extent which they had not seen for some time, and he hoped what Dr. Mellor had taught them would live and grow and not pass away like a thing that was forgotten, but that it would have a lasting impression on the congregation and upon the town. (Hear, hear.) He was sure that in Liverpool Dr. Mellor would meet with as fine a demonstration of kindliness and brotherhood as he got in Warrington.. He had received a call to go up higher, and he was ready for the call when it came. They wished him every success. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>"SUFFERING A GREAT LOSS."</head>
        <p>"SUFFERING A GREAT LOSS."</p>
        <p>Mr. PLINSTON seconded and said they were suffering a great loss by Di Mellor's departure. He was a rare minister' who combined splendid teaching abilities with a liking for plenty of work. (Hear, hear.) The two years seemed longer, because he had crowded so much work into them, and had gained such a splendid feeling not only amongst their congregation, but in most of the Progressive Societies in the town. When he first came he did not wait to feel his way, but went on straight away letting them know that a new personality had arrived in their midst with the determination to tell the truth at all times, and in all places. Probably he thought they were asleep, and that was his method of waking them, and he had kept them awake and thinking ever since. His chief interests seemed to centre in the young people, and he had done much good work in the adult classes and Young People's Society. He had always put high ideals of fellowship and personality before them. If his work had not been appreciated they would not complain about his leaving them. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>CHEQUE PRESENTED. The CHAIRMAN then presented the cheque to Dr. Mellor, with the hope that he and Mrs. Mellor would buy something with it that would remind them of Cairo4street. Liverpool was not far away, and they hoped to see Dr. and Mrs. Mellor amongst them occasionally, in order "to shed a little radiance on us poor indivi- duals, who are left so benighted at the present time." (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>DR. MELLOR'S __REPLY.</head>
        <p>DR. MELLOR'S __REPLY.</p>
        <p>_ Dr. MELLOR replied and said he thanked them for their gift and for the abundant overwhelm- ing kindnesses he had received since he came to Warrington. His feelings were of great un- worthiness. They had been very happy in War- rington, and they would treasure in their hearts the tender and delightful memories of their stay in the town.</p>
        <p>- They left Warrington with the feeling that they at Cairo-street would always remain their close friends, and they hoped that those whom they left would also treasure in their hearts some memory of them, and know that they, wherever they might be, were their friends. Their comradeship was not a matter of time or place, but a matter of eternity. If he had helped anyone he was glad, but it was only a slight return for the help they had given him in countless ways to make his life a better and finer thing. A minister could learn from his congregation, as well as they could learn from him. He did not want to leave them sadly, but joyously. They had nothing to regret, but an abundance of things for which to be everlast- ingly grateful and thankful. (Applause.) Votes of thanks to the artistes concluded the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ADULT SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>THE ADULT SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>The Story of a Marvellous Metal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>CYCLISTS MAKE JOLLY.</head>
        <p>CYCLISTS MAKE JOLLY.</p>
        <p>"A Marvellous Metal" was the subject of the lecturette given by Mr Harry Sherburn on Sunday at Stockton Heath Men's. This metal, said the speaker, was not some rare substance, such as radium, or some of the little known metals, but was the most common as well as the most useful of all—iron. He went on to show how widely it was distributed through nature in the shape of earthly oxides and carbonates, . how it was reduced to the metallic state, the ele- ments usually associated with it, and their in- fluence on its character and properties. He also showed how, by the elimination of some of these elements and the addition of others (as in the Bessemer and later processes), steel of many qualities and values are produced. Through these and many kindred discoveries and inven- tions, these industries had grown to the enor- mous extent we now behold. In the discussion it was mentioned that the use of iron seemed to run on parallel lines with civilisation. A gra- phic way to realise the value of iron would be to imagine, if possible, modern existence without it.</p>
        <p>,-Illin</p>
        <p>tAi</p>
