      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>SANKEY BRIDGES MEN'S.</head>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGES MEN'S.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. 0. Bennett, of Stockton Heath, gave a lecturette on "Art and religion." The speaker said there was an idea in the minds of many people that art and religion were opposed to each other. This was a great mistake. In all ages races had not only handed to us by works of art evidence of their occupations and pas- times, but also their conception of God.</p>
        <p>ACADEMY-STREET MEN'S. A well-composed paper was read by Mr. Albert Spittle on "Character." The writer pointed out how varied and innumerable were the subjects and professions specialised in, but the study of character—what is best and noblest, and all that the word involves—seems to have been relegated to oblivion. This is largely due to the debasing and demoralising influence of industrialism. The philosophy of the boy who by stealing the marbles of another boy deterred the latter from stealing his, seems frequently to have proved an indelible principle in manhood.</p>
        <p>LONGFORD ADULT SCHOOL. The lesson for the day was outlined by Mr. H. Broadhurst, who did justice to the subject. namely, "Life's goal" and "Thy kingdom come." Mr. Brown, of Manchester, spoke on war and its evils.</p>
        <p>The weatherCtYuCriLleldN GoutCmL CLUB. . propitious for the run to Itostherne, but only a dozen mem- bers of the .Warrington Adult School C.C. started out. With the exception of one "lost package," the outward journey was simply a spell of adoration for the country's charms. To quote Mr. J. Urry, the veteran cyclist and cycling litterateur, "who, possessing any love for a free and open life, the glory and freshness of the open air, would not be a cyclist? So glad atm I to get away from the hum and bustle of mundane affairs, to find a bosky bank and lie thereon, and think of my fortune of good health, a modicum of strength sufficient for my purpose, a blue sky above, and the daisy span- gled sward before me, sloping gently to ,the valley." The hour of repast, however, gave opportuni- ties for levity and mirth, at which one member proved "Star turn." After viewing the scenery from the churchyard and encircling the Mere, the club made for Hoo Green, and reached home before dark.</p>
