        <p>- Agents in Warringto— Messrs. J. R. Plekm ere and Son, Bridge Street Megsrs. Woods and Son, Commercial Chambers, 55, Horsemark et Street. Mr. Louis A. Voigey, 21, King Street. Mr. B. Lowe, 5, Buttermarket Street.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, MAY 2, 1914.</p>
        <p>THE HOPE OF PEACE. The discussion in the House of Com- mons upon the demand for an inquiry into the circumstances surrounding what Unionists have been pleased to call a Government plot, led, as it was .expected to do, to a complete fiasco. For the forces that had been set in motion matured quickly, and on the eve of the introduction of the vote of censure the Ulster Volunteers suc- ceeded in landing 35,000 rifles and a large consignment of ammunition, by a coup which, for insolence and law- lessness, has not had its equal in the annals of this country. Mr. Asourni described the gun-running exploit as an unprecedented outrage, and pro- mised that appropriate steps would be taken to vindicate the law. To gauge the gravity of this crime against the State we have but to read "The Times" narrative of what happened last week- end. Our contemporary states: "The towns and harbours at which the arms and ammunition were landed were cut off from all communication with the outside world for a space of from four to six hours. The local police in some, if not all, of these places were surrounded by a superior force, and, with the Coastguards and Cus- toms officials, prevented from taking</p>
        <p>action. All roads leading to the respec- tive disembarking centres were pick- eted by strong guards of Volunteers, who allowed no one to pass without a permit, and thus drew a protecting chain around each centre. The tele- phone and telegraph wires were tem- porarily put out of working. order. In short, strict military discipline was imposed for a considerable time, and in certain prescribed areas no move- ment of any kind, nor any communi- cation with outside sources was allowed."</p>
        <p>That surely is an act of war, and even the present Opposition could not per- sist, with a straight face, in persisting in talking of the "plot " against Ulster. If the Liberal Government had had no counter plot to this Ulster menace the Liberal Government would have had to give way to somebody who better understood the task of government. It was at this point thdt the strength ' 'r of the Liberal Government asserted it- self. With its opponents practically at its mercy, from a strategical point of view, they still cast about for a way out. Mr. CHURCHILL was the one to lead the way. After having told the Opposition that their vote of censure was like a vote of the criminal classes upon the police, he turned to Sir EDWARD CARSON in dramatic fashion. "The use of force," Mr. CHURCHILL told the Opposition, " rests with you." But why, Mr. CHURCHILL went on, should not Sir EDWARD,CARSON, who has taken such risks for war, run some risk for peaee? "Why cannot Sir EDWARD Cansos say boldly, Give me the amend- ments I ask for to safeguard the dignity and interests of Protestant Ulster, and in return I will use all my influence and goodwill to make Ireland an integral unit in a Federal system' ? " Concili- atory • speeches followed from Mr. BALFOUR .and Sir EDWARD CARSON, and</p>
        <p>the latest forecasts of are based upon the offer conveyed in Mr. CHURCHILL'S speech. Mr. BONAR LAW made no attempt to substantiate his charges of falsehood against the PRIME MINISTER. He con- fined -himself, as the "Daily Chronicle" says, to "niggling points of apparent contradictoriness." After Mr. ASQUITH had refuted some of his charges, there were cries of " Withdraw " from the Liberals. "I don't care whether he withdraws or not," said the PRIME MINISTER, whose attitude towards the calumniators of the Government .was contemptuous, and to the charges against himself disdainful. The Gov- ernment had a majority of 80 in the division. Sir EDWARD CARSON, after accepting responsibility for the gun- running exploit, spoke with singular restraint: "I am as anxious," he said, "as any man living to find a way out." He expressed his willingness to pro- mote a settlement if Ulster is left out pending a Federal scheme; and .he showed plainly that he views favour- ably the proposal that all Ireland shall be one integral unit in the Federal Con- stitution. "I am, therefore," he said, "not very far away from the right hon. gentleman." Mr. BALFOUR spoke as if</p>
        <p>the Home Rule Bill was an accom- plished fact, and lamented the failure of his life's work. Finally the Govern- ment announced that they did not in- tend to follow up the gun-running by prpsecutions as they had provided for, the surest indication that conciliation, to be followed by settlement, is in the air.</p>
        <p>WOMEN TARIFF REFORMERS. Just a year ago we congratulated the Women's Tariff Reform League upon having assembled in annual meeting as though nothing had happened, when, as a matter of fact, Mr. BONAR LAW had dealt the final blow to the crazy demand for Tariff Reform. The annual meeting of the same "most powerful organisa- tion of its kind in the country" has been held once more this week, but it has been the Unionist side of their character that the members have dis- played. Twelve months ago we were told of the "inaccuracies of the present Radical Government and the disastrous effects of Free Trade in our country." Those disastrous effects resulted in the setting up of yet another record in the trade of the country. This time Tariff , Reform is not so much as mentioned— except as a title, of course. The story now told is about "the Government who, to keep themselves in office, and to save their salaries, are prepared to allow bloodshed to take place." It is really terrible—or would be if it were only half as true as the legends about the disastrous effects of Free Trade. One good purpose this annual meeting has served—it has given us some fig- ures that we have often wished to see. Last summer Mr. HArtoLD SMITH ap- pealed to the Women Tariff Reformers with hearts and no politics to send in their names to Mr. HERBERT HAYES indicating that they would be willing to find homes for refugee women and children from Ulster during the ap- proaching struggle. So far 434 pro- mises to find homes have been obtained and as the membership of this "greatest of all similar organisations"' is placed at tl3 there ar ,53e, thee a comforting inference number of er is that f very sensible women in the W.U.T.R.L. The men of the town signed the Covenant 6 78r strong, but , s rong, we shall have to forgive them, as they knew not ' What they did. One who had been a Liberal" all his life" said: "He would rather shoot down the members of this Government than cast a vote in their favour." The ease with which our friends unearth real Liberals is truly remarkable. . ,</p>
        <p>. _„..</p>
