      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 2, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 2, 1914.</p>
        <p>TEE DEGRADED OPPOSITION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Crosfiehl aid tie Cootelptible losoletce of Mr. hoar Lan.</p>
        <p>OUR "CONSTITUTIONAL" PARTY</p>
        <p>That Faseats gebellici aid tell Cries "?It."</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—' am very glad indeed to fife "The L'xaminer" of Saturday quoting a letter from Colonel Sir Frederick Cardew, sent to the "Morning Post," in which he writes : " an old soldier I would say this, that if Sir Arthur Paget, the General Officer Com- manding the Troops in Ireland, had not made certain dispositions for the protection of military armaments and stores which were exposed to attask, and taken military precautions for the movement of troops in the event of an outbreak on the part of the Ulster Volunteers, he would not have been fit for his position." Of course General Paget was absolutely justi- fied is all the precautioni he took, as he would have bees amply justified in recommending the Government to take still further precautions had he shosen to do so. I'erhaps I may take this opportunity of adding that General Sir Arthur Paget is one of those gallant soldiers who have refused to join in the has and cry against the Ministry and Progressive Politicians of every shade and deasription, in which unfor- tunately in some fashionable military circles it is the custom to indulge. Warrington Liberals and Progressives will not the less appreciate on that account his conduct in circumstances of exceptional and peculiar diffieulty. The difficulty and the tension created by the extravagant proceedings of Ulster Orangemen will not be lessened by this last event of which we have news, the importation of rifles and ammunition of German manufacture. (Will Warrington Tariff Reformers please note that fact as an amusing com- mentary on what they prel‘,ll and what they practise?) What an outrageous piece sf audacity this deliberately threatened high treason ! ! !</p>
        <p>For many a generation past it has been easy to fan the flames of bigotry amounting to fanaticism which is prevalent in that North- east corner of Ireland. Not the as serious on that account is the responsibility of those who, in their reckless partisanship, are seeking to exploit it. What we must 'lo'ls:antif tear in mind is the fact that chief r.nong the ring- leaders who have organised this clot agaiast the Parliament Act, and against erdsrly Demo- cratic Rule itself, are Sir Edward Carson and Mr. Bonar Law. While at the very time they are engaged on their nefarious conspiracy they have the hypocriti- cal insolence to talk, forsooth, of the Govern- ment's "plot against Ulster." One moment they and their satellites of the yellow Press corn- plain that the Government do not take the Ulster opposition seriously enough; the next, when measures of the most common-sense pre- caution are taken, and taken in the least pro- vocative manner, they raise this faked cry about- a "Plot to bully Ulster." I am glad to see in the same article in " The Examiner" from which I have already quoted, attention is drawn to the language used in the House of Commons by Mr. Law, where a man of his stamp and breeding has the unmannerly and contemptible insolence to charge an eminent statesman like the Prime Minister, to his face, with making false statements. We have seen during the struggle in which the "Die-hards" fought for the defeat of the People's Budget, that those who belonged to the party which once claimed the title of the "Constitutional party" were the first when it suited them to pitbh the Constitution to the winds. We have seen the same people, belong- ing to the same party, which, if it stood for any- thing at all, claimed to stand for Law and Order, instigating and encouraging misguided men on towards open, insurrection and strife; and now we have the leader of the party, which also used to claim that it included the " Gentle- men " of England, trying to degrade the Mother of Parliaments to the level of a pot-house. It is not only Liberals who are indignant at the insolence of this ill-bred "leader," who, unfortunately for his party and his country, has been thrust into a position for which he was never qualified; there are no end of reputable Conservatives who shudder at his methods and his ways, and are filled with dismay at the thought of their party being led by such a man. Shades of Salisbury and Sir Stafford, of Devon- shire and Goschen! If they still haunt the scenes of their mundane activity, what must these men of birth and breeding, these former pillars of the Constitution, and Law and Order think of such sayings and doings as are wit- nessed in these days?</p>
        <p>If we seek an explanation of all these impos- sible proceedings I think we have it summed up for us in a remark made last Friday night at Banbury by the Attorney-General. Sir John Simon then said :</p>
        <p>"It was the fact that the Liberals having won three General Elections there had been no swing of the pendulum towards the Conservatives, and as the pendulum would not swing they were determined to smash the clock."</p>
        <p>Yes, they are trying to "smash the clock," and a sufficiently evil example it is for men of wealth and leisure and education and high posi- tion to set others of their less fortunate fellow- countrymen. But their nefarious schemes will be frustrated. Not without infinite pains and labour, and long years and generations of tra- vail, and of sacrifice has that ancient piece of mechanism been constructed, and by no manner of means will the electors of Britain permit a set of worthless iconoclastic political ne'er-do- weels to smash it to pieces.—Yours, &amp;c., ARTHUR CROSPIELH.</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>t' – -t*-14S IN 4 c *</head>
        <p>t' - -t*-14S IN 4 c *</p>
        <p>QUEEN _ALEXANDRA DAY.</p>
        <p>'As willbe seen by an article on our Woman's Page, Alexandra Day celebration this year is fixed for Wednesday, June 24th. The move- ment is growing, and while in London rose- sellers will number 20,000, against 15,000 litst year, the sub-committees in the Provinces *ill number 300, as against 72 last year. It will be remembered that last year Warrington joined in the movement, and that the young ladies who took possession of the streets on a memor- able 'Saturday in June, Succeeded in making all Warrington wear at least one artificial rose before they went to headquarters to reckon up the proceeds. No less a sum than £491 us. Id. was taken of which 270 15s. went to the local</p>
        <p>_ Infirmary, £7O 6s. 3d. to the Nursing Associa- tion, and fire guineas each to the Children's Camp and Cripples' Guild, It seems strange, the local effort having been so successful and the general movement showing such decided vitality that in Warrington there is this year no Queen Alexandra Day. There is a valid reason, however. The Million Penny Fund Committee are to make another great effort on behalf of the Infirmary by means of an Old English Fair in Victoria Park, and the resources of the town are to be concentrated upon that effort.</p>
        <p>GARDEN PARTIES FOR FACTORY WORKERS. I In the same suggestive article reference is made to the good that could be done by those who have country houses and grounds placing their grounds occasionally at the disposal of the workers of the tovtrns. We have witnessed an increased desire to do this kind of thing locally on a limited scale. The invitations have usually taken the form of treats to people inter- ested in specified subjects. It is a wider sym- pathy with the peoples of the cities for which the Bishop of London has appealed to those who own acres in trust for a higher purpose than their own personal gratification.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>GRAMMAR SCHOOL IMPASSE.</head>
        <p>THE GRAMMAR SCHOOL IMPASSE.</p>
        <p>The difference between the Education Committee and the GovernerS of the Grammar School still proceeds. A short discussion at the meeting of the former authority on Monday evening indi- cated that little, if any, effort has been made to bring about an understanding. On the one hand the Governors are proceeding with their preliminaries for the erection of a new school, ignoring the claims of the Committee to be con- sidered in the matter; on the other hand the Committee are not in the least inclined to be trampled upon. So. far as the facts have been made public, they clearly reveal that the Education Committee have a very strong case, and that they may be trusted to vindicate that case to the utmost. As at present constituted, the Grammar School, like most other educa- tional institutions, cannot make ends meet from private sources. Besides receiving Government grants, the Governors were forced to seek and accept from the Education Committee a grant out of the Warrington rates not exceeding £4OO a year, as a guarantee against possible defi- ciencies. In such circumstances the Committee claim a voice in the doings of the Governors, and when the latter proposed the building of a new school, the Committee asked for a confer- ence, at which the higher education needs of the town could be considered as a whole. The Governors were fractious and that conference has never been beld.</p>
        <p>_ Recently the Education Committee received a communication from the Board of Educatien informing them that the Gonrvcrs had laid their proposals before them. 'rhe authorities in London appear to think that the focal authori- ties are due some consideration, for they wrote asking to be "informed whether it is the intention of the local Education Authority to continue' their present guarantee of grant." London has also an eye upon Ihe gen:iv:ll needs of the town, for the communicadon also sug- gested a conference between the Board and the Committee "upon the general question of suit- able and sufficient provision of higher educa- tion in the borough." The School Management Sub-committee, who had dealt with this corre- spondence, instructed the Director of Education to inform the Board of- Education that they were not prepared to consider the r atter until the "Governors' proposals, now in possession of the Board, were received." Mr. C. Sawkins Burgess, representing the Governors, objected to this decision on Monday, ' said it was calculated to delay the plans of the Governors, proceeded to give a rough-and-ready, explanation of what they intended to do, and claimed that the meeting should then and there say whether they would continue the grant. It was magnificent, but it was scarcely business. At all events, the Committee were not open to be hustled in that kind of way. Mr. Hy. Roberts intimated that the proposals must be submitted officially to the Committee for careful consider- ation. Till they are so submitted, the Education Committee will sit tight. lithe Governors want their new school and all their own way they can have both by paying, but if public money is to be asked for the public representatives must be consulted. NVe think the Committee are as firmly resolved upon that point as the Governors appear bent upon getting their own way.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH SWING BRIDGE..</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH SWING BRIDGE..</p>
        <p>The Stockton Heath Parochial Committee hav- ing asked the Ship Canal Company to provide a light in the centre of tne Swing Bridge to make it safe when it is in a position as part of the Kin's highway, the Company have replied that when the bridge is closed to admit the King's passage of a vessel the workmen on the bridge can manage to see their way about by means of a hand lamp. It is a soft answer, but it is hardly the kind of softness that tuineth away wrath. As a matter of fact, the reply was described at the Parochial Committee meeting as a " get-out " and as " tommy-rot." They have a gift for calling a spade a spade at Stockton Heath., It is admittedly difficult to place a per- manent light on a constantly moving roadway, and lights may be confusing to navigation, but the resources of the Company and the states- manship of the rulers of Stockton Heath ought to be equal to dealing effectively with the alleged danger.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>BOY FOOTBALLERS.</head>
        <p>BOY FOOTBALLERS.</p>
        <p>After the several fine displays of clean, scien- tific football that the juveniles have provided</p>
        <p>at Wilderspool, it is not surprising to learn that last Saturday the attendance at the final for the South-West Lancashire Schoolboys' Competition Cup exceeded that at the final for the League Competition. A good game was witnessed—the best seen on the ground during the season was the enthusiastic verdict of many who saw it. The Warrington Club are acting wisely in thus casting their bread upon the waters. The youngsters of to-day will be the town's stalwarts in the years to come, and the suggestion made by Councillor Osborne to form an Intermediate League is one that will meet with the approval and hearty support of all who have the interests of the game at heart. It will make an effective connecting link between the schools and the senior club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>SUNDAY GOLF.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY GOLF.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Golf Club have, by a large majority, decided to permit the game to be played on Sunday. Before this decision was arrived at there was a keen but useless struggle to maintain the status quo, for several reasons, and those members who favour the orthodox view taken of the day of rest will have to hope for the best results from the promises that there shall be no Sunday labour and no Sunday "Bar." The club is in a flourishing condition, and it has one of the loveliest courses in the country.</p>
        <p>LAWN MOWERS from 15/-, GARDEN ROLLERS from 20/-. Lawn Mower Repairs a Speciality.—J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, 51, Bridge-street, War- rington.</p>
        <p>.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>PARR HALL ORGAN.</head>
        <p>PARR HALL ORGAN.</p>
        <p>It cannot be said that the Parr Hall organ fund is making the rapid strides that one would expect. According to the latest list of subscrip- tions, which appears in "The Examiner" to- day, there has been promised £7OB 7s. 6d., leav- ing £9OO still to be raised. That is a goodly sum, but it ought to be within the reach of the committee in a rich community like ours. Have the Corporation considered the advisability of making a contribution? Perhaps they will do so when the fund has got a little nearer the goal. In the meantime those to whom the thanks of</p>
        <p>the town are due for leading the .way in this really important matter are flis Worship the Mayor, who has given £25 ; Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Crosfield, Mrs. Charles E. Parker, Mr. J. Charl- ton Parr, and Miss, M. U. Bylands, £lOO each; Mr. Ziba Armitage, Mr. J. J. Crosfleld, and Capt. G. R. Crosfleld, £5O each; Dr. G. W. Joseph, Councillor Henry Roberts and the Misses White, £25 each; Mr. Boger C. parr, £2O- Caldwells, 1 Ltd., the Stockton Heath Forge Co., £lO 10s. r ' Mr. B. T. Fairclough, £10; Col. Charles Lyon Hobert Bowman and Mr. H. Percy Densham, £2 2s. each; Mr. B. H. Meldrum and Mr. James and the Bev. Canon Penrhyn, .£5 each; Mr. Newall, .£1 is. each; 11.4 r. Louis Henshall, Mr. Wm. H. Hughes (Orford-lane), and Dr. J. D. Liston, halt a guintia each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>From Everywhere.</head>
        <p>From Everywhere.</p>
        <p>Always do your best. but o:wrer your boot friend.</p>
        <p>There ere just as good &amp;eh storie• as have ewer been eaught.</p>
        <p>The bachelcr who sympathises with fair widow is lost. s</p>
        <p>The one particular brand of love that isn't blind is self-love.</p>
        <p>The greatest drawbaek.to a long life is that it necessitates old age.</p>
        <p>Luck doesn't amount to much unless it is mixed with a little brains.</p>
        <p>A successful man is One who can make more money than his wife can spend.</p>
        <p>A man seldom knows who his friends are, bir his enemies are more considerate.</p>
        <p>Many a man who is willing to be a scoundrel would object to being called one.</p>
        <p>To say a man is happy unwarranted as to say a man is healthy because he has enough to eat. because he is rich is as</p>
        <p>There is only one way in which to attain originality now, a very laborious and difficult lino to take; it is to be perfectly natural.</p>
        <p>Against Woman Suffrage. Our main objection to woman suffrage is that when the mistress and the cook both have the ballot there will be nobody to get dinner on election day.</p>
        <p>The Reason. She: "How is it that the Yearwedds do not speak to you now He: "I'm the man who introduced them to each other."</p>
        <p>A Coming Star. "The teacher informs me that Mary Anderson has considerable dramatic talent." " Why, that girl can't recite the multiplica- tion .table without making the most elegant gestures."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <p>Getting on the Safe Side. "So you have sold your pair of horses and bought an automobile?" " Yes," replied Miss Cayenne. "I have con- cluded that it is better to be the cause of a runaway than to be in it."</p>
        <p>Method In His Madness. Mrs. Slips: "If I should die to-morrow would you be much upset?" Hubby: "I should nearly go mad." "Would you marry again?". "No. I should not be quite so mad as that."</p>
        <p>Blarney. Trishisms are frequently heard in our local Police Court, and this week a prisoner came y leave it? out with the sentence, "I had only that pint (of beer), and I had not supped it." The Clerk: Why did you Prisoner: This gentleman (fellow prisoner) kept. agate blarneying with me.</p>
        <p>Women's Rights. In the local Court this week an irate defend- ant asked a witness with all sincerity, "Did you not lend Mrs. a pair of boxing gloves to come and box me?" Women are skilled in the use of a variety of weapons—pokers, &amp;C. but if they develop the pugilistic art "late working" husbands beware!</p>
        <p>--- Why They Don't Tire Of It Mrs. Littletown : "This magazine looks rather the worse for wear." Mrs. Neartown : "Yes, it's the one I same- times lend to the servant on Sundays." Mrs. Littletown : "Doesn't she get tired of al- ways reading the same one?" Mrs. Neartown: " Oh, no. You see, it's the same book, but it's always a different servant."</p>
        <p>Wasn't Looking for Furniture., "Now this," said the agent, calling Billups' attention to a handsome car in the corner, " is a dandy. It runs so smoothly you wouldn't know you were in it. Rides just like a rocking-chair." "What do you think I am, an escaped inmate of an old lady's home?" demanded Billups. "I want a car that I'll know I'm in when I'm in it, and when -I go out looking for a rocking chair I'll go to a furniture store and not to a garage."</p>
        <p>One to the Scotsman. At the battle of Trafalgar, two Scotsmen, messmates and bosom cronies from the same clachan, happened to be stationed near each other, when the celebrated intimation was dis- played from the admiral's ship. "Look up and read, Jock," said the one to the other—"` Eng land expects every man to do his duty'—not a - word for puir auld Scotland." Jock cocked his eye at the object, and turtling th his companion thus addressed him: "Man Geodie, is that a' your sense? Scotland kens well enough that her bairns will do their duty—that's just a hint to the Englishmen."</p>
        <p>Left Him to His Fate. The business man was sitting in his office when a suspicious-looking person came in with a leather bag in his hand. "If you don't give me £5," said the visitor, coming at once to the point, "I will drop this on the floor." The business man was.cool. "What's in it?" he asked. " Dynamite," was the brief reply. "What will it do if you drep it?" "Blow you up i" "Drop it!" was the instant command. "My wife told me when I left home this morning to be sure and order a bag of flour for the mince pies and I've forgotten it. I reckon it will take just about as much dynamite as you have there to prepare me for the 'blowing up' I shall get when she sees me." He threw himself back in his chair and waited for the explosion, but it did not come." "I'm a married man myself," said the dyna- miter, and quietly slipped out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>'N-DAY</head>
        <p>'N-DAY</p>
        <p>Warrington Club Adopt It After Keen Opposition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>DECISIVE VOTE.</head>
        <p>A DECISIVE VOTE.</p>
        <p>Councillor Roberts and the Accentua- tion of Class Privileges.</p>
        <p>Thure waw an unueually large attendance at the annual meeting of the memberw of the War- rington Golf Club, held at the Patten Arms on Thurwday night, cauced probably by a notice of motion to abandon the rule which haw hitherto made Sunday play impowwible. We regret that the Prewo were not admitted to the meeting. The quewtion involved waw of importance to the public ac well ac to the mem- ber., and a full report, pro and con, would have beefl meet valuable.</p>
        <p>It was' moved: " That the rule which forbids Sunday play be deleted."</p>
        <p>Tha diacueaion was conducted in excelleat temper, although it was eTident that the oppo- wition felt yery keenly the undesirability of the propoaed change.</p>
        <p>_ The arguments against the proposal were put with considerable emphasis. The Vicar of Daresbury opposed the motion in a epoech of much eloquence, and instanced the case of a London Club in which Sunday play had been authorised on the distinct under- standing that caddies were not employed, that the bar was not opened, and that no Sunday labour should be permitted. In a few short months all the prohibitions were abolished, and now the bar was open all day, caddies were employed, and there was more playing on Sun- day than any other days in the week. Mr. Henry Roberts appealed to the members to retain the status quo, because the club was originally formed with the distinct understand- ing that Sunday play would not be allowed. If the members departed from that couise they would be accentuating the di:Terence in the privileges possessed by themselves and their less fortunate fellows. In the present times of great labour unrest each a connive was very undesir- able. In any event, Sunday play could not be indulged in, except at the cost of additional Sunda j labour. Another opponent pointed out that the sup- porters of Sunday golf were pressing for privi- leges they had not previously possessed at the expense of those who had joined the club on the understanding that Sunday play was not per- mitted. The motion found very little vocal support; one opponent stated that the majority wanted the motion carried, but would prefer to say as little about it as possible. This view was con- firmed by the decision to take the vote by ballot —a condition not provided for by the rules— and the vote resulted:—</p>
        <p>For Sunday Golf Against „ ..</p>
        <p>Majority for The result was probably secured by the pro- mise made by some members of the committee that they would not permit caddies, that there should be no Sunday labour, bar, or play until noon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>FORMAL BUSINESS.</head>
        <p>THE FORMAL BUSINESS.</p>
        <p>The retiring captain, Mr. W. Greenhouse, pro- posed the adoption of the balance sheet, which was seconded by Mr. Hepherd and carried un- animously. The club has had a very successful year, having wiped out a very large deficit, leaving a total deficit on the year's working of .47 16s. Mr. J. Hepherd was elected captain for the ensuing year.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ilepherd, in a sporting speech, proposed that the club should have a dinner on June 13th, when the captain's prize should' be presented. _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. J. E. Birtles, Who has held the office for so mar4y years, was again elected honorary secretary. Mr. H. Grosvenor, who had presented such a splendid balance sheet, was again elected hon. treasurer.</p>
        <p>The following constitute the committee for the ensuing year: Dr. W. J. Peacocke, Captain B. Evans, Messrs. W. Greenhouse, C. H. Unsworth, E. A. White, F. Pendlebury, 11. G. Roberts, A. Bottoraley, and F. Warburton. A rule relating to the entrance fee was sub- mitted to the meeting, which said that the entrance fee should be £1 Is. up to 200 mem- bers, ..42,2 2s. up to 250 members, and the annual subscription remain the same at £3 38. The " Fairclough " Memorial Competition was arranged to be played in May, June, July and August, the final to be 36 holes and the winner to hold for one year the large silver challenge bowl, having his name inscribed' on the oak panel, the cost of which was defrayed by the members of the club. We understand that the club is in a sound financial condition, and that the owner, Colonel Lyon, intends to build a new club house. The old one, which is quite inadequate for the needs of its members, will be used as a residence for one of the groundsmen. Before long, therefore, the Warrington Golf Club will find themselves in palatial quarters. Nobody will dispute that the ground is in lone of the prettiest parts of Cheshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>DISASTROUS FlRE—contlnued.</head>
        <p>DISASTROUS FlRE—contlnued.</p>
        <p>motor pump and several branches of hose, and as the seriousness of the position was realised, the steamer was hurried on to aid the motor, and in a few minutes many jets were playing on the fiery furnace.</p>
        <p>IN GRAVE DANGER. There was very grave danger of the fire be- coming a very great one. Alongside was the motor car shop, with its petrol, behind was a plumber's shop, and adjoining the latter was a stable with horses, owned by Mr. liallwood. The animals, alarmed and excited, were rescued, and ere this was done the flames were sweeping townwards in a dense column from every window.</p>
        <p>It was evident that the building of West's could not be saved, and the first duty of the firefighters was to concentrate on saving adjoin- ing property. At one time it was feared that even the Old Academy might become involved, but as soon as the water began to play in full force it was seen that this danger was removed.</p>
        <p>FRONT AND REAR. t The brigade were soon attacking the outbreak in the front and rear, and the fire, thus hemmed in, was kept to Mr. West's premises. It burned furiously, however, for well over an hour, and then the water began to tell. In the interval the tram services were com- pletely disorganised, and a temporary arrange- ment was made whereby the services to Latch- ford and Stockton Heath were started from the other side of the Bridge. The outbreak attracted a large crowd, especially after the theatres emptied.</p>
        <p>The firemen were somewhat hampered by motorists persisting in driving through, and several cars nearly drove over the hose. Late last night "The Examiner" representa- tive was unofficially informed that the damage might be about ..t2,000, and it was further added that the damage was partially covered by in- surance.</p>
        <p>It was feared early this morning that several of Mr. West's horses had been destroyed by the fire.</p>
        <p>SPRING CLEANING.-Try the "DAISY" Vacuum Cleaner, 42/-, 63/-, 84/-. Free trial allowed.— J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, 51 to 55, Bridge- street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
