        <p>friend the King-cup. The Tuberous Mosel might easily be passed without notice, ar.cl few except the trained eye of the botanisl aware of its abundane• ix ths woodlands 13 district. Structurally it is one of the lrel of our woodland plants, but it has A 4 lbeauty all its own. The tymna,try and err meat of the five small yellawish green lc into a tiny ball at tie top of a Torl stem, surrounded by a rims of graceful Je is worthy of closer Andy. Thera e.r. flowers of a quiet, retiring nature who unseen in the shade of the weed-lama glade° none so pretty as the lionchatel.</p>
        <p>Feast of Changing Cilium The beauty of colour is lot confoseql ground; around and overhead the tree. rk</p>
        <p>full quota. Not till Julia will they hare maturity of foliage, in the meantime th 4 play a continual change of colour. It 1.6 iquality of change which makes the woodl3lj attractive during May. There is infinite 11 —not even in two specimens of the same 91 is there any constancy of colour. Of too i trees, one will have its young, developing, a pale yellowish green, the other deer, darker. In two Sycamores one has COOP' bud scales of a deep red; the other a 111 green; in another two the long, young stalks will be nearly yellow, and in the red. One Oak has leaves of a brownish</p>
        <p>_ another a bright emerald. In the conif° dark leaves of the Scotch Fir or the V' Pine acts as a suitable background to ell° young, light green tassles of the Larch t fection. Hardly any two greens in the, wood will be quite of the same tint. the satisfying glory of it. To sit on a morning and watch the soft west wind 1)' the ever-changing lights and shadows the wood, and to reap the harvest of ill' btant play of colour, is to fill one's soul quiet content.</p>
        <p>Flowers of the Forest. Let us have a closer look at the treeri, f°l,l have a wonderful story to tell. It is that so many people are quito ignorao</p>
        <p>fact that the trees of our forests</p>
        <p>The Laburnum, with its dangling r/ flowers, and the Horse Chestnut, with It'l nificent waxen pyramids, advertise their be:lf but many of our trees, which are known by their fruits than their blossoms, liav00„1 esting if less obtrusive flowers. The 1:"I on many of the trees are out before the le, The Hazel is the earliest of them all. ea'a even than the Goat Willow, which supPliej children with " palms " at Easter. Thc dangling clusters of male flowers or catlsos :conspicuous objects in hedge and we0d...4 before the rest of the woodland has wakell`, I for its Spring duties. The female fioNver,„ its deep crimson. star, blooms lower door' I branch. The Alder is another catkin beai but never an attractive, tree when in 1-031.1.0 catkins are longer than in the Hazel, ds7i colour, the female flowers are clusters °I green cones. They are easily noticed es, on the same branch, acting as a foil, dead black cones of last year. The fOl„li Poplars, found occasionally on the edge wood, also bear catkins, longer and with a ti of red in their colour scheme. The MeV par excellence, are those of our 'favourlt°o the Oak and the Birch, when they apPe3,6 their best. In the Oak the male and flowers are on the same tree, but are ye ri/ ferent in structure and appearance. _ Tliej</p>
        <p>flowers are in long, slender, flexible c6l without any protecting scales; the fenlaleo ers are solitary, surrounded by a etii`e involuore, the same which matures into of the future acorn. The flowers appeo.eo before or with the leaves, according to CI stances.</p>
        <p>The Reason. 49 What is the reason of the appearance ei vt flowers before the foliage? The flower etf field, the hedgerow, and the wayside do thus. Most of the trees are fertilised v",c,l agency of the wind; winds, too, which er-obarj: veniently soft and gentle in their action 0 the month of May. If the fertilising pollel, developed after the expansion of the ,4 the chances would be that the leaves V intercept much of the pollen intended fe'l female flowers, and reduce their fertilit'Prip/ pollen in wind-fertilised trees is viOndeoft° prolific. A walk near a wood on a warniA noon when the catkins ate ripe often head and shoulders perceptibly ecloul'e'l4,o the yellow pollen dust from the ttees slight tap with a walking-stick on a h9r,e,00`.; will reveal how full of pollen the catlo;l.,, It is evident that much of this golden.;6. borne away by the wind without fulfin/ functions. But the abundance is all ti,leeo necessary on that account. Amidst t14,1014t0 lug prodigality there is a wise reason- tad no real waste. For every exigency provided for. Perverse winds which pollen to the open away from the tree be taken into account in the cstinlole; itibe Nature appears to be wasteful at only part of her scheme to make sere continuance of the speciesd. On the borders of the woods 100-e,,,er;il9 white blossoms is another tribe of , Blackthorn, Bullace, Bird Cherry's Cherry—all members of the family B")'-cia ve present they make a brave show and 0.00 tit note to the glory of the wood. By the tiniot'uftif Hawthorn adds her annual qrrty of c,-,clth'eir the landscape all these will have scatic'a petalous confetti o'er the grounds 50 per, grandeur gone for the year. We must not forget in the galasii for, vernal survey the chief glory of ailetgrV, moment. Probably no plant of the 11°' tio gel appeals so strikingly to one and all 05 pee'bticr Hyacinth. The sight of the extensicead blue is a sight quite unequalled hi ss, nv'4l.lll. flower of our flora. G. A.</p>
      </div>
