        <p>Assistant Weights Inspector. Mr. William Healey, of Warrington, has been appointed asaistant weights and measures inspector under the Warrington Corporation.</p>
        <p>Lorry Accident. George ,ikshley (54), Town Pit, Whitley, lies in a serious condition at the Warrington In- firmary entering from injuries resulting from a lorry accident.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>False Fire Alarms.</head>
        <p>False Fire Alarms.</p>
        <p>- - To test the fire brigitde, false fire alarms are to be given during May by the members of the Fire Brigade Committee. The Mayor is to give the firwt call.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>Councillor Tay/or Doing</head>
        <p>Councillor Tay/or Doing</p>
        <p>We are glad to be able to state that Councillor sylor, whi kas xxiergoir* an operation, in- volving the amputation of one of his feet, was last night progressing favourably.</p>
        <p>More Loans Wanted..</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee recommend the Town Council to apply to the Local Government Board for remotion to borrow £14,878 18s. d. for the Buttermarket-street improvements, and £1,410 Sr. 4d. for other street improvements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Town Hall Extension.</head>
        <p>Town Hall Extension.</p>
        <p>, The proposal for the provision of new muni- cipal offices at the Town Hall, at a cost of ..t50,001, was discussed by the General Purposes Committee on Monday, and the matter was ad- journed until the next meeting.</p>
        <p>Sale of Property. Mr. Frank Hickman will sell on the 13th Ility property in Hultue-street. of Marsh Honse-lane, and in Steel-street, Gorse},--lans. The sale takes place at the Blue Bell Hotel, at 5.30 p.m.</p>
        <p>Deputy Mayor's Gift. The Deputy-Mayor, Dr. Joseph, has presented framed engravings of the Ling and Queen, to be hung in the Mayor's parlour to commemo- rate their visit last year. Ho also gate a silver loving cup.</p>
        <p>Grammar School Captain. Summer term commenced at the Boteler Grammar School yesterday with a good attend- ance. At a meeting of the Games Committee, held in the course of the morning, A. Ferguson was elected captain, with C. W. Healey sub- captain. L. J. Witte again acts as secretary.</p>
        <p>The Market Hours. The Markets Committee of the Corporation have received letters from the Tradesmen's Association and the Shop Assistants' Union ask- ing that the Market be closed at 8 o'clock on Wednesday and 14.30 p.m. on Saturday. The committee adjourned the matter for a month.</p>
        <p>The Cemetery. The annual report on the Warrington Ceme- tery shows that the receipts for the year ending at 31st March were £1,360 25., compared with ..t1,295 3s. ld. for the previous year. There were interred 1,120 adults and minors, and 121 still- born children, compared with 1,088 and 112 re- spectively in the previous year.</p>
        <p>Salvation Army and the Blind. Next week's issue of the "War Cry," the offi- cial organ of the Salvation Army, is to be de- voted to a summary of the Army's work among the blind of all nations. Also on Sunday. May 10th, by order of General Booth, a special offer- ing, in aid of the National Institution for the Blind, is to be taken up at every corps in the United Kingdom.</p>
        <p>Mr. IL Smith and Licensing. Speaking at Manchester yesterday afternoon to licence-holders about the proposed Temper- ance Bill, Ur. Harold Smith said this Bill was an unnecessary and undemocratic Bill, which made no real attempt to solve the problem of drunkenness. Such a Bill would only encourage illicit drinking.</p>
        <p>Record Rate Paying. The Borough Collector reports that £194,095 was collected in rates during the past year. The loss incurred was only .Cl 7 13s. The rates were collected with a minimum of Court cases. The Collector adds: "I do not think a record like this, covering as it does so many rates, is ex- celled in any place in the kingdom, and it speaks well for the ratepayers of Warrington and the state of the trade."</p>
        <p>Tramwaymen's Wages. To make up for the loss sustained in wages by the tramway employes through the curtail- ment of the tram services on Sunday mornings, the Tramway Committee offered an increase of id. per hour. This was not acceptable to the Council at the last meeting, and the matter was sent back. The committee, after re-considera- tion, still recommend that the increase be id. per hour, and the proposal is likely to cause considerable discussion in the Council on Tues- day.</p>
        <p>Guardians' Officials. The following chairmen and vice-chairmen were elected on the different committees of the Warrington Union at a meeting yesterday: Assessment Committee, Mr. W. Furness (chair- man), Mr. G. Mather (vice-chairman); House Visiting Committee, Mr. W. C. Brooks (chair- man), Mr. W. Flanagan (vice-chairman); Finance Committee, Mr. A. J. Jolley (chairman), Mr. Furness (vice-chairman); Building Com- mittee, Mr. E. Mather (chairman), Mr. Brooks (vice-chairman).</p>
        <p>Church and Stage. The Warrington Battalion of the C.L.B. were opposed to Mr. Fred Kitchen's Hippodrome team in a friendly game of football on Satur- day, and this resulted in a draw, each side scor- ing four times. In the evening the Brigade team were the guests of Mr. Kitchen at the first house at the Hippodrome. The C.L.B. team was composed of Staff-Sergt. Howells; Sergt. Mannering and Lieut. Bottomley ; Sergt. Monks, Capt. Elenshall and Lance-Corpl. Allen; Sergt. Dayus, Sergt. Haslam, Corpl. Ashton, Sergt.- Major Goodwinn and Corpl. Appleton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Leather Trade.</head>
        <p>The Leather Trade.</p>
        <p>The trade during last week, says the "Leather Trades Review," was sensibly strengthening again, inquiry was steadily growing in import- ance, sales were numerous though of moderate size, and calls for contract goods were persistent. A very little more life in the demand would jus- tify things being described as brisk, but matters are not quite sq far advanced as to tempt the tanners to expand their production; conse- quently, most of them are still working in hides steadily, but well within maximum input. A feature of the trade is that there is considerable more business offering than is taken up; a large amount is floating about, and there is not much doubt that at the prices current a few weeks ago some large orders might be booked.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>Warrington. Athletic Club..</head>
        <p>Warrington. Athletic Club..</p>
        <p>There was a good muster of members on Saturday evening last at he Winmarleigh Cafe. Mr. H. Hope presided, and presented the prizes to the following: Club championship (decided on points in the West Lancashire, Northern and National Cross-Country Championships) : 1, W. Bolton, 28 points; 2, F. Antrobus, 23; 3, J. Han- son, 20. Senior points competition (in inter- club races, &amp;c.): 1, J. Hanson and E. Scott tied with 39 points each; 3, C. Vose, 34; J. Parsonage, 31, was next, closely followed by W. Bolton, H. Holbrook and F. Antrobus. Intermediate .points (confined to those not prize-winners in senior competition previously) : 1, E. Scott, 60, highest possible; 2, J. Hayes, 47; 3, A. Potts, 33. Novice ,oints : 1, A. Potts, 43; 2, A. Inman, 32; 3, H. Leach, 27. Novice medals: First race, J. Hayes; second race, F. Baker. Attendance medal: F. Price. The musical items, with Mr. Jack Greaves as accompanist, were well received, and the efforts of W. Clayton (baritone), in " Drinking" and patriotic songs; H. Harding, humorous songs; J. Ashworth, humorist and character songs; W. Clayton (tenor) and R. Bennett, duettists, neces- sitated repeated encores.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 2, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>NO LYNN MAY QUEEN.</head>
        <p>NO LYNN MAY QUEEN.</p>
        <p>LAST NIGHT'S MEETING ABANDONS</p>
        <p>THE FESTIVAL.</p>
        <p>I A meeting of the Lymm May Queen Festival Committee was held last night, when it was decided that the festival be abandoned.</p>
        <p>It was stated that the probable subscriptions and guarantees only amounted to £26, and aghinst this there was a debit balance of £l5 from last year. Mr. J. D. B. Smith, the chairman, attempted to have a resolution framed in favour of the festival continuing, but this not being forth- coming, he said it was with great reluctance that he was compelled to move; "That the festival be abandoned through lack of support from villagers and neighbours, and that the guarantors be asked for their guarantees."</p>
        <p>This was carried, and there wS.9 a feeling that it was a pity the festival should have to be dropped. _ _</p>
        <p>The, Chairman said that the ladies who had so kindly sought collections deserved sincere thanks.</p>
        <p>The subscriptions will be returned, and a further meeting will be held to wind up old matters.</p>
        <p>The lowest estimated cost of the festival was £7O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>IN AND OUT STRIKES.</head>
        <p>IN AND OUT STRIKES.</p>
        <p>More Trouble at Fletcher's and</p>
        <p>at Crosfield's.</p>
        <p>FLETCHER'S SETTLED LAST NIGHT.</p>
        <p>Last night Warrington was free of strikes, although in and out strikes have been the order of the week. Messrs. Fletcher, Russell's men came out on Monday, but a fresh settlement was arrived at last night. Crosfield's trouble was settled earlier in the week. At the invitation of Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co. yesterday afternoon, Messrs. Dukes, Hutchinson and Logan met Messrs. Russell, Rowson and Rudge, and the result was that they conveyed the following letter to the men, who held a mass meeting : " With reference to the interview, we agree to dispense with the services of all the men we have taken on since the beginning of the strike within one month from the date of resumption of work. We reserve the right, however, to re-engage several of the men, probably 12 or 14, if they should apply for work, under nor- mal conditions, if we can find work for them to do."</p>
        <p>The men unanimously decided to accept the terms and resume work, and 450 will start on Monday morning.</p>
        <p>Men's Case,</p>
        <p>Mr. Adam Bennett, under the name of the Joint Committee, sent a letter to the firm yes- terday week pointing out that general dis- satisfaction was being expressed at the method of treatment meted out to our first batch of men," and further that unless the terms of the agreement were carried out "we shall be com- pelled to ask our men to enforce its context." A mass meeting was held on Sunday, when the men agreed to remain idle until they had obtained "satisfactory assurances that the agreement would be loyally carried out." • Thus the strike began afresh. The men de- manded that the free labourers must be dis- charged, but this is a point which the firm were at present not disposed to grant, stating that those "who are suitable will be retained." The firm deny that it is their intention to give pre- ference to any of the new hands over the old employes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>CROSFIELD'S TROUBLE.</head>
        <p>CROSFIELD'S TROUBLE.</p>
        <p>Another Strike and Settlement.</p>
        <p>Since the exclusive announcement in the last issue of " The Examiner" of the settlement be- tween Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Co. and their wharf men, trouble has again arisen, but hap- pily once more it has been allayed. The trouble was originally duo to the fact that four of the wharf men, members of the Dockers' Union, refused to pay their union contributions, and the remainder of the men went out on strike. The dispute was settled the same day, when the men were promised that their long service al- lowances would not be prejudiced. On Monday morning the men again struck ' work because a non-unionist was sent to do checking duties on the wharf. Mr. J. Sexton, of Liverpool, and Mr. F. Hodson (local secretary) waited upon the firm on Tuesday afternoon, and at a mass meeting the same night a ballot was taken, when, by a majority, the men agreed to return to work on Wednesday. It is officially stated that the men having struck a second time without notice, now forfeit their long ser- vice allowances.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>Corporation Greens Open.</head>
        <p>Corporation Greens Open.</p>
        <p>The Corporation bowling greens were opened yesterday and were fairly well batronised, the innovation of goloshes being favourably com- mented upon by trundlers. They save the sward to a great extent.</p>
        <p>Queen! She has now in Warrington. It was t loud applause her reignY and and fitting ceremonial g live in the Parr Hall on aeThursdaythatnmLtioedr she nse sthenight. The large attendance of the public bore testimony to the fact that the event, the twelfth of its kind in the town, was as popu- lar as ever, for every available seat was occupied and every coign of vantage was taken up. This sight, and that of the large number of children, all in character, who are pledged to temperance, must have been gratifying to the officials and the committee of the Warrington Band of Hope Union, who work so zealously Y for the welfare of the calni id o ldrsen—detltieerniind nationel of to-morrow. Owing to the sad death of last year's "Queen" (Maud Kirkland) a complete change was neces- sitated in the " crowning" 'ceremony, for the usual proceedings associated with the retiring of the "Queen" had to be abandoned. The May Queen (Miss Eva Tanner) is a pro- mising young soloist, who for several years has taken part in the May Queen Festival. Her solo on Thursday evening was marked with great merit. GRAND PROCESSION.</p>
        <p>The grand procession which opened the pro- ceedings was very striking, the various groups of characters being so arranged as to meet in the centre of the stage, and by means of a wide improvised staircase to proceed down the centre of the hall. The fairies, beefeaters, castanet dancers, Maypole dancers, &amp;e., all slowly passed along. Characters from Dickens met, Pickwick and Sam Weller shaking hands, also passed along. It was a pretty sight; but the value of the striking colours of the costumes was more pronounced, when the children were dotted here and there upon the tiers at the rear of the stage, thus providing a background of gaily bedecked juveniles, fringed by the fairies with their glittering robes, wings and wands. The " Festival March," rendered by the full choir, was given with an expression often lack- ing in such choruses. The children took a pride in the event, and it was with a real sense of this pride that they sang: "Soon we shall see the stately procession,</p>
        <p>Soon will the May Queen come in view, Gay maidens glancing, oh! how entrancing! As we their path with flow'rets strew. Lit by the glow of torches burning; How for the sight we all are yearning !" , MR. PICKWICK ARRIVES. However, before the 'Queen" arrived, that jolly old soul, Mr. Pickwick, made his appear- ance, followed by Sam. They and the remain- der of the Dickensian characters were making a brief excursion from the covers of their books, and although the majority of the characters were overdrawn, they admirably served their purpose—to extract mirth. There was trouble amongst the ladies as to who was to be the "Queen," but as Sairey Gamp fell in a faint into Pickwick's arms the queen-elect arrived, and the full choir burst into a song of greet- ing, embodying all good wishes. Advances were made by Flora and the Fairy, and upon a favourable reply being given to their request the "Queen" was crowned and presented with a garland and sceptre, but ere she reached the thriMe she passed under an archway of the beefeaters' axes, amidst the cheers of her sub- jects. Sairey Gamp and her companions were soon chattering away, but tis was brought to ; a conclusion by the " Royal" command.</p>
        <p>MAYORESS'S PRESENTATION. The MAYORESS (Mrs. Peacock) made a. presen- tation of a beautiful bouquet to the "Queen," and this kindly .act was warmly acknowledged. The Queen," now upon the throne, was enter- tained right royally by song and dance. Master Daniel. Bradbury took the solo in the song "What a relief it. is," and was responsible for much laughter. The ris'(ileene3.layliteCdaasntacenewt Was dancers re- markably p came tripping merrily on with their click! click! click! and after several evolutions, which were followed by their seizing of long streamers of coloured paper attached to the tlop of the pole, the bolstering young Maypole dancers ran riot, singing the while underneath the coloured festoons, These Children were well trained, for it was without the least difficulty that they per- formed the seemingly perplexing movements. The exhibition of skipping by children trained by Miss Spinks was responsible for a hearty cheer from the audience—and it wag worth it.</p>
        <p>FLOWER QUEEN'S SONG. The toy balloon," an action song, was given by Plower Queen, was very sweet, a remark which also girls, applied "Fairies to otil et he Spring."action-song by yThese happy the junior little children were very painstaking in their endeavours, gliding here and tripping there, making everyone conscious of the fact that they were fairies, because did they not carry their glittering wands? The stiff and spontaneous actions of the per- formers in the doll dance made the item very realistic. These children were successful, and as such were rewarded with applause. "Sun, moon and stars," a temperance chorus rendered by the full choir, was a pleaging in- stance of the harmony. They were temperance children, and it seemed that the strength of all their teachings and warnings was embodied in the song, and more especially as they, with deter- mination, exclaimed : "Sun! Sun! 0 beautiful Sun!</p>
        <p>Would that drink had a record like thine! But, alas, it brings sadness, and dims all life's gladness, •</p>
        <p>For naught comes but sorrow from wine. If the singing of the song "Merry maidens</p>
        <p>we" by several small girls was rather weak, compensation was found in the quality of the dancing, for there is no mistaking the fact that in their footwork they did remarkably well.</p>
        <p>_ A selected outtned choir, cl•l - voice"hore anddered i the (pbart,Tsonhg (a) R e sweetest hour," probably were the favourites of the evening. The solo part in "The sweetest hour" was taken by Master Jim Bate, who scored a grand success. It appeared that the audience were determined to hear him again, despite the official note on the programme set- ting forth "that the committee regret it will be impossible to respond to encores."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <p>"MI. PIOKWICK I, ON THE SCENE.</p>
        <p>-' ... 1 I I I, ' II A., • . A ' 'O7/13, '4// ' ' .: . 91. . ' . . .•, 4; • •.'-' 'A ~ •-, 4., '441444 10 i - • -',;,, ~ „ '4 . _ . &lt; ''-''' I , • • , • ....„ r , .. , _ ,r.r. -,ri &lt; • 07. ~,, A . ''% 4', .? . - */ ,V - t '' .‘ ,'" ~,..4- , ..„ ..,.. • .. , , , , , , , - , ~ 4„, • L ' 4 1 ' \ .',, '' i , ' ' ''''' /. .A. ' . '''.• V'l i t f IN 1 I i 4,4/ tif ;.,... ~ ''' ' .•,. ~.., Ar.. ~ .0 it,,. II ' . , • -. ' - ----------- , .-</p>
        <p>THE QUEEN AND ATTENDANTS. Top row—D. Williams, May Ellis, E. Ford, E. Whitfield, A. Hamer. Middle ld eKenwright, W. Rowe, D. Fellows, A. Littlewood, EVA TANNER (Queen), May Tanner, A. War Matt, E. Todd, T. Lows. *fern, C. Houghton, J. Tanner, T. Williams, D. Plinston, V. Gleave.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>"THE TOY BALLOON."</head>
        <p>"THE TOY BALLOON."</p>
        <p>" The toy balloon," an action song, given by a number of dainty little girls, one of whom became despondent and cheered up again because she bursted her balloon. The soloist in this item was Miss Ivy Scarisbrick. "The Tinkers" were the cause of much merriment—and noise— as they marched round the stage, singing and beating old kettles, pans, and the like. The country dance by pupils of Miss E. Spinks was quite a contrast to the preceding item, for the young ladies danced gracefully. " Under the maple trees" and " Cracked Tom" were two excellent items, rendered very tastefully by the whole of the choir, who brought the proceedings to a close by unitedly singing the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>MAYOR'S PRAISE.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR'S PRAISE.</p>
        <p>The MAYot (Councillor P. Peacock), speaking before the festivities commenced, said he could not let the occasion pass without expressing his great admiration for the beautiful sight which the children were making. It reflected the greatest credit upon the parents, whom, he had noticed, always did look after their children, and went to a lot of trouble in preparing them for the event. (Applause.) Praise was also due to the workers in that great cause, because such festivals must entail a lot of sacrifice of time in these days of stress. There was only one thing he regretted, and that was there were so few boys amongst the juniors. He had asked the secretary wlfat was the reason,leibo y sb e rhee saida little tilfee bit d not know, only that tbackward. It was a serious weakness, because, after all, they ex- pected a lot tore wickedness and those things which were bad from boys—(laughter—mostly from the ladies)—and if they could only capture them when they were young it would be better for the town and the neighbourhood. About two-thirds of the crime irf Warrington was traceable to drink and its effects; therefore he hoped that the work of temperance would continue, and, he added. "I hope you will en- deavour to get hold of as many boys as you can. By children joining the organisation they will grow up better husbands and wives, fathers and mothers. I hope the work .will prosper."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>CHARACTERS.</head>
        <p>THE CHARACTERS.</p>
        <p>The following were the characters The " Queen" : Eva Tanner. Lady-in-Waiting : A. Littlewood. Crown-bearer: R. Grounds. Sceptre-bearer: F. Kenwright. Garland-bearer : T. Lowe. Train-bearers: J. Tanner, T. Williams, C. Houghton, and D. Plinston. Maids of Honour: E. Whitfield, A. Nurse, D. Fellows, and M. Tanner. John Bull: T. Littlewood. Britannia: E. Rollings. Beefeaters: B. Naylor, J. Porter, IT. Stringer, S. Newall, 0. Ditehfield and J. Hannan. Hoses: B. Heesom, D. Wilkinson, and E. Eland. Lilies : A. Wood. Marguerite: 31. Wood. Cornflower: D. Ball. Violets: E. Moss. Sunbeam: P. Watkins. Flower Queen: A. Rainer. Flower Girls: E. Ford, V. Gleave, D. Williams, W. Rowe, E. Hannan, E. Bonnell, and R. Redfern. Fairies: J. Davies, E. War- hurst, E. Todd, E. Roscoe, and H. Clare. High- lander: G. Newton. Welsh: A. Leech. Irish: S. Parr, D. Greenwood, D. Wilkinson, and A. Wall. Gipsy: A. Millington. Italians: F. Hinchliffe and R. Challen. Japanese: G. High- ley, D. Savage, and M. Clark. Hungarian Gipsy: Alan Hewitt. Ranchman : W. Holmes. jester: S. Millington. Boy Blue: P. Hazeldine. Red Riding Hood: F. Wilkinson and I. Hunter. Little Miss Moffat: E. A. Hinchliffe. Baker: G. Webster. Footballer: C. Tandor. Milk Maid: M. Smith. Fairies of the Spring: H. Hewitt, H. Apple- ton, M. Jones, L. Gallop, A. Birchall, G. Boyd, L. Williamson, A. Plinston, E. ICenwright, E. Whitby, A. Jackson, A. Lowe, L. Jones, M. Salt, D. Horton, C. Bannell, F. Leach, H. Kinsey, S. Caldwell, E. Sparks, A. Redyern, A. Robinson, A. Shuttleworth. Castanet Dancers: A. Derbyshire, E. Shaw, L. Robinson, M. Thomas, L. White, B. Brown, N. Payne, N. Jones, E. Naylor, L. Jones, E. Alcock, H. Plant, and L. Smith. Maypole Dancers: Girls—A. Wright, M. Furni- val, V. Davies, L. Clayton, J. Wright, W. Palmer, A. Atkinson, V. Thompson, A. Ellis, G. Bonnell, A. Shaw, M. Fellows, C. Ingram, and M. Cawley. Boys—J. Cunnington, E. Hannan, A. Nurse and C. Ellison. Action Song: Girls—D. Eland, M. Hazeldine, M. Hannan, L. Plinston, L. Sandford, A. Rams- bottom, C. Rutter, and E. Rustage. Doll Dancers: M. Harm!). M. Hartsman, L. Pye, M. Hewitt, M. Bissell, A. Yarwood, M. Cheeny, E. Barrett, F. Winstanley, L. Unsworth, P. Lowe, M. Robinson, L. Brown, L. Fox, and R. Cheeny. Balloon Dancers: E. Cotton, F. Smith, A. Ed- wards, H. Taylor, I. Searisbrick, E. Barrett, J.</p>
        <p>Harrop, H. Whitney, E. Davies, L. Green, I. Whitby, and P. Evans. Skipping Rope Dancers: E. Powell, M. Boyd, E. Little, F. Sankey, E Jones, F. Smith, C. Per- eival, H. Price, E. Hatch, M. Tickle, P. Jones, .11. Powell, and E. Johnson. _</p>
        <p>Country Dancers: G. Gallop, I. Smith, IL. Salt, L. Cope, IL Risley, F. Cunnington, J. Cardoe, K. Irons, K. Powell, IT. Palmer, V. Smith, IL Graham, V. Hayes, IL Elliott, I. Griffiths, H. Taylor, L. Newton, E. Clark, K. Warbrick, K. Randle, N. Shaweross, B. Rush- ton, A. Edwards, E. Roscoe, and E. Bowers. Action Song: Boys--E. Ashworth, T. Bibby, D. Bradbury, Ft. Gallop, E. Horton, F. Littlewood, R. Lawson, C. Sankey, H. Scruton, E. Swindles, and A. Nurse.</p>
        <p>_ Barbell Drill: Boys—J. Bate, T. Hatton, W. Houghton, F. Lowe, H. Blythe, J. Spencer, P. Butler, W. Maine, F. Edwards, and P. Yarwood. _ . _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Dickens' Characters: Mr. Pickwick, J. H. Bibby; Sam Weller, J. Nurse; Captain Cuttle, A. Appleton; Dick Swiveller, 11. Isherwood; Dan Peggotty, G. Tanner; Mr. Micawber, F. Jones; Mark Tapley, A. Mellor; Sairey Gain p. M. Stan- way; Mrs. Gummidge, E. A. Williams; Miss Miggs, E. Smith; Betsy Prig, F. Hunt; Agnes Wickfleld, E. Hazeldine ; Miss Nickleby, E. Gleave; Dora Dardle, G. Evans; Miss Min- flathers, L. Davies. Trainers and Pianists.—Maypole Dance: Trainer, Mrs. Whitmore; pianist, Mr. G. A. Whitfield. Skipping Exhibition: Trainer, Miss Spinks; pianist, Mrs. S. Woods. Action Song, "Fairies of the Spring " : Trainer, Mrs. Fellows; pianist, Mrs. B. Forman. Doll Dance: Trainer, Miss Spinks; pianist, Mrs. S. Woods. Action Song, " Merry maidens we": Trainer, Miss E. Cooper; pianist, Mr. D. Cooper. Barbell Drill: Trainer, Mr. D. Plinston ; pianist, Miss D. Jones. Song and Dance, "The toy balloon " : Trainer, Miss Spinks; pianist, Mrs. S. Woods. Action Song, "The Tinkers " : Trainers, Messrs. J. Nurse and J. H. Bibby; pianist, Mr. E. Perks. Country Dance: (Trainer, Miss E. Spinks; pianist, Mrs. S. Woods. Dickens' Pageant and Crowning Ceremony: Trainers, Mrs. Whitmore, Mrs. Shaw, Mr. G. Whitfield, and Mr. C. S. Fellows. LAST NIGHT'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>There was a good attendance at the Parr Hall last night, when a number of cinematograph pictures were exhibited. Illustrated songs were given by Miss Eva Tanner (the May Queen) and Mr. A. C. Hodgson, and recitations by Miss Amy Littlewood and Miss Annie Edwards. Thursday's programme will be repeated to- night, when the President (Mrs. Dodds) will preside. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>MAY-DAY IN WADDINGTON.</head>
        <p>MAY-DAY IN WADDINGTON.</p>
        <p>May Day revels have not altogether gone into the irretrievable past in Warrington, and the Maypole .was not forgotten by the children yes- terday. For many weeks they had been rehears- ing their parts, and in many quarters of the town beautiful spectacles were witnessed, the children being attired in multi-coloured cos- tumes as they tripped around the decorated pole singing appropriate old-time songs. The drivers of horses in the town seem to have been less interested, for only a few beribboned and brightly-harnessed horses were to be seen. Since the motor car has, to a large extent, super- seded the horse, it is only natural to expect that they should be decorated, but we only saw one or two with anything like a festive appearance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>PENKETII'S MAY QUEEN.</head>
        <p>PENKETII'S MAY QUEEN.</p>
        <p>The Infants' Department of the Penketh Council School, under the direction of Mrs. Bell (headmistress), Miss Routledge, and Miss Board- man, held their May Day celebrations yesterday afternoon in the school grounds, before a goodly number of parents and friends. The May Queen, who is entirely the children's choice, was Edith Griffiths, who looked a charming little ruler amongst her gallant courtiers. The onerous duties of " King" were lightly borne by Willie Leigh. The two Maids of Honour—Ruth Waine and Lizzie Maines—car- ried the "Queen's" train in right noble style. After the crowning veremony the whole assLim- bly sang "Come, lassies and lads." Follo‘ving that came the plaiting of the May-pole by IT. Leigh, M. rye, B. Thompson, A. Swift, T. Walker, A. Bowyer, 0. Marshall, N. Parsons, W. Roberts, G. Pusill, L. Thomas, and P. Ward. The children then rendered several appropriate songs, after which the Headmaster (Air. Grif- fiths), in a short speech, thanked the teachers and scholars for their fine display, and also the parents for their interest in the children's work. The Morris dancers were well reproduced by F. Dobson, B. Smith, F. Atkins, M. Pye, W. Leigh, A. Swift, 11. Ainsworth and P. Ward. Taken as a whole, the performance was a great credit to all concerned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>KNUTSFORD MAY QUEEN.</head>
        <p>KNUTSFORD MAY QUEEN.</p>
        <p>Yesterday afternoon the picturesque ceremony of crowning the May Queen was celebrated at Knutsford for the 50th time, in the presence of 'large crowds. Mrs. A. S. Jackson (nee Pollitt), the first "Queen" to hold such office in Knuts- ford, attended, and was accorded a place of honour in the procession. Miss Doris Pember- ton, the new May Queen, was neatly dressed in 'white, and bore herself with dignity throughout 1 the proceedings.</p>
        <p>In the course of the afternoon Miss Stevenson presented Mrs. Jackson with a handsome shower bouquet, and Mr. W. Wildgoose, as chairman of the committee, and on behalf of the organ- isers, handed to her a beautiful jewel brooch, as a memento of the occasion..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>BLVEBOT f LE.</head>
        <p>THE BLVEBOT f LE.</p>
        <p>Not So Dangerous as the House Fly But Breeds Maggots in Food.</p>
        <p>BY C. E. PARKER. Last week I wrote about the col:ninon hoists fly. His big brother, the bluebottle, deserees attention. Everyone knows the big ly, with its black, blue, shining armour, whiell, i aid' autumn, gets into our rooms, and whom ea its flight keeps up a continual buzzing, and duke all efforts to catch it. If, in our-bedrooms, we chase it round the room with a towel, it ligats oa the ceiling and laughs at us,again begins ita flight and buzzing, and at length aliguts en the edge of the mantel-shelf. We flick at it, Mi3S too fly and bring down the photograph of our bit girl. We give it up in despair and try to 1.1/41 the buzz of the insect to lull us to sleep. Probably most are familiar with this o,r in its maggot form. In. August we buy a brace of grouse, and invite some friends to tho meal. The birds are :cooked and dished, possibly the cook thinks them high, but, then, grouse ought to be high. The poulterer says "they are just shot, have only been kept by us fer three days." As the carver puts hie knife into a breast he finds the interior full of roasted • maggots, and the dish is sent out, with various signs of dis- gust and disappointment Our friends try their best to be polite, and say there is nothing they like better than rice pudding and bananas. These maggots are our bluebottles in their infancy. I have known grouse only two days old filled with these loathsome, but, at the same time, useful creatures. I remember shooting a bird on going up to a grouse drive. I put it into a bole in a dry stone dyke. Coming down in the evening, I 'found it full of maggot.. The- day was hot, and the surrounding stones probably warm. A bluebottle must have got early te work, laid its eggs, and within eight hours these had developed into maggots. The bluebottle is not like the house fly, which lays its eggs in any sort of refuse; it goes for the flesh of some animal, butcher's meat, or game. Under normal conditions the hatching takes place in a couple of days, but in warm, places the process is more rapid. Both the bluebottle and its allied species, the flesh fly, choose the mouth, eyes, or some wound in the bird in which to lay their eggs.</p>
        <p>SOME OBSERVATIONS. M. Henri Fabre, a very painstaking aad ass:i- rate observer, experimented oa blusbottles, awl an article on his work appeared lately ia oa• of the Reviews, lie caught a female bluebottle ready for laying, and Put it ilt a bell-thulted cage of wire gauze, ia which was also the body of a dead bird. He describes the laying promos in the mouth as follows:</p>
        <p>"The beak of the bird invaded was 'loud as far as the natural sontact ef the 'mandibles allowed. There remained a narrow slit at tha base, sufficient at most to admit ths passage of a horse hair. It was through this that the laying was performed. Lengthening her ovipositor like a telescope, the mother inserted the point of her implement, a point slightly hardened with Ai horny armour. The fineness of the probe equals the fineness of the aperture. But, if the beak were entirely closed, where would the eggs be laid then?</p>
        <p>" With a tied thread, I keep the two mandibleS in absolute contact; and I place a second blue- bottle in the presence of the linnet, which the colonists have already entered by the beak. This time, the laying takes place on one of the eyes between the lid and the eye-ball. At the hatching, which again occurs a couple of days later, the grubs make their way into the fleshy depths of the socket. The eyes and the beak, therefore, form the two chief entrances into feathered game. "There are others; and these are the wounds. I cover the linnet's head with a paper hood, which will prevent invasion through the beak and eyes- I serve it, under the wire-gauze bell, to a third egg-layer. The bird has been struck by a shot in the breast, but the sore is not bleeding; no outer stain marks the injured spot. Moreover, I am careful to arrange the feathers, to smooth them with a hair-pencil, so that the bird looks quite smart and has every appearance of being untouched. "The Ay is soon there. She inspects the lin- net from end to end; with her front tarsi she fumbles at the breast and belly. It is a sort of auscultation by sense of touch. The insect becomes aware of what is under the feathers by the manner in which these re-act. If scent comes to her assistance, it can only be very slightly, for the game is not yet high. The wound is soon found. No drop of blood is near it, for it is closed by a plug of down rammed into it by the shot. The fly takes up her position without separating the feathers or uncovering the wound. She remains here for two hours without stirring, motionless, with her abdomen concealed beneath the plumage. My eager curiosity does not distract her for a moment from her business. "When she has finished, I take her place. There is nothing either on the skin or at the mouth of the wound. I have to withdraw the downy plug and dig to some depth before dis- covering the eggs. The ovipositor has therefore lengthened its extensible tube and pushed be- yond the feather stopper driven in by the lead. The eggs are in one packet; they number about three hundred."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>PRACTICAL VALUE OF THE EXPERI-</head>
        <p>PRACTICAL VALUE OF THE EXPERI-</p>
        <p>MENTS. Monsieur Fabre gives some striking incidents of the perspicacity of instinct of the mother fly. He narrates an experiment he made with a tin about four inches deep, containing a piece of butcher's meat. The lid was laid on a trifle off the straight, leaving a narrow slit at one point of its circumference, too small to allow the fly to enter. When the bait began to give off scent the flies were attracted to it. They explored the metal receptacle for some time, seeking an entrance. Finding nothing, they began laying their eggs on the tin, on the edge of the aper- ture. By some means the fly knew that the new- born worms would be able to find the slit, and creep dewn the sides of the tin to the food. The. investigator repeated a similar experiment with a test tube, with a wire-gauze over the top. The flies laid their eggs upon this gauze. He gradu- ally lengthened the test tubes to 25 inches, and the laying continued. Then he tried a tube 46 inches deep, when the flies ceased to lay. This refusal could not have been a question of smell, as the smell from the meat was clearly perceived by the investigator. Did the bluebottle reason that a fall of 46 inches would damage the soft grub, while it would not do so at 25 inches? And how was it able to judge the depth? Was there some difference in the smell caused by the greater length of travel? Who can tell? These experiments have a practical applica- tion. An ordinary meat-safe keeps the flies out, but does not prevent the flies attracted by the smell laying their eggs upon the gauze at the top, which, as they mature into maggots, will drop down through the gauze and attack any- thing lying on the floor; hence the necessity of hanging meat Or game. We suggest covering the top of the safe, and top only, with paper or cloth. Monsieur Fabre vecommends that game should be enclosed in separate paper bags or envelopes, which, if of the right character, will allow a sufficient air circulation, but form an impenetrable barrier to the flies. Paper is equally successful against moths, those plagues of our furs and clothes. This is far more effec- tive than camphor, lavender, and the like. Newspaper will answer the purpose if the edges are joined with a double fold and well pinned. The bluebottle is a much more respectable individual, and does far less harm, than the common house fly. As we showed lastweek, latterthe h better while an feed th d o bluebottlegarbage, prefersthe filthier s sugary exudations of trees and flowers. se s. food from decayed animal matter, but prefers sweet substance meat NN;h ic h provide food for the young maggots. • But the eggs -are always laid in things like i• As a disease carrier, he is not to b. e compared_ _ with the common house fly. Still to could • . very well do without him, and for this reason be buried, as all decayed animal matter should ; it provides a nest and Underground, for thd, other the development • of healthy maggots. L , of life will effect its decomposition, with no after,o forms evil consequences.</p>
      </div>
