      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>. ' , ~,, – ~---_,„„ ~ ~ _ -4.,._,,,,, :;-----.: DISTRICT PIE f 5 i'N'... – 1 .....„.. . _._ __. .__ -,.._. _:i: :__ .. -'`.....';•</head>
        <p>. ' , ~,, - ~---_,„„ ~ ~ _ -4.,._,,,,, :;-----.: DISTRICT PIE f 5 i'N'... - 1 .....„.. . _._ __. .__ -,.._. _:i: :__ .. -'`.....';•</p>
        <p>STOeKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>revenue the hoardings would be removed. He hoped the County Council would take steps, under the powers they possessed, to have the hoardings removed, as they were a source of public danger.—The matter was left in the nands of the Clerk.</p>
        <p>Exciting Scene.—An exciting scene was wit- nessed in Stockton Heath late on Tuesday. Some men who have returned to work at Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co.'s works were escorted home by a large body of pickets and others. On the return journey a stone was thrown through the window of a shop in Walton-rd, com- pletely destroying the large plate-glass pane. Extra police have been drafted into the district to maintain good order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>IOWNSIIIP THILE-TATTLE.</head>
        <p>IOWNSIIIP THILE-TATTLE.</p>
        <p>The Bridge of Sighs, again. Thera ia more than sighing, However, over that same Swing Bridge. Of tea Tau naughty language is used. Thie bugbear of the " Heathens" was again Disoussed at the Parochial Committee meeting. I The Canal Company say they cannot Put a light in the centre of the bridge. The local Councillors say that they can do so, And se more ink is to be spilt Over this battle of the bridge lighting. There is no doubt, as some Councillors said, The bridge is dangerous at night time. The eentre is wrapped in Egyptian darkness. Surely it is not beyond the wit of the clever Eleetrioians to instal a light which will Meat the requirements of the public and the company. Councillor G. 'Pilling has been re-elected chairman of the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee, and he</p>
        <p>Desarres the honour, for he is an ideal chair- man.</p>
        <p>Sonie "Heathens" want electric light for the Purpose of illuminating their "mansions." The Parochial Committee, like cautious men, Are first of all to inquire the cost of it. The Brewery Fire Brigade answered the first Call made upon them to attend a fire In Stockton Heath on Tuesday, but luck</p>
        <p>Wa.i dead" agin" them in the Shape of the Swing Bridge, which closed as they approached. Then they were informed that the fire was out, nd So they returned after a short spin.</p>
        <p>Some strikers rushed to the fire, but Their services were not at all required. "Another job lost," quoth one of them. Strawberries are now in bloom up the Heath Way,.</p>
        <p>This is a fortnight earlier than in past seasons. The cuckoo has not yet arrived, although Local naturalists have been on the outlook. Thus we cannot say Summer has come.</p>
        <p>Chairman Re-elected.—Councillor G. Pilling, on the motion of Councillor J. Hallows, was re- elected chairman of the Parochial Committee. Anything I can do, he said, in expressing his thanks, in the interests of Stockton Heath, I shall do so. On the motion of Councillor Mclntyre. Councillor Clarke was elected vice- chairman.</p>
        <p>Members Present.—At the annual meeting of the. Stockton Heath Parochial Committee on Tuesday night the members present were: Councillors Pilling, Pearson, Mclntyre, McDer- mid, Warburton, Whitley, Bottomley, J. Hal- lows, C. HallowA. The officials were: Mr. Ash- ton (clerk), and Mr. Wright (surveyor), and Aix, Bradshaw, clerk to the Parish Council was also present.</p>
        <p>Electricity Wanted.—Do Stockton Heath people want electric light? The idea is that some do, and at the meeting of the Parochial Committee on Tuesday, Councillor Bottomley moved that the committee ask Warrington Cor- poration on what terms they would supply elec- tricity to Stockton Heath. There were certain residents who desired it.—Councillor Mclntyre secended, and the resolution was adopted.</p>
        <p>Fire.—An outbreak of fire occurred on Tues- day in an outbuilding at Birchdale, owned by Mr. T. Hall. As a considerable quantity of straw and hay was in danger, the Wilderspool Brewery Fire Brigade were summoned. They turned out with the steamer and were detained some minutes at the Swing Bridge. On reach- ing Whitefield-road the brigade were stopped by the information that the fire had been ex- tinguished by some workmen.</p>
        <p>That "South Pole." The *residents of Grap- penhall-road have been troubled by the G.P.O. erecting a telephone pole on the south side of the road and marring the beauty of the spot. This tall spar, which has facetiously been called the "South Pole," was brought to the atten- tion of the Parochial Committee, who commu- nicated with the Post Office, and at a meeting of- the Council on Tuesday night a letter was read from the Chief Engineer to the G.P.O. stating that the pole was to be removed to a position nearer Sandy-lane. Councillor Pear- wan: That is satisfactory. Scarlet Fever Complaint.—The Parochial Committee on Tuesday had a complaint before them that a scarlet fever patient had been re- moved from one house to another.—Dr. Adams, the medical officer, explained that one of two children in a house had taken scarlet fever, and to safeguard the other it was taken to its grandmother's, next door. When this child took the fever it was removed to its home again.— Councillor McDermid : Just removed home?— Dr. Adams: Yes.—The explanation was deemed satisfactory.</p>
        <p>Which Pavement?—Victoria-road is being paved with blue chequer tiles, and to this two of the property owners have taken exception. A letter was read from one of these at the Parochial Committee on Tuesday, asking that the pavements should be of concrete flags.—Mr. Ashton (the clerk) suggested that the two owners should be asked to pay the cost of re- Planing the tiles with flags.—Councillor Whit- ley: There are complaints all over the village about the chequer tiles.—Councillor Bottomley suggested that the other two landlords in the street should be asked their views about the matter, and this was agreed to unanimously.</p>
        <p>These Hoardings.—At the Parochial Com- mittee on Tuesday, Councillor Bottomley asked if they could not have the hoardings at the Stockton 'Heath end of the Swing Bridge re- raoved. Their presence hid the crossing, and Hag nearly led to a serious motor car collision the ether night. An official of the Ship Canal had told him that the hoardings were a source or revenue to the company, but he felt sure that if the committee paid a sum equal to the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>PARADISE</head>
        <p>PARADISE</p>
        <p>OF THE</p>
        <p>THE !Cie; f I 4W Cllt? - t 1. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>GOLFER</head>
        <p>GOLFER</p>
        <p>5 hours from London. Week-end tickets 50 per cent, reduction from ,Dover. Rising seaside resort. Plots of ground far sale cheap for build- ing purposes. No death duties. Finest golf on the Continent. Englisb Church. Hotels mod- erate. For booklet and Prospectus free; apply FL E. Fuller, Ltd., Southampton Row London, W.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>ZOUTE-KNOCKE</head>
        <p>LE ZOUTE-KNOCKE</p>
        <p>- NEAR OSTEND</p>
        <p>The recent weather has been splendid golf course, which is in tine condition it i said by those who should know, to be one of the best 18 hole courses on the Continentr-and has that additional charm for all lovers of the health-giving game of being close to the shore, where there is a beautiful Leach of fine white sand some fifty yards wide at low tide, and extending about twenty-five miles along the coast, running by Ostend and Blankenberghe and along to the frontier of Holland, only a *oft distance from Le Zoute-Knocke. Owing to the fine beach and good bathing, this golfing station is a great favourite with the children.</p>
        <p>for the is</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>MIXED BATHING WANTED. Matter Discussed and Dropped.</head>
        <p>MIXED BATHING WANTED. Matter Discussed and Dropped.</p>
        <p>The question, much discussed elsewhere, of permitting mixed bathing has at last been raised in Stockton Heath. The topic ctop'ped up at the annual meeting of the Swimming, Boating and Angling Association, held in the Institute on Monday night. Mr. A. Bottomley, who presided, regretted the fact that the people of Stockton Heath took so little interest in the work of the association, and those who ran it considered such indiffer- ence was not fair treatment to them. The report and balance sheet showed a defi- ciency of £9 on an expenditure of £5l 195., and in moving adoption Mr. J. Fletcher said if the public did not want to use the " lake " the association would have to come to an end.</p>
        <p>Mr. Sutton thought a way to save the situa- tion would be to allow mixed bathing. It would attract members.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said the rules did not permit it, and to change these a general meeting of the members would have to be held. The subject then dropped, and all the retir- ing officials were re-elected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>SWING BRIDGE DANGER SPOT. Parochial Committee Demand a Lamp in Centre of Bridge. COMPANY AGAIN APPROACHED.</head>
        <p>SWING BRIDGE DANGER SPOT. Parochial Committee Demand a Lamp in Centre of Bridge. COMPANY AGAIN APPROACHED.</p>
        <p>The question of lighting the Swing Bridge came up again at the meeting of the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee on Tuesday, Mr. G. Pilling presiding. Mr. Ashton (the Clerk) announced receipt of a letter in reply to the one he sent to the Ship Canal Company. He had also sent the resolu- tion passed by the committee. The company's secretary wrote saying that he was not aware the coroner's jury attributed the accident to one of the employes to the lighting of the bridge. The safety of the public was not con- cerned in the lighting of the bridge after the gates had been closed and the bridge swung. Their servants had no difficulty in carrying out their duties through lack of lighting on the bridge. While the bridge was being swung for ' the passage of vessels they were provided with hand-lamps. The youth who lost his life should not have been on the bridge, but at the gates. He had seen that Mr. Whitley had stated that there were two gas lamps, with incandescent, burners, which threw a good light on a portion of the bridge, whereas the Warrington Corpora- tion only had one lamp hid behind a standard at their end, and he understood efforts had failed to get better lighting at the Warrington end. He asked for some more information so as to make representations to the Warrington Corporation to provide better lighting.</p>
        <p>"IT IS TOMMY-ROT." Councillor Bottomley : I do not think that is an ansc‘er to our complaint. It is simply a " get out." (Hear, hear.) There is no doubt the bridge is dangerous, particularly if there are motor cars crossing. This letter—it is all tommy-rot. (Hear, hear.) The Chairman: They talk about the lighting of the bridge after it is swung. We did not want to know about that.</p>
        <p>Councillor nottomley : We did not refer to their workmen. We want to secure the safety of the public. (Hear, hear.) Councillor Mclntyre: It is when the bridge is open for road traffic that we want light.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S POSITION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S POSITION.</p>
        <p>Councillor Whitley said the public had noth- ing to do with the bridge when it was turned for vessels to pass through. He had met Alder- man John Evans, chairman of the Warrington Gas Committee at the bridge, along with Mr. A. Hallway, the Deputy Town Clerk, and they talked the matter of lighting over. Both were agreed that the lamp at the Warrington end was at the wrong place, and it would be better if it was placed in another position. Alderman John Evans had said he did not see that the Warrington Corporation were bound to light the Ship Canal Company's bridge. (Hear, hear.) It was also stated that it was not for them to do so, because the Stockton Heath boundary went to the edge of the water nearest Warrington, but the Stockton Heath boundary did not go over the old canal, and therefore there were quite 20- yards of the bridge in Warrington. He thought Warrington were responsible for the lighting of that part. He was willing to meet the canal officials and show what was required. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>DANGER IN THE MIDDLE.</head>
        <p>DANGER IN THE MIDDLE.</p>
        <p>Councillor Bottomley : The danger is in the middle of the bridge, and it would be remedied if a lamp was put there. If it is a question of s. d., and the company are not prepared to provide it, I propose that the company be asked to allow the District Council to erect a light in the centre, where it is positively dan- gerous without a lamp. Councillor Mclntyre: Are there any other bridges so lighted, and is it possible to have a lamp in the centre? Councillor McDermid : The company say a lamp cannot be put there. •Councillor J. Hallows: What is to prevent them putting an electric lamp there, and when the bridge begins to swing it could be switched out automatically? Are we to light the bridge for nothing for them? Councillor Pearson: No, certainly not.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>ADVANTAGES TAKEN AWAY.</head>
        <p>ADVANTAGES TAKEN AWAY.</p>
        <p>• Councillor Whitley: The company took many of our advantages away, and I think in return for these the Ship Canal Company should do something. (Hear, hear.) The Chairman: They made an excuse that a light on the bridge ~vould make it imi.ossible for vessels to be ste(l safely through. At Latchford there is a light almost over the centre of the canal, and yet the vessEds are steered all right. Councillor Bottomley : It is a matter that can easily he got over. I think the committee will do well to record in the minutes that they asked the Ship Canal Company to allow them to erect a light on the bridge. I feel sure you will not get permission, but at the same time it will show we have tried our very utmost to remove a positive danger, and if something serious should occur in the future no responsi- bility could be placed upon our shoulders., move such a resolution.</p>
        <p>SHOW THEIR DETERMINATION. The Chairnian: It will show our deternaina- tion to remove a danger. Councillor Whitley: Would it not be wise to ask the Warrington Corporation to join ith us?</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I don't hinii they will have anything to do with us. Councillor Whitley: The Deputy Town Cl?rli thought something should be done. The Chairman: Would it not be advisable to add to your resolution, Mr. Bottomley, that an electric lamp should be erected? Councillor Bottoinley. : I meant that. The Chairman: If at all possible, the com- pany will get out of anything we suggest. Councillor Whitley: Will Warrington be pre- pared to find the electricity? The Chairman: Yes, if we .pay for it. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>Cotincillor Bottomley's resolution was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 2, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 2, 1914.</p>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.-"-</p>
        <p>Postman Honoured.—Mr. T. Pitchford, for many years postman in the district, has, on his retirement, been presented with a purse of gold.</p>
        <p>Independent Methodist Church.—The Sunday School anniversary sermons in connection with the above place of worship were held on Sunday, The special preacher being Mr. J. Crumblehulme, of Southport, who preached to large congrega- tions, both morning and evening. Mr. T. Leah presided at the organ. In the afternoon the Rev. W. Reid, of the Warrington Presbyterian Church, delivered a very instructive address to the children. Special hymns were sung by the scholars. Madame Lily Fairhurst was the solo- ist. She sang with very pleasing effect "The Raft" and "The Voice in the Old Cathedral." Mr. A. E. Trow was the accompanist. The col- lections, which were on behalf of the Sunday Schools, realised the sum of 1:14.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>DARESBURY.</head>
        <p>DARESBURY.</p>
        <p>Bellringers Meet—The quarterly meeting of the local branch of the Chester Diocesan Guild of Church Bellringers was held on Saturday, when about 40 members from neighbouring parishes attended. A good attendance marited the short service, which preceded the meeting. Miss Annie Houghton, who presided at the organ, played Elgar's " Carissima" aid Lemare's "Salut d'Amour," following these by Bach's Toccata. After tea, provided by the Vicar, the bells were rung in different methons. The tones of the new peal of bells afford general satisfaction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>ST RET TON.</head>
        <p>ST RET TON.</p>
        <p>Late Mrs. Bostock.=—The funeral of the late Mrs. Bostock took place at Stratton Parish Church, when, owing to the illness of the Vicar, the Rev. T. C. Trampleasure, of Whitley, conducted the last rites. Floral tributes wire received from the following: " Brother and sister," Birchdale Farm; "Alfred. and Annie," Dr. and Mrs. Bower, Miss Pen, •` Pollie and Ted," Mr. Derbyshire and family (Sheperoff! Mrs. Spencer, "Alice and family," Mrs. 1.70 n, "Stanley, Sam and Fanny," "Amy," 1%17'5. Howells, Mrs. Martin: Mrs. Furnival, Miss Howard and Mrs. Davies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Primrose Leaguers.—ThePrimrose League held the annual re-union with a whist drive and dance in the Sankey Sunday School. The M.C.'s for the evening were: Whist, Mr. U. Darling- ton; dancing, Mr. E. Duinbill. Messrs. J. and W. Ray 'provided the music for the dancing. Mrs. Clarke superintended the wfreshment section.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Weddings.—The account of two Penketh wed- dings will be found in another column under the heading of At Hymen's Altar."</p>
        <p>Narrow Escape from Drowning.—Albert Ains- worth (12), son a Mr. S. Ainsworth, Victoria- road, Penketh, had a very narrow escape from drowning in the London and North-Western Canal on Sunday evening. He sat down upon the end of a wooden swing bridge which spans the canal near Messrs. Hayward's Sheep Dip Works. A flat arrived and the bridge was pushed open to let it pass. The jar caused by the bridge reaching the limit of its swing, threw the lad into the water. The flat passed on, those in charge of it apparently unaware of what had taken place. Fortunately help ar- rived in the shape of a young man and lady companion. Using the scanty means at hand, they hauled the lad to the bank, then taking what measures seemed necessary they got him comparatively right again. Unfortunately the names of the rescuers are unknown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>PENKETII LIBERAL CLUB.</head>
        <p>PENKETII LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>Ilurnorous Way of Electing a Secretary.</p>
        <p>ROUTLEDGE AGAIN PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>The Penketh Liberal Club held their annual meeting on Thursday. Before business com- menced the members sat down to a generous meal, provided by their president, Mr. R. Rout- ledge, who has held this honoured position for some years.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. Routledge presided. The treasurer, Mr. W. Dwerryhouse, gave an account of his stewardship, and showed that he had a 'balance of £8 14s. in hand. The Chairman said he felt particularly strong on the social side of the club, ,and as a man,of matured years, he was most pleased to see so many young men taking part in its work, and he hoped that all members would do all they could to make a new member feel perfectly at home from the commencement of his association with the club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>Political Situation.</head>
        <p>Political Situation.</p>
        <p>As to the political side, he thought that just at present the least said the better. But he was proud to see a man, who thirty years ago was pointed out as the coming man, stand out prominently through a trying ordeal, which would have crushed many another of less de- cided character. That man was the Prime Minister.</p>
        <p>The election of officers then took place. There was a general wish for the President to accept re-election, but Mr. R. Routledge thought that he had held office long enough, although he ad- mitted that during his tenure of office he had thoroughly enjoyed the official position, and the companionship of the members. The vote being put to the meeting it was unanimously agreed that they could not have a more worthy president than Mr. Routledge, who ultimately agreed to hold office for another year. The vice-presidents for the next twelve months are: Messrs. F. Dale, H. Wilkinson, C. E. Parker, F. Hughes, S. Hughes, J. Turner, Dwerryhouse, T. B. Aitken, P. C. Garnett, E. A. Murphy, and W. Lightfoot, vice the late Mr. T. Bell, deceased.</p>
        <p>The Secretary, Mr. Herbert Davies, opposed re-election, pointing out that he had been prom- ised a release when attaining double figures, and as he had served 10 years he was quite ready to accept what was due.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>True Wit.</head>
        <p>True Wit.</p>
        <p>A Member then pointed out that nought stood for nothing, so eventually he was prevailed upon to accept the vote of the meeting. The Chairman, in introducing the election pf treasurer, said that they could not be too care- ful whom they selected for such a position of trust, a matter of £59 being handled during the year, and as he appreciated to the full the way the accounts had been kept, he,felt that it was necessary for the well-being of the club that the treasurer, Mr. W. Dwerryhouse, should be re- elected. This was promptly done. The committee are: Messrs. F. Dendy, J. J. Foden, B. Darlington, D. Houghton, R. Acomb, D. Matthews, W. Leigh, and A. Higham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>HIGH LEGFI.</head>
        <p>HIGH LEGFI.</p>
        <p>Wedding.—Au account of the wedding of Mr. W. A. Jones and Miss A. Ford will be found under the heading of "At Hymen's Altar" on another page.</p>
        <p>Farm Fire.—ln the early hours of Thursday morning a fire occurred at the farm of Mr. W. E. Yarwood, Sowton Heath. The local brigade, under the direction of Capt. J. Melrose, found a large haystack ablaze. The men worked strenu- ously to save the farm buildings. The stack was destroyed. The cause is unknown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>LINES FROM LYMM.</head>
        <p>LINES FROM LYMM.</p>
        <p>Nature is an excellent decorator. She has now painted Lymm in All the gorgeous colours of the Spring. The recent heat made the "nuts" ' Don flannel shirts and straw hats. The "nutesses" did not lag behind. They appeared in summery muslin. The Councillor's lot is not a happy one. If in doubt, ask a member of the District Council. Councillor Gotobed made an effective reply To the remark's by Chairman Warburton. It was alleged that the Council was respon- sible For the raising of the rents of cottages. Councillor Gotobed proved it was not so. The sensation of the week has been the Sudden resignation of Councillor S. Davies. The Council have thus lost a keen, honest worker. He made an ideal chairman of the District Council. Who will be the Heatley Ward candidates? In a certain Warrington school the pupils Were instructed to write an essay Descriptive of a fishing expedition. One youngster described how he caught Salmon in the waters of the Lymm Dam. Lymm has much fame, but it cannot Yet boast possession of a salmon stream. A census of vehicular traffic in Lymm Has been taken, and some interesting facts are disclosed.</p>
        <p>The Rector Away.—The Rector (the Rev. G. B. Thurston) has, during the present week, been enjoying the beauties of another district, as a brief respite from his duties.</p>
        <p>"Co-op." Stores.—This week has seen the com- mencement of the building operations of the new Co-operative Stores at Church Green. This is another branch of the Warrington Co-operative Society.</p>
        <p>Lymm Bel[ringers at Daresbury.—Lymm 'sent its quota to the quarterly meeting of the local branch of the Chester Diocesan Guild of Bell- ringers, held at Daresbury Parish Church on Saturday. A short service preceded the meeting and a display of campanology was given.,</p>
        <p>Illness of Rev. J. A. Penaliggon.—We under- stand that the Rev. A. Penaliggon, pastor of the Lyrnm and Bucklow Hill Congregational Churches, is very slowly recovering from his illness, but it will be a long time before he will be able to resume his duties. , •</p>
        <p>Old Mill Machinery Scrapped.—Many villagers have been alarmed during the week by the ex- plosions necessitated by the blasting operations at the Old Mill at the foot of Eagle Brow. There being no further need for the old machin- ery, it is be.-g broken up and carried away for scrap.</p>
        <p>Bridging ' Dam.—The building of the bridge over the Lym n Dam is proceeding satisfactorily. The woode- supports have been erected, and when the s ructure is completed it promises to be a worthy addition to the district. The fine new road leading to the bridge is an excellent approach.</p>
        <p>War on Sleepers-out—At an Occasional Court held at the Lymm Police Station on Monday, four of the wandering class were charged, be- fore Mr. J. Gould, with sleeping out, and were each sentenced to 14 days, with hard labour. The action of the police in attempting to stop this nuisance is commendable.</p>
        <p>Heatley Girl's Success.—Miss Isabel Ingham, the 14-year-old daughter of Mr. F. Ingham, of Heatley, has performed a praiseworthy feat in having recently passed the advanced senior ex- amination of the London College of Music. The young lady, who is a pupil of Miss Wright, of High Legh, has previously won five certificates.</p>
        <p>Traffic Census.—The Surveyor repoAed to Lymm Council on Monday that a census was taken of the traffic in Booth's Hill between March 30 and April 3 inclusive, and had resulted In the following figures: Total tonnage, 3,840; total number of vehicles, 5,302; and the total number of animals, 1,123.</p>
        <p>Late Mr. Gould's Will.—The will of Council- lor John William Gould, Higher-lane, Lyrnm, who met with a tragic death on the Midland Railway by falling from an express train when returning from the horse show in London, was proved on Tuesday. His business was stated as a cloth merchant in Manchester, and he left 48,140, of which .45,353 is net personalty.</p>
        <p>Horse On Strike.—The hot weather of the pre- sent week has not been conducive to the best of work, and this sentiment was embodied in the actions of a Lymm fishmonger's and green- grocer's horse. The animal, whilst in the neigh- I bourhood of High Legh on Wednesday after- I noon, was affected ivith the strike fever and refusing to work, laid down its weary body on the road, with the result that the cart over- balanced, and—fish and oranges gambolled in the sand,</p>
        <p>"The Choicest Gift," Service of Song in Aid of I nfirmaries.—A beautiful rendering of a service of song, entitled was given on Sunday afternoon by the choir and scholars connected with the Lymm Congregational Church, when Mr. J. D. B. Smith presided. The singing was much admired and reflected credit upon the organist and choirmaster, Mr. A. Leather, who was responsible for the training of the choir and children. A substantial collection was taken, by which the Warrington and Manchester Infir- maries, will benefit to the extent of .C 5 each. Commencement of Cricket.—The village -iicket team commences the season to-day, when the Earlestown second team are to be entertained at the Lymm ground. There i every prospect of a good season, the majority of last year's players s again throwing in their lot with the team, as well as a number of hew men, who are expected to show up to advantage. There is no doubt that the services of Mr. Bell, who has done so much for the club, will be greatly missed. Mr. H. S. Saville, jun., will captain the senior team this year, and Mr. E. Waterworth the second.</p>
        <p>Increased Gas Consumption.—At Lymm Coun- cil on Monday the Gas INlanager reported that the make of gas for the month ending March 25 was 1,936,000 cubic feet, being an increase of 100,000 cubic feet over the corresponding month last year. The amount of coal used was 904 tons year, giving the average make of gas at 9,600 cubic feet, against 9,900 cubic feet last year. The Gas Manager also asked for permission to close Burford-road Bridge for two consecutive days in order to lay and test an extension of the gas pipes. This the Council agreed to, stating that notice of the closing would first be made.o —Conill eor Goto- bed asked why on a recent night every alternate lamp in Eagle Brow was unlit, and the Gas Manager, in reply, stated he was unaware of anything wrong.— yield Gotobed asked why there was a poor from the coal.—Mr. Don- aldson: It is the quality. Ile had complained, but there was no alteration at present.</p>
        <p>Nursing Lectures.—A series of special lectures for women on sick nursing (second course) are being given in the Pepper-street Schools, Lymm, by Miss Hawkes, a lecturer appointed by the Higher Education Committee of the County Council.</p>
        <p>To Aid the Blind.—The Lymm Congregation- alists, in common -with their fellow worshippers throughout the country,iwill on May lath have special collections in aid of the blind. Mrs. Egerton is the local collector.</p>
        <p>Motor Mishap.—Whilst a Liverpool motor car was passing along the road between Lymm Church and the bridge on Monday afternoon, the steering gear went out of order, with the result that the car crashed into the wall. The two occupants of the car were shaken, but un- hurt, and the front of the machine was buckled. The car was placed on a truck and towed to Mr. Saville's garage for repairs. The wall was slightly damaged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>COUNCIL SENSATION. • Councillor Samuel Davies Suddenly Resigns HIS Seat. SEQUEL TO THE. ANNUAL MEETING.</head>
        <p>COUNCIL SENSATION. • Councillor Samuel Davies Suddenly Resigns HIS Seat. SEQUEL TO THE. ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>A Guinea Retiring Fine Paid. A sensation has been occasioned in Lymm by the unexpected resignation of Councillor Samuel Davies. The surprise was sprung upon the meeting of the District Council on Monday night, and the retirement of Councillor. Davies is believed to have been a sequel to an incident which occurred at the annual meeting of the Council last week, when Councillor Davies was ruled out of order by the new chairman. Mr. Davies, who has been a member for the Heatley Ward, and until recently.was chairman of the Council, tendered a guinea as a fine for retiring.</p>
        <p>The first public intimation of his resignation came in the form of a letter which stated:</p>
