        <p>MR. SAMUEL DAVIES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>LETTER OF RESIGNATION.</head>
        <p>LETTER OF RESIGNATION.</p>
        <p>lleatley Platt Farm, Heatley. Dear Mr. Mullard,— It is with regret I send my resignation as the member for Healey Ward on the Drban District Council, and I wish especially to thank all the members who have been on the Council during the ten years I have been a member, and also yourself and Mr. David Mort, and all the officials, for the kindness you and they have shown to me. For the ten years I have been a member I have always tried to be a regular attender, honourable and straightforward to the best interests of the ratepayers, and perhaps in some future time I may again join your Council. Again asking you to accept my thanks, I am, yours truly, SAMUEL DAVIES.</p>
        <p>GUINEA FINE PAID. The Clerk (Mr. Mullard) intimated that Mr. Davies had paid the retiring fine of one guinea, and that the seat would have to be declared vacant.</p>
        <p>• _ Councillor Dixon moved that the resignation be accepted with deep regret. Councillor Holt seconded, stating that the Council had suffered a great loss, and that Mr. Davies would be a "missed man." He had left them at a bad time, for be was a most regular attender to the Council's interests. The amount of time Mr. Davies had put in was "enormous."</p>
        <p>A SERIOUS LOSS. Councillor Gotobed supported. The Council had previously lost two old memliers, and now they were losing Mr. Davies. These three mem- bers between them represented a total of 40 years' service, and no small Council could afford to lose such, without feeling the loss very, very much. Mr. Davies had occupied many positions in the Council, but he was best as chairman of the Roads Committee; in fact, they were all aware of the amount of work he did in that capacity.</p>
        <p>_ Councillor Kershaw asked the Clerk if the fine could he remitted, and received a negative answer.</p>
        <p>NOT "SPORTSMANLIKE."</p>
        <p>Councillor Cowper thought it was a great pity Mr. Davies had resigned. He did not consider it was sportsmanlike. It was only a matter of pique. _</p>
        <p>e'ouneillor Kershaw retaliated by saying that now the matter had been drawn out he would say he quite sympathised with Mr. Davies, for he believed he was treated with scant courtesy. (War, hear.) The resolution was carried unanimously. The seat in the Heatley Ward was declared vacant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>I_ A TOUCHING INTERMENT.</head>
        <p>I_ A TOUCHING INTERMENT.</p>
        <p>Funeral of Mrs. Molesworth.</p>
        <p>• There were many touching incidents and tokens of respect at the funeral at Lymm on Saturday of Mrs. Molesworth, wife of Mr. H. N. Molesworth, of Brook House. _</p>
        <p>The deceased lady, who died on the previous Wednesday, was exceedingly well known, and a large number of villagers assembled, both in the church and at the graveside, the Revs. G. B. Thurston (rector), Horace Gray (Warring- ton), and S. E. Clarke (vicar of St. Matthew's, Burnley) Officiating. The service in the church was fully choral, Mr. W. Cooke presiding at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>MOURNERS.</head>
        <p>MOURNERS.</p>
        <p>The principal mourners were: Mr. Moles- worth, Miss Molesworth, Mr. W. N. Molesworth, jun., Mrs. Pleignier, Colonel Healey, Mr. and Mrs. L. Healey and Mrs. Clarke, Mr. and Mrs. J. M. Molesworth, Mr. Alex Molesworth and Mr. A. H. Molesworth; Mrs. Wilde, Miss Kin- sey and maids; Mr. and Mrs. Morris.</p>
        <p>Amongst the others present were: Mr. Alfred Watkin, Mr. Ed. Watkin and Mr. Hugh Wat- kin, Vice-Chancellor Stewart-Smith, Mr. W. Peter Rylands, Dr. and Mrs. H. A. Burrowes, Mr. and Mrs. 11. M. Johnson, Mr, and Mrs. W. M. Beckett, Mr. and Mrs. A. W. Callender, Mr. Geoffrey Callender and Miss Callender, Miss and Miss E. Moorhouse, Mrs. Newmareh, Mr. J. G. Lister-Farrar, Mr. Norman Wood, Mr. A. de C. Parmiter, Mr. J. Palmer, Mr. A. B. Boumphrey, Mr. F. Seddon, Mr. Henry Greenall, Mrs. A. E. Sidebotham, Mr. Sturton, Mr*. P. E. Evans, Mr. Frank Warburton, Mr. Henry Thomason, Mr. Hodgson, Mr. and Mrs. Railton, Mr. Eric Moles- worth, Mr. J. H. Macmillan (Southport), Mr. W. Becker (Chapel-en-le-Frith), Messrs. T. and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>continued.</p>
        <p>R. T. Boyd (Romiley), Mr. Battersby (Brook- lands), and Mr. W. Howarth..._</p>
        <p>THE WREATHS.</p>
        <p>Many beautif iii wreaths were sent by the fol- lowing :Mr. W. N. Molesworth and family, Mr. and Mrs. H. Pleignier, Colonel H. Healey (Rochdale), Mr. and Mrs. Lionel Healey, Mr. and Mrs. J. M. Molesworth, Mr. Alexander Molesworth, Rev. S. E. and Mrs. Clarke and family, Mr. F. N. Molesworth, Mrs. H: B. Moles- worth, Mr. and Mrs. Peacock, Mr. and Mrs. W. Brierley, Mr. and Mrs. H. Gray (Harrow), Mr. and Mrs. Mallows and family, "Agnes, Lily and Beatrice," Mr. and Mrs. N. Wood (War- burton). Mrs. Vaughan and Mrs. Blair, Mrs. Parmiter, Mr. and Mrs. Callender, Mr. and Mrs. Simpson, Mr. and Mrs. Helm, Mr. and Mrs. Palmer, Dr. and Mrs. Burrows, Mr. Adamson, Mr. •H. M. Atkinson, Mr. and Mrs. Watkin and family, Mr. and Mrs. F. A. Frost, Mr. and Mrs. Boum- phrey, Mr. and Mrs. Farrar, Mr. and Mrs. Bat- tersby, Mr. Hodgson, Mrs. Henry and Mrs. Fred Bradburn„ Mr. and Mrs. Ridgway, and Miss Ridgway, Mrs. and Miss Nares, Mrs. De Angelid. Mr. and Mrs. W. Peter Rylands (Thelwall), the Rev. G. B. Thurston, Mrs. Leech, the Misses Murray, Colonel and Mrs. J. E. G. Groves, Mr. and Mrs. Boyd, Mr. and Mrs. Oswald Moseley (Agden Hall), Mrs. Henderson and Miss Adam- son, Mr. and Mrs. Johnson, Mr. and Mrs. F. Peel, Mr. and Mrs. Beckett, Mrs. Newmarch, warehouse staff at Messrs. Bradshaw, Hammond and Co., and the staff and foremen of Reddish Vale Print Works.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>CHAIRMAN ANSWERED.</head>
        <p>CHAIRMAN ANSWERED.</p>
        <p>Councillor Gotobed Makes His Reply.</p>
        <p>COUNCIL CANNOT RAISE WATER RATES. At the meeting of the Lymm District Council on Monday the remarks of the new Chairman (Councillor Warburton), on his election, were responsible for a reply from Councillor Gotobed, who referred to the water charges. Councillor Gotobed said he wished to refer to the remarks which fell from the Chairman immediately after his appointment. He was not going to deal with the remarks by which the Chairman prefaced his speech, he would ignore them, except those upon the water question. He did not wish to say anything provocative, but he wanted to elicit what was meant by the re- marks, and then to make a general statement, so that the public may know what was the posi- tion of the ,Urban District Council. The Chair- man mentioned that certain members of the Council held meetings "all over the place in support of the purchase of the water works." They were faced at that time with a perfectly legitimate and determined opposition, which opposed the policy of the Council, and those of them who favoured the Purchase, believing it was in the best interests of the place, felt it their duty to hold those meetings, without Councillor Warburton's assistance, to try and persuade the ratepayers ,of Lymm that the course they proposed was the wise one. They made certain statements at those meetings to the effect that the water charges would not be raised. He did not quite, know whether Mr. Warburton wished to infer that they had raised the charges, or were guilty of a breach of faith to those who attended the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>ALLOWAN6ES MADE.</head>
        <p>ALLOWAN6ES MADE.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: No, no. He took it that at the time they were not aware that allowances were made in certain cases, and when it was taken over they found that the same thing could net be done.</p>
        <p>Councillor Gotobed : That is not the point at all, for that was not raising the water charges. Those who addressed those meetings did so in perfectly good faith. Councillor Cowper: But you broke it! Councillor Gotobed : I thank you, Mr. Cowper. All we said we meant, and we have not raised the water charges. The facts are these: We have no power to raise the water charges from the statutory ones fixed by the Act which we obtained last year, and to make it perfectly clear, I give the charges, which are: "Dwelling-houses where rateable value did not exceed £5, the maximum charge allowed was 2d. per week; exceeding 025, but not £3O, 7 per cent.; exceeding .230, 6 per cent., with, of course, extra charges in special cases, such as hotels and places of that kind." It had been mentioned by Mr. Warburton that certain cottage rents had been raised. He believed it was so, and they must not blame the Urban District Council for that. He knew what Mr. Warburton meant, that the corn pounding system • was allowed under the old company. Under the new. Act they had no power to compound at all. "The Council a Fraud.!'</p>
        <p>- -----.. - He feared that from the Chairman's words, the unrest which had taken hold of certain in- dividuals would be strengthened. The Council had absolutely no power to consider the com- pounding system. Only a few weeks ago, he was stopped in the street by an individual,' who said, "Your Coun- cil is a fraud." After thanking him for the compliment, he asked the man to explain, and he got a reply to the effect that under the private company this man had had a rebate of 3s. per quarter, which was allowed under the compounding system, which meant that whether a man's cottages were tenanted or not, he paid the full water rate, and received a rebate at the end of the quarter. _</p>
        <p>The present system was that they charged for what was consumed, and if a cottage was empty for a period, the proportion of the rate was re- mitted. He began to make inquiries and found that this gentleman had anticipated the Coun- cil being a fraud by immediately, when the Council took over the water undertaking, rais- ing his cottage rents by 3d. per week. He was prepared to leave it with the public to settle who was the fraud. He only wished to make that statement to allay the suspicion that the Council had done something it ought not to have done.</p>
        <p>" Honestly Dealt With." At the November meeting Councillor Groves and himself were appointed, he believed on a resolution proposed by the late Councillor Gould, to go through the water assessments and regulate them. The reason was that under the Private company the water assessments had not been attended to for some considerable time, in which, interval the assessments had changed considerably. They worked upon the Poor Law basis, and every ease was dealt with honestly and fairly, and brought into line with the poor rate assessment. To say that they 'had raised the water charges was absolutely wrong, be- cause they were not allowed to do so, but they hoped that as the rateable value increased they would be able to lower the rates.</p>
        <p>"Trust Good Judgment." He did not quite think that Mr. Warburton would have any fear in the exercise of his pen, for the speaker thought that the Chairman could trust the good judgment of his fellow councillors, some of whom were larger rate- payers than himself, and wlose sense of honour and integrity was quite as keen as his own. The Chairman said he was pleased the matter had been, brought forward. He had no inten- tion of implying in any shape pr fm that the Council had gone about with any knowledge of raising the water charges. Councillor Gotobed : I accept that. CHAIRMAN'S ARGUMENT. The Chairman added that his idea was that the Council found that they could not grant the allowance, and when they had to pay more for water they could not say the charge_ had dropped. It must have risen. Councillor Critchley said he had a case in point, and they could not get beyond the fact that it had been raised to him. Before the old company was removed he had some cottages in his charge which had not taps to each house, but one tap to a dozen cottages. The company charged him otl 55., and allowed rebate of ss. per quarter; the Council charged ~tl. as. 7d. per quarter, and no allowance. He was over- charged to the amount of 3s. 7d. per quarter. Councillor Groves said that probably the cot- tages were assessed more than 20 years ago, and perhaps from the cottages Mr. Critchley men- tioned Gd. a week more was drawn now than 20</p>
        <p>WHY ACID STOMACFIS ARE DANGEROUS. the SCIENTIFIC REPORTS, April.—Acid stomach is dangerous and frequently fa' because acid from its corrosive nature h •as. natural tendency to destroy the mucous of the stomach 'itself. No one intention!'f, swallows hydrochloric acid, but it is often Prieg; eat in large quantities as the result of f°°d tbe mentation in the stomach resulting from le chemical action, which so frequently followo eating of the daintiest and most tasty, food.s 4 "g, the indulgence in any form of alcoholio st;, lants, including beer, wine, liqueurs, ul fermentation causes pain, uneasiness anre s comfort because of the acid which burns 'tell red-hot iron, and the formation of gas °II of distends the stomach, causing shortne"of breath and palpitation of the heart. D. trouble being, entirely due to the unnatural Oaf dition of stomach contents, and not in all1;:.5 to the fault of the stomach itself, PhYs—bisn have adopted with marvellous success the Pfer, of neutralising the acid and stopping tin of mentation by means of half-a-teasponn'ili;to simple bisurated magnesia taken in 3 er water immediately after eating or wile/3'1;4 discomfort is felt. The immediate relief followrp the use of BISURATED Magnesia is striking of the contention of medical men that fa",igo to digest food properly is almost invariably uie to the acid condition of food contents, Wl'3'). ~ no way the fault of the stomach or vital org</p>
        <p>years ago: He asked Mr. Critchley if he</p>
        <p>7 _ . it would be honest for the water</p>
        <p>remain so low as that if they were getting 6' week more for the cottages. They must eider the whole of the ratepayers and not All the cottages that had been risen in would have to be risen in water.</p>
        <p>FOR RATEPAYERS' SAKE.</p>
        <p>Councillor Critchley said it was Pelle right that he should bring the matter foil; for the sake of the ratepayers. There / other cases as well as his. He maintained that the particular cottages had been assel less than 20 years ago. • Councillor Gotobed : You say you were ell&amp; Li ss. under the old company and .4 38' under the Council. My contention is Y°ll paying is. sd. less than you used to pay. Councillor Holt, in winding up the discus/ said the raising of the charge applied ord: cottage property. He had cottage propel which he used to receive a rebate; now be an allowance for empties. They must acl, the explanation, and he hoped it would go that there was some compensation in the 31 ance for empties.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>MAY QUEEN OR NOT?</head>
        <p>MAY QUEEN OR NOT?</p>
        <p>The Question Hanging in the</p>
        <p>A VITAL MEETING LAST NIGIff• What is to become of Lymm's May Festival? This question is one which ha_,s toted the minds of not a few of the townsr for there seemed everV likelihood festival would have to be abandoned for finall</p>
        <p>reasons. Quaint Lymm would not be the le if the scheme was allowed to go on, for visitors to the village do not come 021 handed—they spend liberally, as folks en sure bent do. It is granted that Lymm is t up-to-date than ever she was, but the deePol hands of the moderniser have as yet done 13 ing to rob the village of the natural backgrc, so much desired for an old-world custom ' as is embodied in the May Queen revels. For several days past a number of lg( alarmed at the situation, have donned their, smiles and taken upon themselves the t5,.5 canvassing, for has it not been stated tJI ..t5O was not forthcoming before the end 0, present week the idea would have to be 11 doned? It is indeed a •miserable sum to cl,e whether or not the children of Lymm are robbed of so pleasurable an event, but su the village possesses at least a few peoPle, love the children and who would not think of helping to pay for the youngsters' enjoY°l Cannot they step forward and prevent the 14 May Queen Festival from being placed up° dusty shelves of the past? A meeting was held last night in the chial Schools, at which the_ resnit of the vassing was made known. A report ; meeting will be found on Page 7.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>SEWAGE WORKS</head>
        <p>THE SEWAGE WORKS</p>
