        <p>11</p>
        <p>SHOWMAN'S ADVENTURE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>" This is the first time our engine has been in Cheshire. We had not been in the county an hour when the police officer stopped us," said a brother to George Ryan, a showman, who was summoned at Runcorn for contravening the regulations affecting heavy motor locomotives; for not having a cord affixed from the engine to the last waggon, as a means of communication; for not having the weight of the engine in a con- spicuous position, and also for not having affixed the weight on one of the waggons. Ryan said he had driven the engine in almost every part of the country, and it was the first time he knew that the weight had to be written on the engine. Ire had a man on the rear wag- gon with a whistle, who signalled any danger. It was impossible to have a cord on the engine on that particular road, because of the sharp turnings. It was not necessary in Lancashire to have a cord affixed on the engine. The Clerk informed the Bench that the cord offence was under the Cheshire County Council bylaws, but the other offences were Local Gov- ernment Board regulations. Fines of 2s. 6d. and 10s. 6(14 costs in each of the three cases were imposed. '</p>
