      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <p>' - _</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAYS MAY 2, 1914. THE DISCOVERY OF THE PACIFIC OCEAN</p>
        <p>The Four Hundredth Anniversary of a Notable</p>
        <p>Event Told for Young People.</p>
        <p>BY ARTHUR</p>
        <p>The completion of the Panama Canal, which cuts through the slender neck of Central America, as well as recent happenings in Mexico, have called our attention to a corner of the world around which a lot of romantic his- tory was made. And last year there slipped by almost unnoticed—altogether unnoticed by many of us—the four hundredth anniversary of the day when, from one of the mountain peaks of the isthmus, the first European saw the waters of the Pacific Ocean. The poet Keats has referred to the stirring moment in the closing lines of one of his sonnets:</p>
        <p>Then felt I like some watcher of the skies When a new planet swims into his ken;</p>
        <p>Or like stout Cortez, when, with eagle eye, Ile stared at the Pacific—and all his men Looked at each other with a wild surmise, Silent, upon a peak in Darien. But Keats was wrong in supposing that Cortez was first to see the Pacific. It was not Cortez, but Vasco Nunez de Balboa, as we must try to tell.</p>
        <p>THE FIRST EXPLORERS OF AMERICA, Following Columbus across the Atlantic several intrepid explorers had added to the world's knowledge of the Western world. Our own John Cabot, of Bristol, had discovered New- foundland and Labrador, and had touched the mainland of America (it should be known) at least a year before Columbus. Amerigo Ves- pucci had touched at Honduras and Florida, and Alonzo de Ojeda had reached the north coast of South America, naming Venezuela (which means Little Venice) because he found there a native• village built upon piles driven into a lagoon. Cabral, a Portuguese, discovered Brazil, and Pinzon in the same year had found the Amazon River. Very puzzling must the ex- perience have been. Pinzon crossed the Equator, losing sight, as he did so, of ihe familiar Pole Star and Great Bear, and losing his bearings along with them; the sea appeared to change from salt to fresh water, due to the water flowing from the great Amazon, and the navigator, with new stars above and fresh water beneath, may well have felt that he had sailed into a new world.</p>
        <p>HOW AMERICA GOT ITS NAME. Special interest attaches to the voyaging of Amerigo Vespucci. Entering the service of the King of Portugal, he sailed down the coast of South America, christening Cape St. Roqut after the Saint's day on which they sighted land, and naming also Rio de Janeiro. From La Plate river he steered south-west to the Ant- arctic, sighting South Georgia, and then hurry- ing back, after many dangers, to the African coast. One thing was made clear by Vespucci's voy- age, that this great land was not part of the Continent of Asia as they had supposed, but that it must be a new portion of the globe; in fact, a New World. This is what Vespucci called it in a letter to a friend, but they still went on believing that what we call North America was part of Asia.</p>
        <p>The land was soon to find its name, and the suggestion came from one Waldseemuller, who wrote a book in 1507. The proposal he made was that seeing that Africa, Europe and Asia had received their names from women, nothing could be better than to christen their new world after a man. And who more worthy than its explorer Amerigo Vespucci. Slowly the sugges- tion took force. South of the Equator was called "America" and north of the Equator was still Asia, but as better knowledge came they called it the Western World, and the name lingered until "America" took its place.</p>
        <p>LOOKING FOR THE WAY TO INDIA. Two things were fixed in the mind of Amerigo Vespucci, as the result of his explorations in the West. One was that there was a way round the South of the New World that led to India, and he made an expedition in 1503 to try to find it. Another firm belief was—and Columbus shared it—that between what we call North and South America there was a strait, which they thought must lead out into the Indian Ocean. Colum- bus tried to find it, and Amerigo intended to make the attempt, but was hindered by mis- fortune. As to what lay upon the other side of the land no one, at the beginning of the year of grace 1513, had the least idea. Then came the sight which brought the "wild surmise" to the amazed beholders "upon a peak in Darien." .</p>
        <p>Ojeda had been made governor of a colony formed at Darien, and to this rude little placc. there came a young adventurer named Munez do Balboa. Ile was a Spanish farmer who had become bankrupt, and who began a new kind of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST —continued.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST —continued.</p>
        <p>of white and damask roses; a lute in one hand, and on the forefinger of the other a nightingale in full song. It was at this period that in Lon- don I delighted in the antics of Jack-in-the- Green more than 50 years ago; and since then in various parts of the country I have witnessed more or less successful efforts—generallY less rather than more successful—to revive the an- cient Maypole festivities. Happily we have still the May Queen with us. The Magic Cheque Book.</p>
        <p>. " How tp use a cheque book" I read last week. I remember a case of a minister who received a gift in the form of a cheque, and hastened with grateful heart to turn it into much-needed cash. It was his first errand of the kind, and he pre- sented it at the bank without having complied with the essential conditions. "Endorse it, please," said the cashier, and handed it back to him. "Oh, certainly, with pleasure," returned the rev, gentleman, and he forthwith wrote on the back, "I heartily endorse this cheque." It is only of parsons and ladies that such stories are told. Says a certain bank manager concern- ing women's ways: The most amusing client : I ever had complained bitterly when she was told she had overdrawn her account. 'why,' she said, 'I have lots of cheques left in my cheque-book. How can I be overdrawn?' One came in with a cheque drawn to her own order and endorsed. She wanted money for her weekly bills. But she had actually 'crossed' it, and when it was pointed out to her that this made it payable only through a banker, she said, 'how funny. I never knew what those scratches meant before.'" Another story is that a woman who had overdrawn her account proceeded to write out a cheque to make up the deficit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>MOUN FIELD.</head>
        <p>MOUN FIELD.</p>
        <p>life as a stowaway in a water cask. Finding himself at Darien, he persuaded the handful of colonists to elect him their leader, and as his first achievement he erected the first church ever built upon the American continent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>AN HISTORIC CONVERSATION.</head>
        <p>AN HISTORIC CONVERSATION.</p>
        <p>Of course, these colonists were not, as modern Yankees would say, "out for their health." They were there in quest of treasure. Gold was extracted from the natives, who did not always understand why the Spaniards should quarrel about the yellow metal. It is said that one of the natives, rather disgusted at their covetousness, exclaimed: "Why fight among yourselv,es over this yel, low stuff? There is enough and to spare in a land of which I can tell you." "Where? Where?" cried the Spaniards, ex- cited at the announcement.</p>
        <p>Pointing to the mountains the chief said: "Beyond them the rivers—full of gold—run into a mighty sea. If you would have gold, go there." "But how can we get 'there, 0 chief?" said Balboa. " Tell us the way." "The way is hard and dangerous; it leads through the lands of fierce chiefs, who will be sure to come out against you. Therefore take a large force and food and arms."</p>
        <p>"Tell me," cried Balboa, "are you sure of all this?"</p>
        <p>" Sure? So sure that I will come and prove it true."</p>
        <p>It is probable that the natives desired to get rid of the troublesome Spaniards, and were sending them upon a dangerous expedition with the hope that they would never see them again. But the predicted dangers could not deter Balboa, and on September Ist, 1513, with 190 men be began the adventure. A pack of blood- hounds did good service in striking terror to the natives, and with only one battle fought he found himself at the foot of the great moun- tains to which the chief had pointed. This great and rugged barrier lay between him anct the Pacific, but no hint had reached him of what lay beyond.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>FIRST SIGHT OF THE PACIFIC.</head>
        <p>THE FIRST SIGHT OF THE PACIFIC.</p>
        <p>Of the 190 men, only 67 had strength left to commence the ascent, and many of these fell out of the party on the upward climb. A poor, weary and blood-stained party, they were bidden to halt, while Balboa climbed alone to the top- most crag. It was almost mid-day, and as he looked eagerly beyond the mountain the sun shone upon an ocean of gold. Could this be the In- dian Ocean, of which they had talked and dreamed? He believed that it was, and began to sing the "To Deum " in acknowledgment of the good fortune a kindly Providence had given to him. ,</p>
        <p>Full of joy, Balboa called his followers, and they joined in his song of praise. Then in a solemn covenant they vowed to follow him even to death in the exploration of the new land and sea that lay at their feet. _ _</p>
        <p>Thus on September 25th, 1513, the greatest of the five oceans was sighted by European eyes. Balboa formally claimed it for Spain, and went forward to the more difficult task of sub- duing it</p>
        <p>They reached the shore on Michaelmas Day, and in honour thereof gave the name Gulf St. Miguel. There were some canoes on the shore, and one of the party, Alonzo Martin, jumped into one of these and called out to his friends to bear witness that he was the first to embark on the new sea.</p>
        <p>Until the month of January and with very good fortune the explorers examined the coast and loaded themselves with treasure, The natives were friendly and all went well. Then, eager to send the news to Spain, they returned to Darien.</p>
        <p>It was great news, indeed, but jealousy and intrigue had been at work. A new governor had been sent from Spain, and an era of bit- terness lay before Balboa. Three years later, upon a false charge of treason, he was executed. It is sad to reflect upon the clouded ending of his life, but they were rough times, and Balboa was probably neither better nor worse than his contemporaries. His courage brought him into unusual honour, and though a man of lesser genius, his name will for ever be associated with those of Cabot, Columbus, Vespucci and Magel- lan as one of the outstanding heroes of dis- covery.</p>
        <p>After exactly four centuries we have cut through the narrow neck of land on which Balboa sang the " Te Deum," and another great and unknown chapter in the history of the Pacific begins.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>ST. HELENS COLLIER'S MEAN THEFT.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS COLLIER'S MEAN THEFT.</p>
        <p>For tampering with the tallies on the tubs of his fellow miners, Robert Twist, miner, 227, Derbyshire Hill-road, was sentenced to three months' hard labour at St. Helens Police Court, the Chairman characterising the offence as a very bad one. The charge was one of having obtained money by false pretences, through altering the tallies, from the Havannah Colliery Compa7.</p>
        <p>_ _ A fellow workman named Robinson said that he concealed himself in the brow early in the morning before the men, went to work and watched them pass. Twist came along by him- self and stopped at some filled boxes near where witness was concealed. He saw him interfering with the tallies, jumped out and caught him by the shoulder, and said "Now, Bob, this is a fair cop. Tha wants drowning. It is not locking up tha wants." Mr. Turner, the under-manager; said that other boxes were found that morning with pris- oner's tally on. them, but it was not his coal.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said it would be very easy for a man to make up the amount of his wages by that practice. He might go down the pit and have a sleep and then by changing tallies make up his wages. It was not only swindling the colliery company, but it was swindling the other work- men.</p>
        <p>The resolution to introduce Sunday play at the Darwen Golf Club was withdrawn at the annual meeting of members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>Literary Gleanings.</head>
        <p>Literary Gleanings.</p>
        <p>MUSTARD AN ANCIENT MEDICINE._</p>
        <p>Mustard is one of the most ancient of medi- cines. Pythagoras, who flourished between five and six hundred years before Christ, mentions it. Ilippocrates, who was born in 460 8.C., em- ployed it. Pliny the Elder writing in 77 A.D., describes three different kinds of mustard, and says the seeds were imported to Italy from Egypt originally. The Romans used it as a stimulant after a cold bath; they mixed mustard oil and olive oil in equal parts and used this as a liniment for stiff- ness of the muscles. They knew the virtues of mustard poultices and of mustard as an emetic. As a remedy for the stings of scorpions and sen pants, they pounded it, mixed it with vinegar and applied it to the wound. They also made a drink out of it, fermenting the seed in a fiery spirit. The liquor thus pro- duced they called mustum ardens, which means burning wine. The word mustard is probably derived from this.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>SEA OF _GALILEE SARDINES.</head>
        <p>SEA OF _GALILEE SARDINES.</p>
        <p>A company is about to be formed for the estab- lishment of a fish-curing and sardine factory on the shores of the Sea of Galilee.</p>
        <p>It appears (says the Jerusalem journal, "The Truth") that for the last year or two great shoals of sardines have appeared in these waters, and it is thought that a remunerative industry could be formed if only a concession could be obtained for a long term of years at a uniform rate. Representations on the subject have al- ready been made to the authorities. The inhabitants of Tiberias, who are extremely poor, would certainly be greatly benefited (adds "The Truth"), as the products would certaiCy be patronised by Jews and Christians alike, for besides possessing a superior flavour, the fact of their coming from the Sea of Galilee would facilitate the disposal of them even in Western countries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>WHITE SLAVE FALLACY.</head>
        <p>A WHITE SLAVE FALLACY.</p>
        <p>It is not possible to say that no woman was ever without her knowledge given a drug hypo- dermically which produced unconsciousness (says the "Journal of the American Medical Association"). It can, however, be said very positively that there is no drug known to scien- tific men which could be administered in the manner or which would produce the effect de- scribed in recent newspaper reports.</p>
        <p>One of the laws of hysteria is that when any peculiar phenomenon is reported similar in- stances immediately appear throughout the country. We may now expect a spring crop of magazine stories and popular novels based on the poisoned needle as a motive. Scientifically the thing is as ridiculous and impossible as hypnotism of an unwilling subject or instan- taneous aniesthesia. Popular beliefs travel in waves and hysteric and excited imaginations help them along. The history of popular delu- sions, from Salem witchcraft to present-day vagaries, is full of such instances.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>GLORY THAT WAS GREECE.</head>
        <p>THE GLORY THAT WAS GREECE.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Kaines Smith has produced a volume (I) that reveals a well-informed mind and a whole- hearted enthusiasm coupled with a sanity of judgment not always found in enthusiasts. 'That these works of art, these artists, are Greek, are ancient, is nothing; what matters is that the creators were once human beings, inspired to deliver to their race a message enshrined in writing, or bronze, or stone, and that their works themselves proclaim their human origin and their intensely human interest. To proclaim the Greeks the greatest artists of all time is cold gospel; they were so, they are still, not because they are so long dead, but because they. were once so intensely alive." This is the spirit in which Mr. Kaines Smith has written. He traces the romantic story of the means by which we have been enabled to recover the right point of view from which to judge the Greek race and its products. As he, with characteristic neat- ness, expresses it: "It was a strange freak of perversity that made the scholars of the last years of the eighteenth century first cut away the ground from which Greek civilisation grew, and then wonder that it should have grown from nothing. For us, however, the history no longer begins with 776 8.C."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>ON THE LEFT OF A THRONE.</head>
        <p>ON THE LEFT OF A THRONE.</p>
        <p>"The Stuarts are not whitewashed," says Mrs. Evan Nepean, and the apology is true enough. You either love the Stuarts, or hats them; it is probably a matter of temperament. Under the magnifying glass of the moral analyst their faults will never bear examination, and yet there hangs a certain glamour about them, as of the last glimpse of romance in British monarchy, which will never fail to appeal to the imagina- tive. And Mrs. Evan Nepean knows the secret of that glamour by inheritance. "My people," she writes:</p>
        <p>"My people—mine. I know your shames and sinnings, I know your joy and pain, The blackest endings to the white beginnings, Ris'n stars that set again. I know your broken faith (and what comes after), The love you deera your due! I know your tears, and oh, I know your laughter, Since I am one of you."</p>
        <p>And accordingly, although the Stuarts may not be whitewashed, she has produced a brisk, enthusiastic, rather exclamatory study of James, Duke of Monmouth, which certainly stops short this side idolatry, but is nevertheless as near to an exercise in hero worship as any self-respect- ing historian ought to go. ' A man of follies, steeped in indiscretion, Mon- mouth was nevertheless the darling of his gener- ation. Whether Charles 11. ever married Lucy Walter, and whether Monmouth was not after all a legitimate heir to the throne of England, are still debatable questions. Mrs. Nepean decides against the marriage, but there are potent rumours in its favour still. At any rate, whether an Isaac or an Ishmael, Monmouth had true Stuart blood in his veins, and his grand- mother, Henrietta Maria, loved him only a little less dearly than his own mother did. Their womanity devotion in the nursery was soon succeeded by a world of womanly devotion in the salon, for Monmouth, from his yonth was the favourite of the other sex. A gallant boy, as Feffs says, " skittish and leaping," he carried vitality and charm into every side of life. There were hidings in cabinets and escapes (like Falstaff's) in a basket, and the spirit of wayward youth went rioting down the White- hall of many Stuart memories. These things cannot be condoned any more than rebellion and civil war can be condoned; and yet Monmouth carried them all off with such an air that their squalor seems to vanish in the restrospect. "It is of no use blaming these people," says our author; for one thing, they were the creatures of their generation, and for another they were as brave as they were reckless. And without their hot blood and superabundant energy they could hardly have endured the hardships of their adventurous careers. Monmouth's death, at any rate, makes a splen- did tableau. He had loved life well, and fought hard for its hazards; but, when it ,!am a to an end, he parted with it without a backward glance. After all, they had the secret of the grand manner—these Stuarts who may not be white- washed. They played the part of royalty with a royal prodigality; they were born to the purple of Tragedy and of Fate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
