      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <p>- - -</p>
        <p>TABLE TALK.</p>
        <p>The World's Tribute of Interesting Items of News.</p>
        <p>QUAINT AND AMUSING.</p>
        <p>A wind motor claimed to be the largest of its kind in the world has lately been brought into use at Harlingen, Friesland, for draining a tract of lowland redeemed from the seas by high em- bankments, with an area of 1,850 acres. This machine, which has steel sails and is mounted on a steel tower, has a diameter of fifty feet, and is said to be capable of dealing with 64,000 to 70,000 cubic feet of water an hour.</p>
        <p>Dirigible Operated by Wireless. A new type of dirigible controlled and pro- pelled by wireless waves has been tested at Genoa, Italy. IQ v —3' transforming the intermit- tent impulses of the wireless into a continuous current the inventor has been able to operate therf variousthe Propellers at will. The transform- ing device is contained within the basket or e dirigible and also operates the o bomb- dropping device by which the dirigible is de- signed to aid the army and navy in case of war.</p>
        <p>The Dead Sea a Health Resort. Sir John Gray Hill, a Palestine explorer, be- lieves that the curative value of the waters of the Dead Sea will some day make its shores a Place of resort in the winter months for cura- tive Purposes. "I have found the use of the Dead Sea water most invigorating," he says. "I get a supply at my house brought in old petroleum tins on donkey-back, and use it some- some time before the invalid tide sets that way, bwuhtatthdeilut other attractions of the country should aid its popularity.</p>
        <p>Uses of a Moustache. Dr. Paul Klager, the famous Viennese physi- cian, declares that the fashion of shaving the moustache is responsible for the growing num- ber of patients suffering from headaches and diseases of the respiratory organs. Among 500 cases of serious headaches and diseases of the nose and throat he had counted 420 patients who had their moustaches shaved off. The moustache is, of course, a natural protection for the nose against the invasion of cold air, dust, and microbes.</p>
        <p>Coast Defence by Rail. An entire coast defence battery mounted on railway trucks is the answer of French mili- tary authorities to the question of how to secure positive defence over long coast lines with economy. The advantages claimed for these mobile batteries are the economy in the pre- paration for the defence of any place, as the batteries on wheels cost less than permanent fixed batteries and can be sent to threatened points from places where they are not needed, the only expense being for laying the rails.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>Horses Stabled in Ice in Greenland.</head>
        <p>Horses Stabled in Ice in Greenland.</p>
        <p>- A Danish explorer who has just returned from a journey across the inland ice of Greenland made use of Icelandic ponies, which he found to be well adapted to Arctic travel. When stops were made the horses were sheltered in holes cut in the ice. These icy stalls were quite com- fortable, and in severe weather were covered with tarpaulins to keep out snow and wind. The small horses gave a good account of them- selves, and will doubtless be used extensively in future Arctic expeditions. "Popular Mechanics' Magazine."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>What Colour is a Red-Hot Poker?</head>
        <p>What Colour is a Red-Hot Poker?</p>
        <p>Whenever the process of burning is in pro- gress anywhere it is always accompanied by the production of a certain amount of heat, and usually a certain amount of light also. Burn- ing, the "Children's Magazine" reminds us, is the union of some inflammable substance with a gas which allows of combustion. Any chemical substance, if its energy is sufficient, may pro- duce combustion, and whether the burned sub- stance becomes luminous or not depends on the degree of heat and the composition of the sub- stance burned. So that it is the energy of the chemical and physical action which colours the poker red, influenced by the matter of which the poker is made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>Paper Made While You Wait.</head>
        <p>Paper Made While You Wait.</p>
        <p>, . ..._ A novel test of speed was recently made by the directors of a paper mill at Esebthal, Ger- many, according to "Ideal Power." At 7.35 a.m. three trees were cut down in a grove near the mill. The wood was sawed, barked, ground, screened and washed, mixed in the beater and flowed on to the weir, and at 9.34 paper was being reeled on the winder. A waiting auto- mobile carried this to a newspaper office two- and-a-half miles away. The roll was fed to the press which had been made ready, and the first newsboy rushed from the office with the printed sheets at 11 o'clock sharp, just 3 hours and 25 minutes after the trees were felled.</p>
        <p>Marriage Law Curiosities.</p>
        <p>In West Virginia a man may marry his de- ceased marry herwife's t e sister,pda u but is forbidden by law to hter.</p>
        <p>- In Massachusetts a man may marry his de- ceased wife's sister, but is prohibited from wed- ding her grandmother. In Maryland whites and negroes or mulattoes who intermarry are "deemed guilty of an in- famous crime," and are subject to ten years' imprisonment. But if they go across the border, into Pennsylvania, and get married there and returz\ to Maryland they are not breaking the law.</p>
        <p>New Metallic Building material.</p>
        <p>The French inventors of a new system of metallic wall covering claim quite a superiority over plaster or wood, and cheapness as well. On the laths are mounted light stamped sheet steel plates of handsome decorative design, made in a variety of standard sizes from 2 feet square up to 6 feet by 2 feet and the like. Ornamental cornices are also used, so that there is no diffi- culty in giving a metallic covering to ceiling and walls, and the effect is most handsome. The advantages of metal are numerous, for humidity does not penetrate, and good hygiene is secured from absence of shelter for microbes and.insects. The thickness is not over 0.0012 inch and the weight 41b. per squaye yard. Matched joining of plates gives a uniform surface whose joints the eye cannot detect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>To Commemorate a Miracle.</head>
        <p>To Commemorate a Miracle.</p>
        <p>_ In granite and bronze a memorial shaft has Who fled across the ftwoyund a colony in Y the descend- ab neetns Greatorf aAti imsteeed 11‘. ificonarni nS aopli e sLe ra pioneerstoCi providen- a moment ttihael appearance wilderness, ocfo ma mfl oecniko or af ti nugll gulls the hate when a plague of crickets had threatened the very existence of the infant colony. The monu- ment consists of a slender shaft rising from gulls, with out- theth e monument opedestalu surmountedme ntand portray b ycrownedtheb a huge granite ball,dramatic rescue bronzeof stretched wings. Suitable tablets on the base of the pioneers from famine, and a fountain, 40ft. in diameter, surrounds the base. A simple tablet bears the legend: "Erected in Grateful Remembrance to the Mercy of God to the Mor- nalm Pioneers!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>84-YEAR-OLD SUITOR'S DILEMMA.</head>
        <p>84-YEAR-OLD SUITOR'S DILEMMA.</p>
        <p>Mr. Aaron Rodgers, aged eighty-four, of Mat- lock, who applied to the guardians to find him a third wife, interviewed a prospective bride at Bakewell. lie requires a partner who is an old- age pensioner. "We didn't agree very well," Ile stated on his return, "and it may fall through." lie confessed he was not enamoured of the looks of any woman. "None of these old ones are good looking," he remarked, "but, then, younger ones haven't the old-age pen- sion."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <p>My Dear Children,— I suppose you were all delighted to see that the representatives from your schools won such a creditable victory on the football field on Saturday. It is very pleasant to think War- rington could turn out such a fine body of foot- ballers. They have had a good time since, have not they?—gold watches, a fine dinner from the Mayor, and a visit to the pictures. I am sure you join me in sending our congratula- tions to these clever schoolboy footballers. Now, as I know you are all keen to be out at nights, I shall not trouble you with a long letter, but I hope you will all read the selections given below.</p>
        <p>Your loving</p>
        <p>UNCLE JACK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>CONUNDRUMS.</head>
        <p>CONUNDRUMS.</p>
        <p>When does a donkey resemble an unpaid credi- tor?—When he gets nothing in the pound. Why is a good husband like dough ?—Because a woman needs him.</p>
        <p>_ What man is always over head and ears in debt?—The man who wears a wig which isn't paid for.</p>
        <p>Why is it easy to break into an old man's house P—Because his gait is broken and his locks are few.</p>
        <p>Why is a whisper like a forged banknote? Because it is uttered, but not aloud. What coin. is doubled in value by deducting its "half "P —Halfpenny.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>FOREST.</head>
        <p>THE FOREST.</p>
        <p>I wandered through the forest In the early morning time; The dew was on the buttercups, The harebells seemed to chime, And all the great oaks seemed to say "Good morn," as I passed by; And, as I watched, the morning star Died in the brightening sky.</p>
        <p>I wandered through the forest When the sun was at his noon. The flowers were sighing softly. As they whispered "Moisture soon." The birds had all retreated 'Neath the cool shade of the trees, And nothing broke the stillness Save the humming of the bees.</p>
        <p>I wandered through the forest When the evening sun was low; The lark was singing ere she dropt Down to her nest below; The flowers had folded up their leaves, The sky was tinged with red; The sun sank slowly o'er the hills, Down to his heath'ry bed.</p>
        <p>I wandered through the forest, When the moon was at her height, And through the open spaces poured Soft beams of silvery light. The stars were twinkling up above, And in the distance clear The sweet notes of the nightingale Trilled softly to his dear.</p>
        <p>A DAY WITH THE WILY KANGAROO. One Christmas I spent my holidays at a station near the river Murray, in North Vic- toria, Australia. It was a very wild country, of about a hundred thousand acres. Miles of it were covered with scrub and young timber, gidya, pine, mulga, and belar; and, over it all, kangaroos swarmed. There were "old men" that stood six feet high, vicious old chaps who, when we chased them, would hop along for about a mile, and then turn at bay. It took a bit of manceuvring to kill them safely. Then there were blue kangaroos, smaller, but much faster. The blue kangaroo is the fastest animal in Australia. It clears all small obstacles in its stride, and dodges the big trees very cleverly. There were also thousands of small rusty-red kangaroos. These were the greatest pest of the lot. Each one eats about as much grass as a sheep; and when there are twenty thousand or thirty .thousand on your station, .you soon observe the effect. There were also thousands of wallabies, a small variety of kan- garoo.</p>
        <p>- While I was at the station, the squatter and his manager had several earnest talks about abating the kangaroo nuisance. At last they decided to yard some of them. Kangaroos are splendid jumpers, but if left alone they rarely jump. But set a dog after one of them, or give him a bad fright, and he will clear a seven- foot fence. We were going to drive the animals, and, of course, that would frighten them greatly, so we had to be prepared for the worst.</p>
        <p>The wings of the yard or corral were easily made, for the kangaroo, if he is not stopped in front, will edge along a very low wing. We built one wing by hanging branches of scrub along the top of a five-foot wire fence for about two miles. It served admirably. In a couple of places the wind made gaps, and through these a few kangaroos escaped. There was no eastern wing; that side was guarded by men on horse- back. The yard itself, built by the station carpenter, was a work of art. It was made of ten-foot poles, sunk into the ground, and laced with saplings (about twenty feet long and from three to six inches thick). About two feet above the top of the yard was stretched a tight, almost invisible, wire. Soon after breakfast, the whole party, some thirty strong, set out for the drive. We were all mounted on fast and sure stock horses, and we each had a stock whip, with a lash about twenty feet long. The line was stretched right across the pad- dock, about three miles in length. The paddock was full of trees and scrub, and we were so far apart that it was rare for one beater to catch sight of another. Al! that you could hear was the occasional cracking of a stock whip. As we rode on, we saw plenty of kangaroos dashing pell-mell away from us. I was on the left side, often within sight of the wing, and I saw plenty of kangaroos running down beside the fence. They could easily have jumped it, but did not do so. At last I saw a blue one come right across my front, and take the fence like a bird. Now and then we came to a bit of open coun- try, across which scores of kangaroos were sweep- ing. At last we sighted the yard. The kangaroos were a bit suspicious about entering the yard, but a few whip cracks sent them flying into it. One blue one dashed back, and the boss said, "Frank, give her a go for it';; so I set after her. But she was far too fleet. I had to pick my way, while she seemed to go straight through , everything. I gave up the chase, and came back to the yard. There was a scene of great excite- ment and confusion. Nearly all the catch were of the red sort. They were very wild, and made frantic jumps at the fence. A few of them reached the top, but the carpenter's wire bumped them back every time. I was watching the scene with great interest, when I noticed a blue kangaroo, frantic with terror, make a tremendous leap for freedom. It was successful. She hit the wire, but fell over it, and dropped with a thud on the other side I ran round, thinking she must have broken her neck; but she was off at full speed into the bush. We killed the lot before lunch, and found w had yarded eight hundred, which my uncle reckoned would be a great relief to his grass.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <p>AMATEUR PHOTOGRAPHY.</p>
        <p>Warringlonians Pay a. Visit to the</p>
        <p>Weaver Valley.</p>
        <p>BY SILVER-STREET.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>KODAKS.</head>
        <p>KODAKS.</p>
        <p>Last week I referred to the Eastman Kodak Company, one of my statements being that "the action between them and the Ansco Company had been settled by the payment of a substantial sum of money." One would have thought that the payment of a sum reaching to millions of dollars would make a secretary squirm. Not .a bit of it. Here is an extract from the last report of the Kodak Co.: "This sum has been charged against reserves, so that neither the surplus accounts nor future profits will be affected." Another extract will explain the unconcern of the company: "The net profits for 1913, after making provision for depreciation on buildings, plant and machinery, amount to £2,920,090, as against £2,886,401 for the previous twelve months." Compare this with the profits for 1895; they amounted to ..t50,000.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON PHOTOGRAPHIC SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Ten oembers joined the leader, Mr. G. H. Walker, on Saturday, in an outing to the Weaver Valley. The 1.17 p.m. was patronised as far as Preston Brook, whence a walk of two miles, mostly along field footpaths, brought them to the brink of the valley. This point gives, in Mr. Walker's opinion, and we may say in that of most of his companions, the very finest pros- pect over the river and its surroundings. The trees were generally just putting forth their earliest leaves, but the Blackthorn and the Wild Cherry trees, as usual in a great hurry, were already in full blossom. The woods are car- peted with Anemones, and King-cups and Prim- roses are beautiful in their first joyous opening. I may say that on the 16th of April I was one of a party of seven who walked from Halton Station to Pickering Bridge by the river bank, and we found 33 species of wild flowers open.</p>
        <p>To return to our photographers—after -ninny plates had been used they found tea at Mrs. Turner's, Pickering Bridge, and, crossing the bridge, a pleasant evening walk ended at Acton Bridge Station.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>SNAPSHOTS.</head>
        <p>SNAPSHOTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. A. Ormerod, a leading Blackburn cot to manufacturer, and Liberal worker, has bee] nominated for the position of president of ell local Free Church Council in succession to tb, W. Harrop.</p>
        <p>The parishioners of Walmersley, near Bury, have presented the Rev. T. IL Florence with, a china cabinet and a silver lamp and vases on his preferment to the living of St. Thomas's. Garetang, and on the occasion of his marriage.</p>
        <p>Which is the best printing frame for general use? asks a correspondent. I always use the Jaynay, and always a half-plate. Of course, I am assuming you are an ordinary modern amateur, and indulge only in small size nega- tives. In my opinion the only size possible for small workers is a half-plate printing frame. They allow the paper or post card to be twisted until it is straight and square with the picture. They are exceedingly convenient for post card printing, and for printing lantern slides they are a sine qua non. Therefore, get a half-plats- A hint taken from "Photography and Focus" may be worth inserting here :—Printing frames that have not been used for some time should be examined for warping before entrusting a valu- able negative to them. A very slight bend in the frame may cause the negative to crack, not necessarily when the frame is being loaded, but at some time during the printing. When the plate is put into the frame, its corners should be touched in succession to make sure it does not wobble. If it does, there is risk of break- age. A warped frame is best broken up or burnt forthwith, so that its career of destruction may be closed at once.</p>
        <p>Our congratulations to Mr. Harry Boscow, whose print received "Hon." mention on April 13th, in the A.B. Competition.</p>
        <p>ENSIGN FILM COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Powell Ayles, 5, Baycliff-terrace, Wey- mouth, Dorset, is the winner of the "Ensign" , Roll Film Competition for April. Messrs. Hough.. tons, Limited, offer a four-guinea camera every month for the best negative on "Ensign" film. Entrance is free. The competition closes on the 3rd of the month, and an entry form is enclosed with every spool of "Ensign" film. TONING BROMIDE PAPER.</p>
        <p>A difficulty may at times be experienced in getting some particular make of paper to tone satisfactorily to a sepia colour. The following bleaching bath gave me a very good rich sepia colour with a paper that quite refused to tone at all with either the iodide or ferricyanide- bromide bleacher :</p>
        <p>Bichromate of Potash 45 grs. Common salt *l4O grs. Sulphuric acid lOO minims. Water 5 ozs. • -</p>
        <p>" British Journal of Photography." CUTTING BOTTLES. A method of cutting the top off a bottle with- out a diamond, as given in the " Deutsche Photographen-Zeitung," is the following:— Narrow strips of moist blotting paper are pasted round the bottle, leaving between them a narrow space which occupies the line of the desired cut. The bottle is then held over the flame of a spirit lamp and turned slowly round, so that the bare strip may be heated evenly; and after about a minute the glass will separate. If the heating has been done uniformly, states our contem- porary, the break will be quite clean, and will only need the sharp edvs taken off with a file.— "Photography and Focus."</p>
        <p>Academy,</p>
        <p>111 na le te</p>
