        <head>saace</head>
        <p>saace</p>
        <p>on your plate - H.P. is thick, fruity, and delicious can be taken with the meat just like mustard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <head>MOTOR FASHIONS.</head>
        <p>MOTOR FASHIONS.</p>
        <p>The advent of the motor has brought about many changes in the domestic life of this and other countries; it has revolutionised travel and requisitioned the addition of new departments to the big stores,and shops, where the numerous and varied requirements of motorists are specially dealt with. But most of all, the motor . has influenced feminine fashions, and in noth- ing, perhaps, so much as in the case of furs. Many skins of fur-bearing animals have of late years been added to the list of pelts worn by motorists.' The latest of these come from the denizens of the jungle and tropical forests. A hundred years ago, had a woman dared to appear in public apparelled in a long coat made from the tawny hide of .the tiger, she would have been mobbed. To-day, not only the tiger, but the leopard and the Cyprus cat, have been commandeered by furriers, to provide coats for women, and that without exciting the smallest surprise or astonishment. On the contrary, un- stinted admiration has been called forth, for these pelts, which, subjected to the present day Processes of treatment, are exceedingly hand- some and desirable, and few women can resist a longing to possess a motor coat of one or other of Ahem. Their only drawback is their costli- ness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>WORK-A-DAY WONDERS.</head>
        <p>WORK-A-DAY WONDERS.</p>
        <p>111.-TELLS HOW COALS OF FIRE WERE HEAPED ON THE HEAD OF A CRITICAL</p>
        <p>NEI€UIBOUR.</p>
        <p>"You would have thought," said Patty, "that after I had made such a success Diamond-dye- ing my serge skirt and Mrs. Finch's silk blouse, I shouldn't have been nervous when it came to helping Mrs. Bridges dye a long straight piece of faded linen. "But you TRY it yourself—specially if you've got a great big loud-voiced woman fussing around, and hoping you aren't going to spoil her , stuff like you spoiled some of your own clothes, when experimenting with one-packet-for-every- thing dyes. " We're on sure ground with Diamond Dyes," I said to her, "wool dye for woollens, cotton dye for cottons and linens—no hazard whatso- ever. And if you had read the Diamond Dye Direction Book the chemist gave you when you bought these two threepenny packets of dye you would have known better than to give way to such fidgets. "I worked on with the dyeing all the time I was talking, and, by-and-bye, there was the linen, one solid beautiful stretch of colour hang- ing out on the line in Mrs. Bridges' garden. It, had been a faded greeny-grey before, and we had dyed it Sevres blue and—well! When Mrs. Hendry, who lives next door to Mrs. Bridges, saw the linen made up, she fairly begged me to give her a hand dyeing some heliotrope feathers and sky-blue sash-ribbon to an even shade of elephant grey. I was a bit doubtful about trying FEATHERS; but it was Mrs. Bridges herself who declared that if anybody or anything could manage them I and Diamond Dye could." (To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0251" n="art0251">
        <head>MAKE YOUR OLD CLOTHES LOOK NEW,</head>
        <p>MAKE YOUR OLD CLOTHES LOOK NEW,</p>
        <p>In c Colours 3d. &amp; 6d. packets. Direction Book Free trout Diamond Dyes, 86 Clerkenwell Road, London.</p>
        <p>LAST LETTER ON COLLAR FRONT.</p>
        <p>It was stated at an inquest at Tamworth on Samuel Cooper, a labourer, who was found hang- ing from a plum tree in an orchard, that he cut off his collar front and wrote on it a letter to his wife, with a postscript saying that the rope had broken twice, but he hoped the third time would be successful.</p>
        <p>Five eggs have been found in a robin's nest built in the clothing of a scarecrow at Hemel Hempstead, Hertfordshire.</p>
