        <p>My friend Elliot Ellis was a young artist upon whom Fortune may be considered to have smiled broadly, since, besides much success in his chosen art, he had become en- gaged to a very charming in whose beauty, birth and wealth were all that could he desired, and more than could have been expected by even so rapidly rising a man. Hence I was surprised one October afternoon when he entered my study looking haggard and dejected. It soon appeared, however, that he was worrying himself about an ill- ness, a sort of comatose brain-fever which he had had in the preceding year upon his re- turn from an autumn sketching tour. His recovery had been swift and complete, but, as is quite common in such cases the attack had superinduced total forgetfulness of all that had occurred for some time before its coming on He knew from external evidence that he had spent several weeks in farmhouse lodgings near a little Welsh village; but for his own internal consciousness that frag- ment of space and time had been annihiLat- ed. a fact which now seemed, for no sufficient reason discoverable from his discourse, to be weighing heavily on his mind. " Well, if that's all," r said at last, "I really can't see how you should keep on both- ering your head about it. Practically it's of no more importance than that you should remember what you had for dinner this day week. "esidea—if it's any comfort to you—.. quite your memory may suddenly relitetiurn: that sometimes occurs in such cases: and you'll find yourself recalling everything you can wish—more so maybe: kaoswelbeaorrley as d you were on wet days, and what atrocious stuff your landlady's tea was., and all that—" When you seek to administer oonsolation, it is decidedly mortifying if your patient bounces up with a suppressed friend Ellis did this,.uisntteirt7ptiknweg exclaimed- wildly, harangue. "That's j en "that's exactly holwiatnids. it's am onbegh- ginning to remember it al_ to drive one mad ! " "Well, well, man, don't rave premature- ly'" I said, " sit down quietly, and ll anything you have to tell." He obeyed, regaining with a perceptible effort a composed demeanour. .. you know, Stewart,— he began, ``it's about a month since I went down to Dorset' shire. On the day before I started, I was getting my trap Welsh s together, and among a lot s of things I had had with me on that used expedition last autumn, and had never ses since. I found a lie:osh overcoat, MILL; I</p>
