      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <p>wanted. I was just folding it up, when I felt something hard stinking in the lining ot the right-hand cuff, and saw that the' cloth had been torn in a long slit close to the wrist, and from between the cloth and the lining I poked out—this." " This ' was a very beautiful sapphire and diamond ring, which he took from his waist- coat pocket and handed to me. " I had never seen it before," Ellis con- tinued, "and was at a loss to imagine how it got there. However I set off next day, and didn't think much about it for some lit- tle time. But in the meanwhile another queer thing happened. I began to recollect bits about that confound. i little Welsh place. Llandolwyn. An odd, eerie sort of experience it is. Shreds and scraps of things heard and seen keep drifting into one's mind, appearing and vanishing and re-appearing like a range of mountain-peaks in a mist. All manner of trifles—snatches of talk about the weather or the crops—a black oak cup- board with a stuffed cock on the top of it—a girl singing somewhere and clinking crockery and an old waman's voice objurgating her in an unknown tongue—these emerge every now and then very clearly. But if I try to make anything coherent out of them, it's like mending a torn-up sketch when you've lost all the important pieces. So far I re- member only one part of it by any means consecutively, and even that is fragmentary enough. Yet I think, I fear—l can't be mistaken." .</p>
        <p>smtsll herolrolfe'pahPerws7 with him, and now he laid one of them on whhaisethily tuhehrandin gbroovuegr hat the table before me saying: " There—it's all in some wc‘y connected with that." malftu wfeaasturae rwouhtwlavatleark-cesololuiurkesdketth, together cgethietrs by a short stream. One was encircled by steep wooded slopes like the rim of a round green nest, the other lay, a long oval, be- tween lower and barer hills. I'he sketch had evidently been taken from a height, and was in a very unfinished state. " Before I began to remember," Ellis said, " that little daub used often to puzzle me. I was continually trying to remember some- thing about it, and I've succeeded now, with a vengeance. I'll just tell you what at present seem to me clear matters of fact. I much admired these twin Llandolwyn lakes, anl wished to sketch them, and, through the intervention, I think, of my landlady Mrs. Price, I got access to a place in the neigh- bourhood, which I was certain must com- mand exactly the view I wanted. I can't re- member its name, but I know well the look of the house and grounds. "So I went up there one day, in the after- noon, and found that from the south front of the house, which stands high, I could get a splendid point of view, showing the beauti- ful rose-leaf curve of the inner lake—this thing's in too great a mess to give any idea of it. A tiled verandah with ivy-grown pil- lars ran along that side of the house, and a flight of stone steps led down into a steep E!lro!'llery with wlnding walks through it,</p>
