      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>•••••••1••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••:••••••••• •••••••••••••••••••• •••••••••••••••••••••••••••••• • ••••••••••••• 44. • • • • – •:•• • • .).:* •• • • • • •:••:* 4.4. • • •• • • 4." • • .:,,,</head>
        <p>•••••••1••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••:••••••••• •••••••••••••••••••• •••••••••••••••••••••••••••••• • ••••••••••••• 44. • • • • - •:•• • • .).:* •• • • • • •:••:* 4.4. • • •• • • 4." • • .:,,,</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S WORLD.</p>
        <p>whom hostesses could arrange to entertain par- ties of guests on stated occasions, providing them with tea, and possibly facilities for out- door amusements. Here, at least, is one way in which wealthy householders might respond to the Bishop of London's appeal. There are, of course, some people who can say with Milton,. "my spirit .hath dwelt much alone," and not complain, but this is not the case with the vast</p>
        <p>majority who ,are forced by circumstances into furnished-room life. The story of the solitude of those who struggle alone in a 'great city has been told ;many a time, yet how few realise the wretched loneliness of such people, with no amusements, no change, no society, and who know no home other than a cheerless small bed- room. Engineering for Women. •</p>
        <p>Engineering seems at first glance a very un- likely profession for a woman to follow, but it has for some time provided re career for hun- dreds of Russian women, the greater number of whom are chili qualified. At the present moment there are quite as many women as men students at the Higher Technical College in St. Petersburg. America, of course, has long been to the fore in this, as in other respects, where Masculine professions are concerned, and some years ago a lady, after being made a member of the New York City staff of Civil Engineers, was given the charge of a water supply system. Italy also has awarded diplomas to qualified women engineers and several other countries have followed this example. Britain has lagged behind somewhat, but Miss Griff, who is ex- hibiting at the Women's Welfare Exhibition, has removed the reproach of backwardness in going forward in this respect from her country women. As a child she took unusual interest in mechanics. On leaving school she obtained certificates for no fewer than three branches of mechanical engineering. She has gone through precisely the same training as a male student, which included a four years' apprenticeship. To that she has added seven years' experience in mechanical work of all kinds, besides some time</p>
        <p>spent in heavy commercial motor works, and has since set up in business for herself:</p>
        <p>The New Wide Skirt.</p>
        <p>The flounced skirt is a fait accompli, and will be very much in evidence this summer in silk and lace, and now come the tidings that a new model has been launched by a noted Parisian house that measures at least five yards round</p>
        <p>the feet. This skirt fits tightly round the hips, has no draperies, but flares out from the knees after the fashions of the last century. It would appear that the tight skirt is doomed to oblivion, but numbers of women will hesitate to adopt a fashion that interferes with their pro- gression and movement, quite as much as the tight skirt, but is lacking in lightness, which is the latter's chief recommendation. Another rumour is to the effect that the/shawl is to be revived. The art of wearing 'and 'folding it, however, is a forgotten one, and who will teach us how,to wear it effectively?</p>
        <p>Black Satin with Light Dresses. A very pretty substitute for the belt is the flower girdle, which young girls are wearing with white dance frocks. The cottage and hedgerow flowers are often used massed tightly .in bunches, and intermixed with graceful trail- ing foliage, always small. Quite a distinctive note is given to a light walking dress by means of a black satin broad band at the waist, and collar and cuffs of the same. In a few instances a band of black satin is placed at the hem of the skirt. For hats one of the prettiest trim- mings is.the pom-pom, which has been lately revived' in ostrich feathers. Possibly the silk variety may be again used later on. The long ostrich feather has still a vogue, and is seen on many of the new 4ts. The flue-brush variety and upstanding mounts of ridiculous height are as much used as during the winter. Veils are sometimes put on below the eyes; which are un- covered, but as th mode is unbecoming it does not stand much chance of being generally adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Queen Alexandra Day. The Alexandra Day celebration is to take place on Wednesday, 24th June, and it will be on a much larger scale than on the two pre- vious occasions. There will be sub-committees in 300 centres throughout the United Kingdom, compared with 72 last year; and the money col- lected in these provincial areas is to be devoted to local charities. London had about 15,000 rose sellers last year, but it is expected that they will number fully 20,000 on this occasion, and it is probable that in Manchester and district they will number nearly half as many. Queen Alex- andra has expressed the desire that the girl rose sellers should co-operate in pairs, and that none should be under 16 years of age. All are to be dressed in white, with light-coloured hats trimmed with wreaths of the emblem of the day, and it is hoped to sell no fewer than five millions of the little wild roses. "Rose Day" secures a doubly charitable result, for it not only raises large sums for useful public insti- tutions, but also provides work for the cripples and blind inmates of the Crippleage in London. Garden Parties for Factory Workers.</p>
        <p>The Bishop of London lately referred to the social isolation of people who work and live alone in our great cities, and appealed to the well-to-do to help in some practical way to lighten the weight of solitude that oppresses them. Such an opportunity is always open to people with country houses on the outskirts of manufacturing cities, who, with little expense and trouble, could entertain small parties of factory workers or others, and show them the delights of country amenities in the summer time. In every city there are organisations with</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>MEMS. FOR MOTHERS.</head>
        <p>MEMS. FOR MOTHERS.</p>
        <p>The Care of the Baby.</p>
        <p>A baby should have every opportunity for exercise. Many are so loaded with clothing and constricting bands that they are hampered in their movements. Formal gymnastic exercises, managed by the parent, have been advised for bodily development; but the spontaneous II:1070. ments of the child are quite sufficient. The child needs no rocking or singing to put it to sleep, though there is little reason to suppose that it is any the worse for this entertainment. The mother might have more time for other things if sleep were more spontaneous. The child is apt to become tyrannous in this as in oilier matters which afford it pleasure. A child should never be urged to walk or to exercise in other ways. Bodily movements come spontan- eously with the development of the organs con- cerned.</p>
        <p>The child should never be unduly excited to play. Play is the result, the outward manifesta- tion, of superfluous nervous and muscular energy. If it does not play of its own accord it is an indication of the need of rest or of better nourishment.—James Frederick Rogers, M.D., in "Good Health."</p>
        <p>Dull Day Amusements. Little people soon tire of doing one thing, so one needs to have a great many ways of keeping them occupied. A few of those I have tried are as follows: GI-t an advertisement-book, out of which they can cut ladies and gentlemen, chil- dren, animals and flowers, and then paste them into an old• copy-book. Afterwards colour them as they think fit. All nice pictures should be kept for scrap books, screens, and various articles which the elder ones will like to do. Animals may be laid on cardboard and pencilled round, cut out, and made to stand with a small piece of cardboard at the back, pasted, too. These can also be coloured.</p>
        <p>Little girls will enjoy laying paper animals on cardboard and piercing round it with a sharp tool, afterwards working the outline in silk, also flowers. They will often make them into small photo-frames, little mats, toilet tidies if a little care is taken with the piercing out, in card- board. The piercing may be done on cloth, too, and a simple cross-stitch worked all over in different-coloured silk. These can be formed into lots of useful articles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>COOKERY CORNER.</head>
        <p>COOKERY CORNER.</p>
        <p>Some Cheap and ,Dainly Recipes.</p>
        <p>Here is a recipe for a cheap and most nutri- tious boiled rice and raisin pudding: Wash half a pound of rice, stone one pound of raisins, and mix them well together, tie in a scalded and floured cloth, leaving room for it to swell; boil for two hours. Serve the pudding with a nice sweet sauce. Potato Cheese Cakes.</p>
        <p>If you have any cold potatoes left do try con- verting them into cheese cakes. You will like them, everyone does. Beat six ounces of cooked potatoes until light and smooth. Melt two ounces of butter, and add it to the potatoes ieith four ounces of castor sugar, four ounces of cur- rants, one ounce of candied peel, the juice (and rind, if liked) of one lemon, and one egg. Line some patty-pans with pastry, put a teaspoonful of the mixture into each, and bake in a rather quick oven for about twenty minutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>Good Ice Cream.</head>
        <p>Good Ice Cream.</p>
        <p>Take a cupful of milk, and in it beat the yolk of an egg; add a spoonful of cornflour, sugar to taste, and flavouring of lemon or almonds. Boil till it thickens into a kind of custard; thin it down with milk, till it is like thin cream. Pour it into the freezer; put on the lid, catch the handle of the can, and twist it round among the ice as fast as possible. Take off the lid, scrape the mixture (frozen tO the sides of the can) among the rest; replace the lid, and again twirl away, until it is all frozen.</p>
        <p>Dandelion Wine.</p>
        <p>Gather a quantity of the blossoms (about four quarts) and put triem into a large earthen pan, and pour over them one gallon of boiled water, slightly cooled. Stir, cover with a blanket, an leave for three days, stirring frequently. Strain off the liquor, and boil it for 30 minutes with the rind of a lemon and of an orange, and ounce of ginger. Now add the fruit (lemon and orange) sliced, also 311 b. of loaf sugar to each gallon of liquor. Stir till the sugar is dissolved, and when lukewarm ferment with ounce of yeast dis- solved with a teaspoonful of sugar, and spread on toast. When fermented for a day or two, put it into a cask or a stone bottle, but do not bung down till the slow fermentation has ceased. Bottle in about two months' time.</p>
        <p>lisalk 'mak ,ssimk Wm. WOW WOK VIIII\VII% snut in say tniCi evergreens—tnose woolly blobs in the foreground represent their tops. I saw nobody about the place, and made this beginning from the top of the steps. I know that I went away fully intending to return next day and finish, however I have not the faintest recollection of going near the place again until the evening of what must have been my last day at Llandolwyn. A singu- larly fine evening it was, with one of the honey-coloured sunsets you see in autumn, and a great amber moon getting up in the east, and I thought I would take a look at those lakes by moonlight. My room. must have been on the ground-floor, for I remem- ber stepping out of a window, and a short walk up some hilly fields brought me to the place. The lakes certainly did look fine— blue steel and frosted silver in and out of the shadows, which were growing sharper as the moon brightened. But I'd been watching them only a few minutes, when I heard steps on the verandah close by, and turning round found myself face to face with an old lady— evidently the lady of the house. She was a very little old woman, in a black gown and a white shawl, with black lace over her white hair, and she had dark/eyes with thick black eyebrows—somehow she suggested one of those quaintly marked, fluffy-caPed moths. " But what struck me most in her appear- ance were her wonderful rings. I had never seen anybody wearing so many. Her fingers literally blazed with them—all diamonds and sapphires: the sparkle caught your eye at once. Of course I apologised for being there, and she said something civil . . . And then it is all .a blur of confusion. But what seems to come next is a recollection of strug- gling there in the verandah with somebody —somebody who clutched me by the arm and whose hand I tried to wrench open; it un- closed easily, as if it hadn't niuch strength in it—and of a voice shrieking close to my ear. This lasted perhaps a minute before we toppled backwards over the edge of some- thing—that flight of steps—and went rolling down together, till I felt a tremendous thump, and everything ended in a shower of red sparks . . . . Beyond that, except a hazy impression of coming up here by rail, it is all a blank. How I got back to the farm, or anything that happened until I found myself again, in the Hospital, is a mystery to me— There." Ellis began to roll up his sketch wwhitihch ahoshwo.vays ofevicdaernetfulylnewsastauhni4er acnoxveiorusloyf the effect of his communication. " Your landlady's account," I said, "was that she had seen, you aPparentiv -lust as usu- al the evening before you left, but found you next morning so queer and dazed-like ' that she sent her son Hugh up to town with you to look after you. I know you did ar- rive escorted by a little coppery-haired man, not very fluent with his Sassenach.' " " Yes, I believe so," Ellis said in a tone of genuine inctiffereince. He wanted something more to the purpose. "Well now, Ellis, what do you infer from the facts you have just related ? " I asked after a short pause. He, too, hesitated slightly before Ile re- plied: " That I tried to snatch off the old Lady's rings, missed my footing in the scuffle, and fell with her down the stepf—which was probably the death of her; gave my head an</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>_ – -- -yr</head>
        <p>_ - -- -yr</p>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH. Various Subjects Reviewed.</p>
        <p>Hurried and rapid eating, which to-day is too common among all classes of society, prevents anything like efficient mastication, and as a result the food has a very poor start in the digestive process before reaching the stomach. It is always wiser to take less food if need be, and then masticate that thoroughly rather than hurry rapidly through a big meal. There is no doubt but that "bolting" the food is one of the most prolific causes of indiges- tion, dyspepsia, constipation, torpid liver, head- ache, and many other symptoms and disturb- ances that might be mentioned. While preven- tion is the best cure there is every reason to believe that the majority of people who suffer from digestive disorders would derive great benefit by taking iffenty of time to " fletcherize " their food, that is, chew it thoroughly before swallowing. Thorough mastication is also essen- tial for the preservation of the teeth, for neglect to use these organs encourages decay, and brings about their early loss.—A. B. Olsen, M.D., in "Good Health." Hysteria. In an article entitled " Hysteria : Causes and Symptoms," Dr. A. B. Olsen, Superintendent of the Caterham Sanitorium, makes the following statement in May "Good Health " : "The most striking sign of hysteria is the obvious loss, more, or less complete, of will power and nerve con- troL The patient is extremely tvhimsical, emotionable, impressionable, and excitable. Laughing or crying, gladness or sadness, smiles or tears, are readily forthcoming without rhyme or reason. The typical hysteric is very fond of sympathy, and loves to be pettied, coddled, and coaxed. Her fondness for attention and caress- ing often causes her to feign symptoms. The patient is self-centred, is given to intro- spection, and is supremely selfish and egotistic. She thinks that no one suffers as she does, and that her complaint is unique and incurable. Lonely and unappreciated, sad and sorrowful, she imagines that the, whole world is unsym- pathetic, and even her relatives and friends have forsaken her. Irritability, more or less constant and chronic, with quick temper and a marked tendency to sulking when disappointed or crossed in any way, are cardinal symptoms of hysteria. Patients are also subject to stupor, delirium, and may go into fits of ecstasy, or catalepsy, or a trance. Exaltation of feelings is another symptom."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>GIRLS NOT TO MARRY.</head>
        <p>GIRLS NOT TO MARRY.</p>
        <p>"Do you see that girl over there," a friend of mine once said to me, "well, she's one of the girls NOT to marry I" But the girl was a pretty, crisp, well- clothed sort of girl and I protested. "Oh," I asked, "and why not? She's sweet looking, and fresh, and likeable—" "But her stockings are wrinkled about her ankles. Women who allow their stockings to hang about thbir ankles• are not the women to marry. They make bad wives. They are lackadaisical, slack, untidy about the house. It's a sure sign. If I see a girl that is pretty and one I would think likeable I always look at her ankles first; if her stockings are loose I know just what she is. I have never known it to fail." This friend is not alone in his strange and fantastical test of domestic )rth. I have another who always looks at a woman's boots. He says that even if a woman dresses badly and has neat boots, there is a lot of worth in her; but if a woman is well dressed and has slovenly and ugly or ill-Zopt boots, then don't look at her. She'll let your home grow untidy, dirty: if you have babies they'll nev- er be decently washed and servants, under a woman like that, will become slack and im- possible to work. No, never wed a woman who can't take trouble over her boots, he says. A WOMAN'S WAIST.</p>
        <p>I have a third friend who always looks at a Woman's waist; he says that there is some- thing potential about the way a lady keeps her waist. It should be trim and shapely. If it bulges, if safety pins or skirt lining, or blouse ends show; or if the belt itself is old and broken, frayed or discoloured he shuns that woman like a plague. She, too, will make but an indifferent wife. Another man I know will not have the wo- man who "finishes up" with pins; the sight of pins sticking in at various import- ant junctures of blouse or' collar, or coat or skirt drives him frantic: he says a woman like that will run the home for ever on the " anything-will-do-just-now " line. He'd be always having his dinner off cold ham brought in at the last moment from the cook- chop round the corner, and the corners of the rooms would.be choked with dirt until the woman got a dust-pan "presently." Yet another friend detests girls whose nailft are not up to the high-water mark of lus- trousness begot of manicure sets. Another who detests a woman who indulges in low- heels and "sensible" clothing (he says that this type of woman is so anxious to take care of her mind that her home and her husband can go to the dickens). Another who can't bear dingy blouses and vet another who would rather not marry than marry a girl whose bootlaces were ever allowed to be broken, or who carried an old purse; and a whole collection of men join in avoiding that sort of girl who wants to be loved for her beautiful nature, and not for her beauty of face or dress—and shows it. "Those girls who look soulful and who dress in an 'arty' fashion," said one, "always make a man feel that they will read Browning, until the breakfast bacon has been burnt to a cinder, and the coffee has boiled away." But, curiously enough, no man seems td mind the girl whose hair looks, generally, on the verge of tumbling down; as one fellow told me. "Sometimes it looks rather charm- ing, and at anytime a woman's hair is pretty —but of course it depends upon the woman.' . _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>WORD OF WARNING.</head>
        <p>A WORD OF WARNING.</p>
        <p>Though on the whole a man has a decided liking for law and order in his home, even when the is by nature untidy and careless himself, many a one will feel that there can be too much of it. One bachelor, and a methodical one too, uttered a word of warn- ing against the house-proud woman. He was convinced that his life with her would be sst to the tune of "Don't," that he would not be permitted to smoke in the room where cDean curtains had been hung; that the cu- shions must never have their inviting plump- ness ruffled no matter how weary his own back might be; that he could never bestrew the table with the paraphernalia pertaining to his special hobby, because it looked untidy. He felt and not unreasonably that a house presided over by such a girl would not be in the best sense a home, a place where he could take his ease, a haven where his heart would turn fondly during his absence. One of the reasons which hare been serious- ly put forward to account for the reluctance of young men to marry, is that girls of to-</p>
