hining on the commended a survey from the west terrace, Paulina Ellis, the wife of a disti and no sooner were you gone round the cor- 13-21- ner there, than that wretch attacked me from behind, having stolen up through the shrubberies, and snatched away my watch, and was tearing off my rings, when hearing my scream, SOU nobly sned to my rescue. and</p>
        <p>awful bang, but managed to make my way home hau-stunnen—Can- you, Stewart, hon- estly say that you think I have not strong grounds for coming to such a conclusion ? " "I can," I asserted boldly., " and I do, by Jove. If you ask my candid opinion, I sim- ply believe that finding this ring in that un- accountable way—whiai I don't pretend to explain—has set your imagination working on the merest trifles, till you have fabricated yourself a sort of permanent nightmare." " An.d if I had nothing more to go upon, I'd be ready enough to think so too," Ellis said. " But look at this." He took from his pocket book a crumpled printed scrap with rough edges. "I chanced on it the day be- fore yesterday," he said. " The paper came round a parcel from Mudie's, and this bit was torn off. I happened to see it, lying about, and the names caught my eye. Then, I tried to get the rest of thelpaper, but na- turally theiy had lit a fire with it by that time."</p>
        <p>The scrap was headed EWS, in the large Gothic characters fashionable for newspaper titles, and in the line below was ober 7, 1899: the paragraph to which Ellis pointed re- mained, however, intact, and ran as fol- 10W8</p>
        <p>"Murderous Attack upon an Old Lady. " Our correspondent reports a dastard:y outrage from the retired hamlet of Llandol- wYn. Yesterday morning Miss Wynne, an aged and much respected lady, was found ly- ing in an unconscious condition outside her residence, having sustained a grave com- pound fracture of the skull. Her gold watch and chain are missing, and several valuable rings, which the venerable victim was accus- tomed to wear, were scattered on the sward adjacent, whence it is conjectured that, be- ing alarmed, her cowardly assailant fted, abandoning a portion of his booty. Miss Wynne up to the present continues insen- sible, and the fatal nature of her injuries unfortunately precludes the possibility of her "And mindy . Noou,, saee,lue to the identity of the miscreant has as yet been ascertained, but it is considered probable that an itinerant tramp may have strayed into the grounds. i sea, my eye travelling along h The police maintain a reticent attitude." that I have told you so far had become clear to me before I read this paragraph, which seems to have hardly any room for doubt. I spent all yesterday looking up files of last year's newspapers for a fuller account, but tidlaEsitlisp, en as penny-line, h- len w I've found nothing. The date, you see, just fits my return." It certainly did; and there were other points in my friend's strange story which staggered me not a little. Chief of these was the fact that I had always considered the symptoms of Ellis's illness to indicate concussion of the brain resulting from ex- ternal violence, rather than any naturally occurring malady; an opinion which none of my colleagues had shared, and which had never been communicated to our patient, but which now reciprocally confirmed and was onnfirmed by his emerging reminiscences. Then the finding of the ring was, to say the least of it, a queer coincidence. Perplexed and uneasy, I sat oracularly dumb. Ellis, starting up, began to stalk aindesely about the room, but soon stnnned at my.. end</p>
        <p>of it and resumed: "Good heavens, don't you see, Stewart, what utter ruin this is to me ? Even apart from any possible legal comequences, and, the horror of having com- mitted the crime—what does it involve ? Why, it proves facts about me which must cut me off henceforward from everythina that makes life 'worth living. Do you think', for instance, that, knowing myself to be a lunatic, subject to kleptomaniacal, if not homicidal impulses, I could dream of marry- ing Paulina. I'm at least not criminal enough for that . . . But get the matter cleared up one way or the other I must with- out delay. At Llandolwyn, now " With that it flashed upon me that he want- ed a companion on his fateful quest. " Let us both run down to-morrow," I said promptly. " Once there, we'll soon clear up everything." We went by the first, dismally early train next morning: yet the autumn day was near- ing its misty close when we arrived at Llandolwyn's Inn, where we straightway en- countered a flaxen-whiskered, ex-waiterlike landlord. With him, after hungry ordering of dinner, we fell into the usual local dig- course, which our peculiar circumstances pre- sently led us to direct towards a definite ob- ject. " I suppose then that except just in the summer h• ummie hereabouts ? season, are not many people I " Well no, sir; indeed I may say that the pammanent residents is very few . " Our host gave an exhaustive list of them, ending witn: " and when I've mentioned old Miss Wynne up at Craig-y-Don, that's about all the gentry we enjoys. The rest is in the farming line, such as Mrs. Price there at High Banks, or—"</p>
        <p>`? Wynne, did you say ? An old Miss Wynne ?''</p>
        <p>t` Yes sir; lives up at the big house on the top of the rise a bit further along Towyth road. Craig-y-Don--I'm told, sir, as it means Rock of the Wild Dog'; but all such is extinc' in this locality." " That's it, that's what the name was, Ellis muttered at my elbow, "and she's alive—thank goodness . " Then aloud: "And she lives quite alone at Craig-y-Don ? " " Well, since her poor sister's death a twelvemonth back, sir—" Ellis started vio- lently—" she's kep' a sort of lady companion. That was a bad business up there, sir, last autumn. You'll maybe remember to have seen accounts of it in the papers ? • It .was reported particular " (with a self-conscious air) "in the Aberyrtwd News." " No: what was that ?" said Ellis sharply. " Why, sir, it was just this month of last year, poor Miss Grace one evening happened to be taking a turn on the terrace as rune round the house, and it seems the villin must have got access surruptuou3 to the grounds, for she was attacked, and knocked senseless, and her gold watch took, and the valible rings tore off her fingers, though some was gubsequent dropped contiguous. But the vii- in got away, as he easy might, for there was only a couple of old servants—females— in the house, and Miss Eleanor being weakly herself that 'night, they were took up witn her, and poor Miss Grace was never missed till they found her next - morning laying there in a terrible bad way. It was young</p>
        <p>Damage to the extent of £250,000 t's done by the burning down of the 1)13,-,11'wei Atlantic and United States Fertilizer v° at Baltimore,</p>
      </div>
