        <p>i•INN-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0273" n="art0273">
        <head>WATCHES PRESENTED.</head>
        <p>WATCHES PRESENTED.</p>
        <p>Councillor Osborne asked the Mayor, on be- half of the Warrington rootball Club Com- mittee, to present each boy with a watch for being so successful in the competition. The thirteen members of the team then re- ceived the handsome presents from the Mayor, who afterwards announced that he would give the two reservists, John Armstrong and Joseph Topping, a watch each himself, for they had always been there in readiness. He thought the generosity of the Football Club was very great indeed. He was grieved to a great extent when Corporation business in London made it im- possible for him to see the final, but he was glad the Mayoress was there. (Applause.) However, he had no liking for the great match at the Crystal Palace, but so eager was he to know the result that he got home as soon as possible. He was proud of the boys who had won.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0274" n="art0274">
        <head>Co-incidence.</head>
        <p>A Co-incidence.</p>
        <p>It was coincident to remark that the War- rington senior team won the South-West Lanca- shire Cup when it was first put up, and now the schoolboys had won the first South-West Lanca- shire Cup for which they had competed. It re- flected great credit on the boys and those who had coached them, also the teachers. He hoped the boys would only be as zealous in their busi- ness life and then they would be equally suc- cessful as they were in football. He enjoyed seeing the boys playing Wigan more than any other match he had witnessed. He had thought it would have been his pleas- ure to have entertained the senior team, but they had failed him. (Laughter.) He was very sorry they had missed the cup, but the boys had done exceedingly well, and he hoped to see them do it again next year. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0275" n="art0275">
        <head>FOOTBALL CLUB THANKED.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL CLUB THANKED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hawkesworth (chairman Schools' Rugby Committee) proposed a vote of thanks to the Warrington Football Club Committee for their handsome gifts, also for the admirable ar- rangements and the valuable assistance they haAl given them, not only in the competition, but in seasons past. He would be sorry if too much honour were given to the winning of that cup, to the neglect of the solid basis built up in previous years. (Hear, hear.) The teachers did not enter into that work solely with the idea of winning the cup, but for the sake of benefiting the no or 400 boys, who were encouraged to play the game not for what they reaped from it. They hoped they would be favoured with the continued sup- port of the Warring,ton Club, and it would be the constant endeavour of the teachers to de- serve that support and encouragement. If the club would help them to give the boys the exer- cise the club itself would ultimately reap the benefit of it. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0276" n="art0276">
        <head>SPORT AN EDUCATION.</head>
        <p>SPORT AN EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Newburn seconded, and spoke of the teachers' endeaVours to encourage the school children in sport, believing that their educa- tion was not complete with arithmetic, &amp;c. There could never be a finer exhibition of real</p>
        <p>sport than was witnessed on Saturday. Ire was sorry it had got about the town that the poor boy who was injured on Saturday was dead, for that was incorrect, and had done a tremendous amount of harm. It would defeat the ends of the teachers if parents naturally became afraid that if a boy did participate in the games lie would be injured. Nothing of the sort had happened, for the teachers were ex- tremely careful that none of the boys should be injured, and kept the sport from being rough in any shape or form. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0277" n="art0277">
        <head>MAYOR'S KINDNESS.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR'S KINDNESS.</p>
        <p>Alderman Tinnion, replying, said it was a gift with the Mayor to be so kind and generous with all. (Hear, hear.) That was a red-letter day in the lives of the boys, and they would look on the Mayor in after years as the great friend of the boys and girls, lie urged upon the boys not to get swollen heads, and pointed out that such affectation marred many lives. He thought, however, that they had sufficient about them to know that that was only an incident in, and not the Alpha and Omega of, their lives. (Hear, hear.) They should be encouraged by their success, and the memory would be a joy and incentive to them. They should take that as an object lesson, as they strove through the different walks of life, and it was only by keep- ing in mind certain aims and objects that they would accomplish something in life, lie was afraid Mr. Locker was going to give up his posi- tion with the football club, and he (the speaker), as ex-president, would fall out of offi- cial position. However, anything he could do would not be lessened by his having no official connection. He did not hesitate to say that the Warrington Football Club was the only one in the world which promoted football sports of all kinds with so noble and true an object, and no club carried them on more honestly and sincerely. They had testimonials from various other clubs to that effect. He thought the teachers should have the acknowledgment for the successful issue they were celebrating that night. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0278" n="art0278">
        <head>AN OLD SCHOOLBOYS' LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>AN OLD SCHOOLBOYS' LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Councillor Osborne proposed a vote of thanks to the South-West Lancashire League Football Committee and also associated himself with the thanks to the(Mayor and Mayoress. He also associated with the vote the teachers of War- rington, for that interest in football was only one of their many activities in which they were engaged on behalf of the school children, whom they were trying to make all-round athletes. For five years now the schoolboys had received tuition in football, but when they left school they had been neglected.</p>
        <p>The committee had been considering an inter- mediate league for boys from 14 to 16 years of age, to be termed the Old Schoolboys' League. He hoped the teachers would help them in that object. (Hear, hear.) He hoped the boys would always be honest in their play and straightfor- ward in their dealings. (Applause.) Mr. T. Pemberton seconded, and said he was sure the committee would be encouraged by the success they had achieved. He heartily sup- ported the idea of an intermediate league, and hoped every advantage would be taken of it if it were formed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0279" n="art0279">
        <head>FINANCIAL SUCCESS.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Millard suitably responded, and said the I committee would go on with the ,competition. They were satisfied with the financial results at Warrington. (Applause.) Possibly they might enlarge the area and see if they could not make a football league for schoolboys all over the Northern Union. The Warrington team was the finest schoolboy team he had ever seen. (Ap- plause.) Alderman Tinnion proposed, Councillor Osborne seconded, and Messrs. Hawkesworth and Bowen supported, a hearty vote of thanks to Mr. Price for the assiduous why in which he had worked. Mr. Price suitably responded. The party then left for the Park Picture Palace, where the film of the match was shown. Mr. Bibby welcomed the party, and expressed his admiration of Saturday's game. The Mayor and Mayoress retained a menu card, with the autographs of the boys on it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0280" n="art0280">
        <p>Continued in next column</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0281" n="art0281">
        <head>"SOCCER" ENDS.</head>
        <p>"SOCCER" ENDS.</p>
        <p>Orford St. John's Double Medal Competition Victory. BRIEF REVIEW OF THE SEASON.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the curtain was rung down on the doings of the Warrington and District League and most competitions in connection with Asso- ciation football in general for the season 1913- 1914. In the Warrington and District League the First Division of the competition started with nine clubs, of which two have resigned, namely, The Divex and Adult School, and their records have had to be deducted from the other members of the League. Some of the clubs have not been able to complete their fixtures, in some cases owing to postponement of fixtures through cup-ties, and in others owing to the expiration of the lease of their playing fields before the end of the season; while in some cases towards the latter end of the season the club committees have had much 4difficulty in raising a team, owing to a lack of interest on the part of their players, because they were not in the running for top positions in the League. This latter fact is much to be deplored, as it is chiefly the players themselves who are to blame for the position they have allowed the club to drift into, owing to their inability to win matches; or, in other words, it shows a poor spirit of sportsmanship in not acknowledging defeat when they meet more capable opponents than themselves.</p>
        <p>The Great Sankey Club have maintained their reputation of being our best junior team in the district, they having gone through their season's League fixtures without sustaining defeat, and will have the honour of having their club's name as champions for the season 1913-14 in- scribed on the magnificent " Dodds" Challenge Shield, of which they have become holders for the next twelve months. The players will be presented with suitable medals, which will be selected at the annual meeting, to be held at the Lion Hotel at the latter end of May. In the Second Division the Warrington Excel- sior are to be congratulated on the splendid form and consistency they have shown during the season. They have only lost one match, drawn one, won twelve, and head their divi- sion with a substantial lead of their nearest opponents. They are to become holders of a splendid silver cup, which has been presented to the Warrington and District League by Mr. Starkey, of Latchford. This will also be supple- mented with a set of medals to the players. THE MEDALS' WINNERS.</p>
        <p>----- ----- As a fitting conclusion to the season Orford St. John's were opposed by St. Barnabas' in the final tie for the Minor Medal Competition; and as both teams had previously met in a Medal Competition earlier in the season, when St. John's proved themselves victors, there was much to enthuse over in the meeting for the local competition, when the Barnabas' players were intent on having their revenge, but the St. John's players thought otherwise, and in- flicted a more decisive defeat on their opponents by winning by four goals to one. Thus the St. John's players become the winners of two sets of medals for the season just closed, and their record for the season speaks well for the club, and the well-merited treputation they enjoy throughout 'the town and district. I believe I am correct in saying that during the season just closed they have been successful in gaining the football prize twice which is given by the "Manchester Evening Chronicle." After their Works' Competition match Cros- field's entertained Altrincham St. Vincent's in their North Cheshire League match, which ought to have been played the previous week, and after being on equal terms at the interval they had to retire defeated by four goals against two.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0282" n="art0282">
        <head>FINAL LEAGUE TABLES. First Division.</head>
        <p>FINAL LEAGUE TABLES. First Division.</p>
        <p>Goals. P. W. L. D. For.Agst•Pts. Great Sankey Stockton Heath Orford Barracks Crosfield's Weaver Refiners Daresbury Weston The Divex and Adult School Clubs having resigned, their records are withdrawn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0283" n="art0283">
        <head>Second Division.</head>
        <p>Second Division.</p>
        <p>Goals. P. W. L. D. For.Agst.Pts. Excelsior Bank Quay Bees l3 7 2 4 38 18 18 Orford St. John's l2 7 2 3 40 13 17 *Emmanuel 0.8 l4 7 5 2 39 25 14 St. Barna bas' Fairfield Fairfield 'United Wilderspool B. 9 0 7 2 7 45 2 *Emmanuel have two points deducted for playing ineligible players.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT MINOR MEDAL COMPETITION. Final Tie. Orford St. John's V. St. Barnabas'. On the Penketh Recreation Ground. Teams: St. Barnabas'—J. Long; Spruce and E. Long; Appleton, Challinor and Foster; Maddock, Wood, Shuttleworth, Vernon and Bevan. Orford St. John's—J. Forber; Harrison and Mill- ward; Denny, Stretford L.nd Atherton; Tickle, Meathin, W. Smith, Smalley and Hankinson. Referee—Mr. H. Smith, Latchford. Wood opened the score for St. Barnabas'. From the centre-kick St. John's showed more settled form, and, gaining ground in their opponents' half, Smalley equalised the score. The game I was now fiercely contested, and St. Barnabas' had hard lines, but the St. John's defence pre- vailed, and play was transferred to the other end, where Tickle hit the cross-bar with a fine shot. St. John's were not to be denied and were soon attacking again, when Smalley sent in a slow shot. Long made no effort to save and St. John's took the lead. Half-time arrived with St. John's leading by two goals to one. St. John's were soon attacking and Tickle in- creased the lead. This reverse had its effect on the St. Barnabas' players, who seemed to lose heart and could make little impression on the St. John's defence. Smalley was next prominent with good play, but was overhauled when near goal, and then Shuttleworth shot over at the other end. St. John's attacked again and gained a penalty kick, from which Millward increased the lead. Result: Orford St. John's, four goals; St. Barnabas', one goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0284" n="art0284">
        <p>MANCHESTER AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. First Division. Rylands' Recs. v.-Prestwich Church Institute.</p>
        <p>Tins match, the last of the season, was played on the Gorsey-lane enclosure before a poor at- tendance. Stretford kicked off for the !` Wire- men." Forster returning from a mark, Prest- wich tried at goal, but failed. Rylands' next at- tacked, and Griffiths scored from a pass by Warren. The latter added the extra points. From a cross kick by Horrocks, Griffiths scored again, Morris failing at goal. Griffiths was car- ried off hurt. Although a man short Rylands' still had the upper hand, and tries were scored by Morris, Slater and Welsby, Warren placing one goal. Half-time arrived with the score: Rylands' Recs., 2 goals 6 tries; Prestwich, nil. Prestwich, commenced the second half in a de- termined 'tanner, and their efforts were re - warded, Forster scoring. The goal kick failed. Roscoe, who was playing a good game for Prest- wich, had hard lines. Rylands' at last wore the visitors down, and tries were scored by Stret- ford, Morris and Brandwood, Stretford goaling twice. Rylands finished up their League en- gagements without a defeat, thus winning by 4 goals 9 tries (35 points), to Prestwich, 1 try.. Warren and Gallop were in fine form for the homepters, as were also Horrocks and Stret- ford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0285" n="art0285">
        <head>Rylands' Recs. V. Rest of the League.</head>
        <p>Rylands' Recs. V. Rest of the League.</p>
        <p>At Cadishead to'-day. Hylands' team: F. Pot- ter; F. Horrocks, J. Brandwood, A. Horrocks, P. Morris; G. Warren, J. Gallop; A. Stretford (eapt.), J. Whittaker, H. -Morris, F. Mason, D. Slater, J. Broadhurst. .</p>
        <p>The cup and medals will be presented after the match.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0286" n="art0286">
        <head>LADY BOWLERS.</head>
        <p>LADY BOWLERS.</p>
        <p>The Desire for Manly Sports.</p>
        <p>LOCAL NOTES AND COMMENTS.</p>
        <p>By." Thumb Bias."</p>
        <p>Should ladies bowl? That is a question on which there is a great diversity of opinion. Some will answer in the affirmative, pointing out that women need exercise as well as men, and argue that if this form of outdoor recrea- tion is good for the stronger sex it is equally good for the gentler section of the community. The only logical outcome of such an opinion is that Port the football jersey r the the Without going further into such possibilities, "No !" will answer another equally ar- ooriloakdoitesbsa should s we will point out the negative side to the ques- dtieonnt. sportsman, "women should on no account play bowls." For this assertion he may be taken for an anti-suffragist, but quite wrongly. He says that the woman's place is the home, and whilst not objecting to milder pursuits, such as tennis, he has the fixed opinion that bowling is too manly a sport for women to indulge in.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0287" n="art0287">
        <head>NO _MIXED GAMES.</head>
        <p>NO _MIXED GAMES.</p>
        <p>Ladies invaded certain green; during last season, but except in a few isolated cases, noth- orywas reported. Whilst in no ydi sagreeing, but entirely agreeing, that nrngmwl aing hslihy o sual satisfactory t ndu lge in outdoor pastimes, I say that we should draw iho line somewhere, and that somewhere is the b6wling green. It has been argued that the men could teach the ladies some quarters that this end in tt he 1%, nasr ts nogfg egsettetdi nign the greens should be throw "touchers," and to n open to both men and women. Whilst not being so Puritanical and narrow-minded as to hold up my hands in horror or in mock modesty and say, "But what about the language being too strong for the women,"—for, personally, I have heard very little that was objectionable on bowling greens —I would say it is not seemly for women to be seen struggling with that determination char- acteristic of them on the "velvety sward." I also take it this way, that it is not so much that women like the game that they play it, but rather it is that same determination to show the men that they are not to be ousted. Surely some inventive genius will devise a more modi- fied form of outdoor recreation for those " sporty " women who are not satisfied with the games they have. The bowling green is un- doubtedly and exclusively for the men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0288" n="art0288">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES. The Works' Association Matches.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES. The Works' Association Matches.</p>
        <p>The green at the Horse and Jockey was in pass- able condition on Saturday, when the prelimin- ary round in the Works' Association was con- cluded, and the first round proper commenced. Rylands' No. 1 gained the outstanding victory over P. and K. Foundry in the preliminary round, and this was one of the best matches, if not the best match, of the afternoon. T. Humphries (scratch) set a good example when he beat P. Blackledge, who had a start of four, 15-7 in 15 ends. The P. and K. man had no chance at all, Humphries taking four doubles and seven singles to his three singles. A splendid game was witnessed between S. Massey (1), Rylands', and P. Hackett (5). De- spite the advantage of his opponent, Massey ran his woods well and peeled with seven at the eighth end. Again at the 14th end the game was 12 all, but here Massey failed, and the foundry- man ran out with a single and a double. W. Langley (1) and D. Ferguson (4), P. and K., had a good set-to, and after peeling at 5,7, 10, 12 and 14, Langley just managed to come home with the odd one to his credit. These were characteristic games of the match, the Rylands' men feeling the handicap against them in no light fashion However, they even- tually won by 20 points, and felt highly de- lighted with themselves.</p>
        <p>FLETCHER'S VICTORY. Fletcher's 24-point victory over Whitecross Ropery was a true state of the match. The Ropery men had an advantage in the handicap- ping, but Fletcher's only lost four games, two of these being only by one point each. W. Woods's feat over S. Massey (Whitecross) is really worthy of comment. He romped home at the eighth end by 15-6, the Whitecross player not having landed a single block. The C.L.C. No. I beat their rivals from the L. and N.-W. Railway by 19 points, although ' the match was a very keenly contested one. The tightest game of the match was between F. Dale (4), C.L.C., and B. Anderton (2). Dale soon ran to 8-4, but a single and three succes- sive doubles helped Anderton on wonderfully, so that at the 11th end he led 11-10. The players peeled with 14 at the 18th end, and then Anderton managed to get his winning wood in. In the first round proper the Alliance Box Company team had no chance against the Bew- sey Wire Ropery, who won by 76 points. The vanquished are a set of novices who still re- quire a little more practice, but I am sorry, seeing that it was their first time of entering the competition, that they did not do better. New teams are always encouraged in this com- petition. Not a "Box" man won his individual game, although some of the handicaps were sufficient to tempt anyone. Very few of them got two points over the handicap. Better form next year! A CLOSE MATCH.</p>
        <p>There was nothing much to choose between . the Fennel-street Tannery men and Richmond's (Academy-street). Both teams were well handi- capped, and Richmond's thoroughly deserved - their 15-point victory. W. Caddick (3), Richmond's, played a fine na e advantage, against TaylorTa3ior (6 a) With such a start- ing certainly have showed poor form had he not led at one period of the game. At the 10th end the game was 11-8 in his favour, but Caddick took the block and ran to 14-11 at the 15th end. Taylor then drew away and with three singles peeled. It was touch and go at the finish, but Caddick got the winner. T. Owen (4), the Fennel-street captain, should have Wen in his game against IT. Smith (5), for he led nearly all the way, and at the 12th end only wanted one to win, whilst his opponent .was 11. Smith took the little 'un, and with a single, double and single gained the winning points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0289" n="art0289">
        <head>KEEN STRUGGLE.</head>
        <p>A KEEN STRUGGLE.</p>
        <p>A keen struggle was witnessed between the Penketh Tannery team and Crosfield's Tin De- partment. Each game was very even, and amongst the best were those between A. Bowyer and H. Littlewood, and the one in which W. Goulden (4) beat ii. Carter (4), Crosfield's, by 15-13. Goulden started well away; and led 8-6 at the fifth end, but here he stopped, Carter running the block on a favourite mark, gaining an advantage of 13-8. Goulden then gained the block, and for the last five ends Carter never scored, the game ending 15-13. " "</p>
        <p>Some of the best individual games of the afternoon were seen in this match, and the game between J. Ashburner (4) and IT. Bowyer (3) was an excellent one for excitement. Ashburner ran to 10-5 at the seventh end, but after he had led all the way, Bowym, who had played splendidly, peeled with 13 t the 17th end. Again the scores were equal when one was wanted, and this came to the Tin Shop player.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0290" n="art0290">
        <p>M Ns, 3IM XAL X. 4 XVI MAE 3IE I\l` rX4 X 3 lir 10 ME7E7b Or MR Ma.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0291" n="art0291">
        <head>P.&amp; K. WIN EASILY.</head>
        <p>P.&amp; K. WIN EASILY.</p>
        <p>Although they had a slight advantage in handicap, Randall Bros.' Tannery were no match for the P. and K. Boiler Yard men. All the former players had a start of six, yet only one, T. Bradshaw, was successful in beating his man. J. Weave (scratch) made short work of T. Seddon (6), for the latter only scored one. There was no doubt about the issue at any time of the match, P. and K. always being favourites.</p>
        <p>A number of interesting matches will be played to-day in this competition, and specta- tors are invited to turn up in large numbers.</p>
        <p>BULL'S HEAD MAIN.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the preliminary main at the Bull's Head Club was won by J. Bate, who in the final beat W. Simister, 15-11. The other two players knocked out in the semi-final were E. Lunt and J. Swanton.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Eyes is the secretary of a new Thurs- day afternoon club which has been established, and this promises to be successful.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>At the Lancashire County Officials' main, at the Roebuck Hotel, Flixton, last Saturday, the Bull's Head Club was represented by Messrs. Albert Gleave, A. Regan and T. Rigby. In the first consolation Mr. Albert Gleave, the county player, carried off a handsome prize. Mr. Regan was beaten in the third round. If he had won he would have qualified for the prizes given to the first twelve.</p>
        <p>The draw for the first round of the Lanca- shire County Crown Green Association's cham- pionship competition was made on Monday, and the following matches, amongst others, were arranged: Bird-in-Hand, Flixton, v. Warring- ton Cemetery, at Newton-le-Willows; Leigh Liberals v. Bull's Head, Warrington, at Prest- wich George; Chorlton B.G. v. Warrington Junior Conservatives, at Bird-in-Hand, Flixton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0292" n="art0292">
        <head>AMATEUR ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>AMATEUR ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Draw for Championship.</p>
        <p>The Warrington and District Association promises to go great guns this season. Twenty clubs have entered for the championship com- petition, and all the teams possess good men, so that the games shbuld be of a very interesting character.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Dean presided at a council meeting of the Association, held in the Liberal Club on Thursday evening, when the chief business was the draw for the championship. Mr. A. H. Crosfield wrote expressing his pleas- ure at being re-elected president, and the secre- tary also read other letters of acceptance of office.</p>
        <p>The general opinion of the meeting, and I cer- tainly agree with it, was that the best thing to do would be to have all the byes in the first round.</p>
        <p>Eight clubs will take part in the first round, and the remaining 12 received byes. The first four matches will be: Hutchins' Tannery v. Earlestown Vulcan; Penketh v. Con- servative Club Juniors; Whitecross v. Rylands' No. 2; Fearnhead v. Crosfield's No. 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0293" n="art0293">
        <head>GOOD SEND-OFF.</head>
        <p>A GOOD SEND-OFF.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. T. Wood, the energetic secretary of the association, announced that for the handicap and the Individual Merit Competitions a player could be registered if he gave his name seven days beforehand. The first round of the championship will be Played on May 16th, and the first round in the consolation will be on June 6th. The annual handicap will commence on June 13th, and will extend to June 27th. The Individual Merit Competition will run from August Bth to 29th. Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co. had re- sponded to a request from the secretary, and had sent £lO 10s., which, in Mr. Woods' words, would give the Association a good send-off. Mr. Woods was also of the opinion that they would have a good balance next year. The firm were thanked for their generosity, on the proposition of Mr. Willett, seconded by Mr. Garnett. Mr. Woods was also thanked for his efforts. A sub-committee was appointed to go into the registration question, the sub-committee to strike out all the professionals. This sub-com- mittee will consist of Messrs. Regan, Bowers, Egerton, Smith and Weston.</p>
        <p>„ The next meeting of the Association was fixed for May 21st, at the Junior Conservative Club, when the draw for the setond round till take place.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0294" n="art0294">
        <head>LIBERAL BOWLERS.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL BOWLERS.</p>
        <p>"Peter Peacock" Cup Draw.</p>
        <p>A record! Such is the report in regard to the entries for the "Peter Peacock" Challenge Cup Competition in connection with the Warrington Liberal Association. There are 600 competitors; and 60 teams, and, given a fine day, the compe- tition will make an auspicious start on Saturday next. .</p>
        <p>Mr. Campbell has got the green at the Bowl- ing Green Inn in a splendid condition, to the satisfaction of the trundlers. Players in this competition will do well to note the following regulations: Membership card must be produced before taking part in the competition; players will only be notified when they have to appear in their first match. All other notifications will appear each week in the col- umns of "The Examiner " ; should any of the matches be postponed on account of bad weather or any other unforeseen circumstances, the committee will re-arrange them after the remaining games in this round have been played. The referee is Mr. R. H. Belford ; assistant, Mr. S. H. Walker; and gatekeeper, Mr. W. Vose.</p>
        <p>• T--he following are the matches to be played next Saturday:—</p>
        <p>_ First Round. 2.o.—St. Austin's A. v. Bewsey•A. 2.4s.—Bewsey D. v. Latchford D. 3.3o.—Howley E. v. St. Austin's E. 4.ls.—Whitecross A. v. Fairfield A. s.o.—Whitecross G. v. a. John's E.</p>
        <p>5.45.—5 t. John's B. v. St. Austin's H. 6.30.—5 t. Austin's I. v. Whitecross N.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0295" n="art0295">
        <head>THURSDAY TEAMS MEET.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY TEAMS MEET.</p>
        <p>The first match of the Bull's Head Thursday team was played on the Bull's Head green on Thursday, when the homesters met the Ceme- tery Thursday contingent. The play was of an interesting character, and the results were as follows : Bull's Head. Cemetery. E. Pennington .... 5 J. Cross ll T. Hockenhull .... 11 13 S. Robinson E. Eyes ll W. Kay 3 T. Hackett A. Gleave (No. 2). 10 T. Ellison J. W. Jolley G F. Freeman... 11 H. Wilcox ......... 3 Tr. Rowley J. Roberts F. Daintith 11 13 S Short H. Cross W. Simister</p>
        <p>123 63 69 58 Bull's Head won the doubles by 5 and the singles by 54 points.</p>
      </div>
