      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0319" n="art0319">
        <head>PLAYER' TRIUMPH,</head>
        <p>PLAYER' TRIUMPH,</p>
        <p>Shakespeareans Wind-Up Season Amidst Enthusiastic Scenes.</p>
        <p>AMATEUR ACTORS SCORE.</p>
        <p>No better tribute could have been paid to the amateur performers than that which marked the conclusion of Saturday night's "Merry Wives of Windsor," for the whole house raised cheer after cheer, and cries for speeches were heard on every hand, and the applause increased in volume as the producer stepped towards the footlights.</p>
        <p>Mr. LAW said this was the fourth year they had had the honour and pleasure of appearing be- fore the Warrington people to celebrate the anniversary of the birthday of Shakespeare. lie thanked the audience most cordially on behalf of all the company for the great encouragement given. For four years they had tried, and it was only by the help they had received that they had succeeded in presenting tragedy. comedy, history, comical, and now they were only left with pastoral, and tragical-comical- historical-pastoral to complete the various forms of Shakespearean drama. (Laughter.) They hoped to commemorate Shakespeare still further if the townspeople would extend to them the support which they must have. They had had very many troubles, difficulties and disappointments, but they felt they were young society, and with the strength of youth they said to themselves: "If we come, and you see, we ehalreenquer.'</p>
        <p>Have Conquered. "You have come," continued Mr. Law, amid applause; "you have seen, and we :eel we have conquered." (Applause.) They hoped to make a name in the town for celebrating the birthday of Shakespeare. There was no other town, with the exception of Strat- ford-on-Avon, where for four successive Years Performances had been given on or about the time of the birthday of Shakespeare, to celebrate the genius of the greatest man who had ever lived. (Applause.) Warrington was in a unique Position. They hoped the commemoration would not be allowed to lapse, but that the town would become as celebrated as Stratford-on- Avon, where for over thirty years performances had been given which had helped to make its name known throughout the world. He thought there was a chance now for Warrington to gain distinction. That night they (the amateurs) retired into obscurity, but before they did he wanted to thank the people who had helped them. In the first place they thanked the President (Sir Gilbert Greenall), the vice-P'' dents and the patrons. They also tendered their thanks to Sir Joseph Beecham, to the management of the theatre, and also to the stage staff. He thanked the members of the company for the loyalty which they had shown to him personally, and the business manager, Mr. W. Mason, who, in the words of the messen- ger in "Much Ado About Nothing," "in the figure of a lamb has performed the feats of a lion." (Applause.) Needleu to say, they could not forget the enormous service rendered by Mr. Ryder Boys, who had given them the experience of his many years as a teaeher and as a pro- ducer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0320" n="art0320">
        <p>MR. RYDER BOYS' SPEECH. There were loud cries for other players at the conclusion of this speech, and Mr. Ryder Boys received a great ovation as he stepped forward. The big Falstaff said he was very proud to have had the privilege of playing with the War- rington Players, and it was, indeed, a great Pleasure to be associated with such a happy band of amateurs. Ire was sure they must indeed be proud of the young artistes they had in the town, who so loyally tried to master the difficulties of the great poet. (Applause.) They were also proud, he was sure, of Mr. Law, who gave so much time to the productions of the memorials to Shakespeare. (Applause.) One of the greatest memorials they could make to the memory of their great poet, in fact the world's great poet, was by building up sufficient means by which those productions could be car- ried on in the town. (Applause.) It should be a proud moment to the players when they saw the house as full as it was. It would be a great calamity if those performances were dropped for if they were not proud of their townsmen it would indeed be a sad thing. (Applause.) At the close of the performance bouquets and other gifts were presented to the artistes. •</p>
        <p>The mourners were as follows : Mrs. Swinton, Mrs. Bennett, Miss Jackson, Mr. James Houghton, Mrs. Isaac Houghton, Mrs. John Houghton, Mrs. James Houghton, Mr. W. J. Bennett, Mrs. Birchall, Miss Bennett, Mr. Harry Houghton, Mr. H. T. Houghton, Mr. Fred Houghton, Mr. T. A. Bennett, Mr. P. Houghton Bennett, Mr. Stanley Houghton, Mr. Geoffrey Houghton, Mr. Birchall, Mr. T. F. Nicholson, Mr. F. Wright, Dr. James Bennett, Mr. Jos. Warburton, Mr. H. Walsh, Mr. P. Royston, Mr. R. Denham, Mr. W. Gibson, Mrs. Gibson and Mr. Arthur Gibson.</p>
        <p>--- The Warrington Board of Guardians and the Rural District Council were represented by Mr. A. J. Jolley (chairman of the Board of Guard- ians), Mr. G. Mather (chairman of the Rural District Council), Revs. T. R. Matthews and A. M. Mitchell, Messrs. Joshua Ball, W. Furness, W. Flanagan, James Robinson, R. Lee, G. Allen, A. Bottomley (clerk to the Board of Guardians), Dr. Peacocke (district medical officer), B. Shearer (superintendent relieving officer and collector), F. H. Aukland (relieving officer), J. C. Sutton (clerk to the Rural District Coun- cil), F. Kerfoot (inspector of nuisances), and 11. H. Burnage (assistant surveyor). The repre- sentatives of the Woolston-with-Martinscroft Parish Council were: • Messrs. N. Massey, R. Hunter, S. Ellam, J. Platt and James Daintith (clerk).</p>
        <p>- Amongst others present were : Alderman Ben- nett, Dr. B. Wilson Sutherland, Mr. and Mrs. J. Walton, Miss Dyke, Messrs. T. Halton, A. Jolley, G. Pilling, Leonard Bennett, Arthur Gibson. W. S. Howard, W. Potter, E. Gorton, T. Welsby, W. Fairhurst, W. Price, Thomas Kilshaw, A. E. Denham, T. Norcott, S. S. Pen- nington (St. Helens), Wilfred Taylor, J. An- drews, J. Darbyshire and B. Grimshaw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0321" n="art0321">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>BOTH WARRINGTON TEAMS DEFEATED. Both the Warrington teams met with defeat on Saturday. There was nothing approaching brilliant cricket in either match, and the bowling was not up to that of the previous Saturday. Playing against Boughton Hall at Chester, the Warrington first team were dismissed for 77, while their opponents declared with nine wickets down for 150 runs. J. L. Thompson was the top scorer for Warrmg con ',kith Li,and only two others got out of the 'single state." Hannam was not so successful as a trundler, and his four wickets cost him 65 runs. He was hit about pretty freely, and in /7 orers only made three "maidens." W. Bennett took one for 25, and B. Silcock cam* out the most successful bowler of the clay. He ad four wickets for 37.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0322" n="art0322">
        <head>RETURNS. Boughton Hall v. Warrington.</head>
        <p>THE RETURNS. Boughton Hall v. Warrington.</p>
        <p>Boughton Hall.—T. G. Fawcus b Hannam 6, A. M. Carr b Hannam I, W. A. V. Churton b Hannam 4, L. N. Jonea b Hannam 13, L. Hales b Bennett 1, R. Frost o Duckworth b Silcock 31, W. E. Jones not out 71, W. Roberta b Si'cock 5, H. L. Churton b Sileoek 4, H. Dryland b Silcoek 3, C. H. Hodgson not out 11, extras 5; total (for 9 wickets) 154.</p>
        <p>Warrington.—W. Richardson b Roberts 8, S. Partridge b Roberts 16, J. L. Thompson o W. Jones b Roberts 21. W. V. Turton run out 0, B. Silcock o Fawcus b W. Jones 1, J. C. Bradburn run out 6, H. Hawthorn b Hodgson 13, Hannam o Roberts b L. Jones 2, S. Baxter b L. Jones 9, W. Bennett b L. Jones 0, A. Duckworth not out 5, extras 2; total 77.</p>
      </div>
