      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0324" n="art0324">
        <head>LATE IL IC HOUGETON.</head>
        <p>THE LATE IL IC HOUGETON.</p>
        <p>imposine Funeral at Warringtol Cemetery.</p>
        <p>The remains of the late Mr. Wm. Houghton, of The Grange, Paddington, were interred in the Warrington Cemetery on Saturday afternoon, where a large and representative attendance of mourners and friends assembled. The initial part of the service was held in Christ Church, Padgate, when the officiating clergy were the Rev. Canon Willis and the Rev. J. Holdom, who has charge of the parish pend- ing the induction of the new Vicar. The hymns " Peace, perfect peace," and "Now the labourer's task is o'er," were sung, and at the close Mr. F. Blackwell (the organist) played the Dead March from " Saul." The Rector con- ducted the service at the graveside.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0325" n="art0325">
        <head>MOURNERS.</head>
        <p>THE MOURNERS.</p>
        <p>LIST OF WREATHS.</p>
        <p>'Wreaths and floral tributes were sent by: Miss Jackson, Mrs. Swinton, Mrs. Birchall and family, Mrs. Bennett, "Nellie and Percy," Mr. and Mrs. James Houghton and "Stanley," Mrs. Isaac Houghton, Mrs. John Houghton, Mr. and Mrs. Harry Houghton and family, Mr. and Mrs. Fred Ffoughton, Mr. and Mrs. W. J. Bennett, "Alfred and George," Mr. H. T. Houghton and Miss Houghton, "All at Holes-lane Farm," " All at Roseneath," Dr. and Mrs. Bennett, Mr. and M. H. Walsh, Mr. and Mrs. Jos. C. War- burton, Mr. and Mrs. P. Royston, "Anne and Jane," "Gardener and family," Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Bennett, Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Bennett, Mr. and Mrs. Leonard Bennett, Mr. and Mrs. Blackwell, the Misses Cotton (St. Helens), Mr. and Mrs. R. Denham, Mr. and Mrs. H. F. Greenough, Mr. and Mrs. Herbert ITatton, Mr. and Mrs. T. Halton and family, Mrs. and Miss Houghton (Southport), Miss Hunt, Mr. and Mrs. Jennings (Doncaster). Mr. and Mrs. Mcßain (Inverness), Messrs. Wm. and Jos. Potter, Mrs. and Miss Richardson, Miss Robinson (South- port), Mrs. Royston and family, My. and Mrs. John Walton and family, Mrs. I. Warburton and family, Mr. and Mrs. Thos. Welsby, Children of St. Peter's School, Martinscroft; Members and Clerk of the Woolston-with-Mar- tinscroft Parish Council, Paddington Football Club, Teachers and Scholars of Woolston C.E. Day School, the Premier Artificial Stone Co., Ltd., per Mr. Thos. Gibson, secretar-</p>
        <p>MR. F. T. NEWI3OLD—MISS M. OWEN. St. Mary's Church, Great Sankey, was pret- tily decorated on Wednesday for the marriage of Mr. Frederick T. Newbold (Vicar's warden) to Miss Margaret (Daisy) Owen, daughter of Mrs. Thomas Owen, of the Poplars, Penketh. The Rev. J. R. Jones officiated.</p>
        <p>The bride looked charming in a dress of white silk eolienne over a satin skirt. She wore her mother's wedding veil (which has been used by 15 brides) and a wreath of orange blossom, and carried a bouquet of marguerites, roses, white heather and smilax.</p>
        <p>The bridesmaids were the Misses Mary and Annie Owen, sisters of the bride. They were prettily dressed in white, with swathe belts of pale blue silk, black hats trimmed with pink roses, and carried bouquets of pink roses. " Miss Margery Haynes Owen (niece) and Master George Potter Owen (nephew) also attended the bride. The bride's mother was dressed in black, and carried a bouquet of forget-me-nots. All the gentlemen wore white heather favours. Mr. W. H. Owen, eldest brother of the bride, gave her away, and Mr. F. C. Newbold acted as best man. After the wedding breakfast the bride and bridegroom left for Torquay, where the honey- moon is being spent. A large number of handsome and most useful inwents have been received, an eloquent testi- monial to the high esteem entertained by the givers for Mr. and Mrs. Newbold.</p>
      </div>
