      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0331" n="art0331">
        <head>SPRING HOLIDAYS ON THE CORNISH RIVIERA</head>
        <p>SPRING HOLIDAYS ON THE CORNISH RIVIERA</p>
        <p>part of the British Isles is more richly endowed with noble beauty than the Cornish Riviera in the Spring. A holiday then is one never-to-be- forgotten for its unfading charm. The weary town-dweller in search of health and rest, the artistic visitor in quest of worthy subjects for his sketch book, and the sportsman yearning for fresh fields to conquer, will find in Cornwall just what they need —a healthful, equable climate, entrancing scenery. and sport in plenty. Let "HOLIDAY HAUNTS" help you to map out your holiday. This profusely illustrated guide-book is obtainable at all G.W.R. stations and offices, price 3d., or from the Superinten- dent of the Line, Paddington, London, W., price 6d. post free. Local Agency: 42, Sankey St. Warrington. GwR Thelfoliciaytine.</p>
        <p>Frank</p>
        <p>a cenerftirists'pr</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0332" n="art0332">
        <p>AT HYMEN'S ALTAR.</p>
        <p>Pretty Wedding Ceremony at Christ Church, Latchford.</p>
        <p>MR. W. H.;YENABLES MISS M. D'ARCY FAIRCLOUGH.</p>
        <p>One of the prettiest weddings seen in the Latchford district for some time took place on Thursday, at Christ Church, when Miss Mar- jorie D'Arcy Fairclough, daughter of Mr. John Fairclough, Latchford Grange, was married to Mr. Alex. Lones Venal)les, son of Mr. W. H. Venables, Brougham House, 0/ton, Warwick- shire.</p>
        <p>The ceremony was performed by the Revs. G. C. Briggs and W. 11. Cock. Mr. Fry played de- lightful wedding music on the organ. The bride was given away by her father, and was beautifully attired in a dress of white satin trimmed with silver tissue. She carried a sheaf of lilies. The bridesmaids were Miss Kathleen Hoskins, Miss Judith M. Venables (sister of the bride- groom), and the Misses Mary Fairclough and Mary Theodora Briggs (nieces of the bride). The elder bridesmaids wore dresses of pale blue crepe de chine and mauve chiffon, with hats to match. They carried bouquets of pale blue and mauve iris. The little girls were neatly dressed in white embroidered muslin dresses, and wore silk hats trimmed with forget-me-nots. They carried baskets of forget-me-note, the gifts of the bridegroom. All the bridesmaids wore brooches of aqua marine and gold, the gifts of the bridegroom. .Mr. Edgar Venables, brother of the bride- groom, acted as best man. A reception was held at Latchford Grange. The honeymoon is being spent in Devonshire. The bride travelled in a coat and skirt of copper and pale blue shot silk with hat to match.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0333" n="art0333">
        <head>HIGH LEGH WEDDING.</head>
        <p>A HIGH LEGH WEDDING.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. A. Jones—Miss A. Ford.</p>
        <p>The wedding took place at the Latchford Bap- tist Church on Wednesday of Miss Amy Ford, daughter of Mr. Thomas Ford, Lower Wash- lane Farm, Latchford, and Mr. William Arthur Jones, son of Mr. William Jones, of Holly Farm. High Legh. The bride, who was given away by her father, looked charming in a grey costume, and wore a hat of pale blue with pink Paisley silk and white feathers, and carried a sheaf of lilies. Miss Lily Ford (sister of the bride) was the bridesmaid. She was attired in a dress of cinnamon brawn crepe de chine, and carried a bouquet of carnations. Mr. Fred Jones, brother of the bridegroom, was the hog} ma*. The gift of the bridegroom to the bride was a gold brooch, whilst that of the bride to the bridegroom was a diamond tiepin. The reception was held at the home of the bride's parents, where over 54 useful and valu- able presents were on view.</p>
        <p>_ The honeymoon ie being spent in Southport, the bride travelling in a dark grey coetume, with a hat of biscuit-coloured Leghorn, trimmed with ostrich feather.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-05-02_WidnesExaminer_art0334" n="art0334">
        <head>PRETTY PENKETII WEDDING.</head>
        <p>PRETTY PENKETII WEDDING.</p>
        <p>Marriage of the Vicar's Warden.</p>
        <p>DETAINED BY FOG.</p>
        <p>A Seaman's Wedding at Sankey.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday afternoon at St. Mary's Church, Sankey, the delayed wedding of Mr. Mark Atherton and Miss Annie Heath took place. Some alarm was occasioned in the morn- ing when the bridegroom failed to appear, as be is one of the men who go down to the sea in ships, but fortunately not anything worse than fog delayed him, and he was able to arrive in time for the ceremony to be performed in the afternoon.</p>
        <p>The bride wore a pale blue silk dress, with hat to match, and looked exceedingly dainty. Her bridesmaid, Miss May Heath, wore pink flowered silk and hat to match.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jas. Woodward gave the bride away, and Mr. Thos. Atherton acted as best man. A reception was held in the Winmarleigh Cafe, the young couple leaving immediately for Gloucester, for their honeymoon.</p>
        <p>"Her Mother to Blame." her Mother to Blame" is the title Of 11:3 play of Indian romance to be present Travis Green and Stanley Hoban's Comperl Royal Court Theatre next week. There WIPI a special cast, including Miss Eveline The horse " Selak and the racing track in death ride are the same which created the reel sensation at the Lyceum Theatre, London, 9 was several times patronised by Royalty.</p>
        <p>THE HIPPODROME FARE. Another Attractive Programme. I The standard of excellence is still imaintaii/' at the Hippodrome, and patrons are being hiVi 1 delighted this week Quite a novel Plaijei.; is presented by Harry Hignold, Melly Shine, and Mr. Drew Mackintosh. ri.h. Inignold, as the bull-dog detective, causes Ord laughter and applause with his cleverly execll,(l realistic imitations of the antics of a dog, lin the run of the play thwarts the evil degro lof a swell cracksman. Naughton and Gold a pair of smart comedians, who know just Mi to please. Their Insurance Act skit is ve_i:e good, being extremely witty. They are expert dancers of a new order. The Brotlie,i Obo are up-to-date in their comedy and origil/ri in their methods. Their laughing song is yet° infectious, and their eccentricity ludicrouS,g the extreme. Little " Zaza," a child of years, delights as well as mystifies with her 810 hibition of mental telepathy, which zemill d one very much of "The Days." Bowden Stol show what can be done in the irale comedy jumping and knock-about business. tit Bouncing Dillons are as daring as ever in tileAl novel gymnastic act, and Nellie Bly is Ic",. received as "The Girl in the Frame," who, Afeog posing in character costumes, dances accordltie to her get-up. The Dancing Middies go a too far in describing their turn as a novel da"io ing act, but nevertheless the dancing they ri°,,e, smart enough to merit the applause they rece"</p>
        <p>Next Week's Attractions. The Kenny Brothers, the funny airmen, re,o Tom Hughes, "One of the upper ten," 0117,4 the top position on an attractive bill of fare ' next week. Other excellent turns will be S. Wyndham, comedian; Robb Wilton, confidentik, comedian; The Gilberts, in bounding cable Aci(i Freda Danilo, male impersonator; RegiLlal Crawford, tenor vocalist; and Queenio character vocalist.</p>
