        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MAY 2, 1914. DR,. MELLOR'S FAREWELL. Cairo Street Congregation Present Pastor With a Cheque. MISS A. HOUGHTON HONOURED. Two important presentations were made at the Congregational social in Cairo-street Schools on Monday evening. The :Rev. Dr. Mellor, who took his farewell for Liverpool, received a (love, and Miss A. Houghton, who for 30 years has been a teacher in the Sunday School, was the recipient of an illuminated address and a purse of gold. slklr. I. W. Monks presided over a large attendance. Mrs. Mellor gave a pianoforte solo in a very able manner, and songs were rendered - by Mrs. Arthur Booth and Mr. H. Bennett. Miss Woodcock and Miss Monks gave a laugh- able skacii, Chatterboxes," in a pleasing fashion. .At an interval lefreshments were served. k SCHOOL TEACHER FOR 30 YEARS. In regard to the preSentation to Miss Hough- ton, Dr. Alkil.Oß said .that some little time ago Miss Houghton decided, for a variety of reasons, that it was necessary for her to give up her regular work with the Sunday School. They all felt they could not allow the occasion of her retirement to pass without making some recog- nition of their tieep appreciation of her many faithful services in connection with the Sunday School and Chapel. There were many present who had known Miss Houghton longer than he had, but it did not take long to know how good, true and leyal she., was—kapplause)—and a relatively short acquaintance soon brought to light the many fine points in her noble and splendid character. Miss Houghton bore a name honourably asso- ciated with that chapel, and her many years of: faithful services had added lustre to that</p>
        <p>,r;tl &gt;`• ‘,4", • ' • ".•*^N'''':,,Z st.q, ;091• &lt; , • REV. DR. ItELLOE.</p>
