        <p>DISASTROUS FIRE. Grain Stores Burn Like a Roaring Furnace. EXCITING SCENES LAST NIGHT. Last night one of the most disastrous fires seen in Warrington for some time occurred. The scene was at Bridge Foot, and the centre of the fire zone was the grain and hay stores of Mr. T. M. West, a five-storeyed building, facing the river Mersey. For about an hour the flames belched forth in huge volumes from the five tiers of windows, and an additional alarming factor was added by the presence of a motor garage, with its store of petrol. The building was passed by the policeman on his beat at ten o'clock, and then there were no signs of fire. Three minutes later the flames burst through with exceptional ferocity, and by the time the brigade got on the scene, a few minutes later, the whole building was involved. "I was there," said one of the spectators to "The Examiner" representative, " five minutes after ten o'clock, and then the building was a raging mass of fire. It was, in a word, a minia- ture Gartons." The brigade quickly got to work with the</p>
