        <p>BRIGHT ,GAIIEER ENDED. Death of Par. John Bunch From Appendicitis. A BRILLIANT SCHOLAR. One of the most brilliant of Warrington's young men was yesterday afternoon cut off by death, at the early age of twenty, just as a most promising career was opening up before him. He was Mr. John Bunch, the second son of Mr. Charles E. Bunch, of Bewsey House. The young man, taken ill a few days ago with appendicitis, was removed to the Warrington Infirmary, where an operation was performed. He gradually sank after the strain of the oper- ation, and passed away in the presence of sorrow- ing relative. yesterday afternoon. With his parents and family the greatest sympathy must be expressed at the untimely end of one so full of promise. • Mr. John Bunch had recently been acting as assistant works manager to his father in Messrs. Pearson and Knowles' forges and mills depart- ment., where he was held in the highest esteem by all. His edusational career was brilliant and brought great honour to Warrington. His early education was received at the Boteler Grammar Sshool, where his record was a most 'honourable one. Study finished there, he proceeded to the Man- ehester School of Technology, And was so sue- easeful that he became a Schuster scholar. Passing to the Manchester University he gradu- ated as Bachelor of Science, and also became a City and Guild Silver Medallist.</p>
